ID   1.1.1.287
DE   D-arabinitol dehydrogenase (NADP(+)).
AN   D-arabinitol dehydrogenase 1.
AN   NADP(+)-dependent D-arabinitol dehydrogenase.
CA   (1) D-arabinitol + NADP(+) = D-xylulose + NADPH + H(+).
CA   (2) D-arabinitol + NADP(+) = D-ribulose + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme from the rust fungus Uromyces fabae can use D-arabinitol
CC       and D-mannitol as substrates in the forward direction and D-xylulose,
CC       D-ribulose and, to a lesser extent, D-fructose as substrates in the
CC       reverse direction.
CC   -!- This enzyme carries out the reactions of both EC 1.1.1.11 and
CC       EC 1.1.1.250, but unlike them, uses NADP(+) rather than NAD(+) as
CC       cofactor.
CC   -!- D-Arabinitol is capable of quenching reactive oxygen species involved
CC       in defense reactions of the host plant.
DR   Q4R0J7, ARD1_UROFA ;
//
ID   1.1.1.288
DE   xanthoxin dehydrogenase.
AN   xanthoxin oxidase.
CA   2-cis,4-trans-xanthoxin + NAD(+) = 2-cis-(+)-abscisic aldehyde + NADH +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- NADP(+) cannot replace NAD(+) and short-chain alcohols such as
CC       ethanol, isopropanol, butanol and cyclohexanol cannot replace
CC       xanthoxin as substrate.
CC   -!- Involved in the abscisic-acid biosynthesis pathway in plants, along
CC       with EC 1.2.3.14, EC 1.13.11.51 and EC 1.14.13.93.
DR   Q9C826, ABA2_ARATH ;
//
ID   1.1.1.289
DE   sorbose reductase.
CA   D-sorbitol + NADP(+) = keto-L-sorbose + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- The reaction occurs predominantly in the reverse direction.
CC   -!- This enzyme can also convert D-fructose into D-mannitol, but more
CC       slowly.
DR   P87219, SOU1_CANAL ;
//
ID   1.1.1.290
DE   4-phosphoerythronate dehydrogenase.
AN   4-O-phosphoerythronate dehydrogenase.
AN   4PE dehydrogenase.
AN   erythronate-4-phosphate dehydrogenase.
CA   4-phospho-D-erythronate + NAD(+) = (R)-3-hydroxy-2-oxo-4-
CA   phosphooxybutanoate + NADH + H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes a step in a bacterial pathway for the
CC       biosynthesis of pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme contains a tightly-bound NAD(H) cofactor that is not
CC       re-oxidized by free NAD(+).
CC   -!- In order to re-oxidize the cofactor and restore enzyme activity,
CC       the enzyme catalyzes the reduction of a 2-oxo acid (such as
CC       2-oxoglutarate, oxaloacetate, or pyruvate) to the respective (R)-
CC       hydroxy acid. cf. EC 1.1.1.399.
