//
ID   1.1.5.5
DE   alcohol dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   PQQ alcohol dehydrogenase.
AN   PQQ-dependent ADH.
AN   PQQ-dependent alcohol dehydrogenase.
AN   pyrroloquinoline quinone-dependent alcohol dehydrogenase.
AN   quinoprotein ADH.
AN   quinoprotein alcohol dehydrogenase.
AN   type III ADH.
CA   ethanol + a ubiquinone = a ubiquinol + acetaldehyde.
CC   -!- Only described in acetic acid bacteria where it is involved in acetic
CC       acid production.
CC   -!- Associated with membrane.
CC   -!- Electron acceptor is membrane ubiquinone.
CC   -!- A model structure suggests that, like all other quinoprotein alcohol
CC       dehydrogenases, the catalytic subunit has an 8-bladed 'propeller'
CC       structure, a calcium ion bound to the PQQ in the active site and an
CC       unusual disulfide ring structure in close proximity to the PQQ; the
CC       catalytic subunit also has a heme c in the C-terminal domain.
CC   -!- The enzyme has two additional subunits, one of which contains three
CC       molecules of heme c.
CC   -!- It does not require amines for activation.
CC   -!- It has a restricted substrate specificity, oxidizing a few primary
CC       alcohols (C2 to C6), but not methanol, secondary alcohols and some
CC       aldehydes.
CC   -!- It is assayed with phenazine methosulfate or with ferricyanide.
DR   P18278, ADHA_ACEAC ;  O05542, ADHA_GLUOX ;  P28036, ADHA_GLUPO ;
DR   Q44002, ADHA_KOMEU ;  Q47945, ADHB_GLUOX ;  P0A388, ADHB_GLUPO ;
DR   P0A389, ADHB_KOMEU ;
//
ID   1.1.5.6
DE   Transferred entry: 1.17.5.3.
//
ID   1.1.5.7
DE   cyclic alcohol dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   cyclic alcohol dehydrogenase.
AN   MCAD.
CA   a cyclic alcohol + a quinone = a cyclic ketone + a quinol.
CC   -!- This enzyme oxidizes a wide variety of cyclic alcohols.
CC   -!- Some minor enzyme activity is found with aliphatic secondary alcohols
CC       and sugar alcohols, but not primary alcohols.
CC   -!- The enzyme is unable to catalyze the reverse reaction of cyclic
CC       ketones or aldehydes to cyclic alcohols.
CC   -!- This enzyme differs from EC 1.1.5.5, which shows activity with
CC       ethanol.
//
ID   1.1.5.8
DE   quinate/shikimate dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   NAD(P)(+)-independent quinate dehydrogenase.
AN   quinate dehydrogenase (quinone).
CA   L-quinate + a quinone = 3-dehydroquinate + a quinol.
CC   -!- The enzyme is membrane-bound.
CC   -!- Does not use NAD(P)(+) as acceptor.
CC   -!- Contains pyrroloquinoline-quinone. cf. EC 1.1.1.24, EC 1.1.1.1.25,
CC       and EC 1.1.1.282.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.99.25.
DR   Q59086, QUIA_ACIAD ;  Q9XD78, QUIA_XANCJ ;
//
ID   1.1.5.9
DE   glucose 1-dehydrogenase (FAD, quinone).
AN   FAD-dependent glucose dehydrogenase.
AN   glucose dehydrogenase (Aspergillus).
CA   a quinone + D-glucose = D-glucono-1,5-lactone + a quinol.
CC   -!- 2,6-dichloroindophenol can act as acceptor. cf. EC 1.1.5.2.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.99.10.
DR   P18173, DHGL_DROME ;  P18172, DHGL_DROPS ;
//
ID   1.1.5.10
DE   D-2-hydroxyacid dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   (R)-2-hydroxy acid dehydrogenase.
AN   D-lactate dehydrogenase.
CA   a (2R)-2-hydroxycarboxylate + a quinone = a 2-oxocarboxylate + a quinol.
CC   -!- (R)-lactate, (R)-malate and meso-tartrate are good substrates.
CC   -!- Ubiquinone-1 and the dye 2,6-dichloroindophenol can act as acceptors;
CC       NAD(+) and NADP(+) are not acceptors.
//
ID   1.1.5.11
DE   1-butanol dehydrogenase (quinone).
CA   butan-1-ol + a quinone = butanal + a quinol.
CC   -!- This periplasmic quinoprotein alcohol dehydrogenase, characterized
CC       from the bacterium Thauera butanivorans, is involved in butane
CC       degradation.
CC   -!- It contains a tightly-bound pyrroloquinoline quinone (PQQ) cofactor.
CC       cf. EC 1.1.2.9.
DR   Q9AGW3, BOH_THABB  ;
//
ID   1.1.5.12
DE   D-lactate dehydrogenase (quinone).
CA   (R)-lactate + a quinone = a quinol + pyruvate.
CC   -!- The enzyme is a peripheral membrane dehydrogenase that participates
CC       in respiration.
CC   -!- Electrons derived from D-lactate oxidation are transferred to the
CC       membrane soluble quinone pool.
DR   Q8NRY8, DLD_CORGL  ;  P06149, DLD_ECOLI  ;  P45295, DLD_HAEIN  ;
//
ID   1.1.5.13
DE   (S)-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase.
AN   L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase.
CA   (S)-2-hydroxyglutarate + a quinone = a quinol + 2-oxoglutarate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Escherichia coli,
CC       contains an FAD cofactor that is not covalently attached.
CC   -!- It is bound to the cytoplasmic membrane and is coupled to the
CC       membrane quinone pool.
DR   P37339, LHGD_ECOLI ;  B7N6P4, LHGD_ECOLU ;  A0A5Y6MCT2, LHGD_SALHO ;
//
ID   1.1.5.14
DE   fructose 5-dehydrogenase.
AN   D-fructose dehydrogenase.
CA   keto-D-fructose + a ubiquinone = 5-dehydro-D-fructose + a ubiquinol.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Gluconobacter japonicus,
CC       is a heterotrimer composed of an FAD-containing large subunit,
CC       a small subunit, and a heme c-containing subunit, which is
CC       responsible for anchoring the complex to the cytoplasmic membrane and
CC       for transferring the electrons to ubiquinone.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.99.11.
DR   M1VMF7, FDHL_GLUJA ;
//
ID   1.1.5.n1
DE   quinoprotein inositol dehydrogenase.
AN   membrane-bound quinoprotein inositol dehydrogenase.
AN   PQQ-dependent inositol dehydrogenase.
AN   QIDH.
CA   pyrroloquinoline quinone + myo-inositol = pyrroloquinoline quinol +
CA   scyllo-inosose + H(+).
//
ID   1.1.7.1
DE   4-hydroxybenzoyl-CoA reductase.
CA   oxidized 2[4Fe-4S]-[ferredoxin] + benzoyl-CoA + H2O = 4-hydroxybenzoyl-
CA   CoA + reduced 2[4Fe-4S]-[ferredoxin] + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- A molybdenum-flavin-iron-sulfur protein that is involved in the
CC       anaerobic pathway of phenol metabolism in bacteria.
CC   -!- A ferredoxin with two [4Fe-4S] clusters functions as the natural
CC       electron donor.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.3.7.9 and EC 1.3.99.20.
DR   O33819, HCRA_THAAR ;  O33820, HCRB_THAAR ;  O33818, HCRC_THAAR ;
//
ID   1.1.9.1
DE   alcohol dehydrogenase (azurin).
AN   QHEDH.
CA   2 oxidized [azurin] + a primary alcohol = 2 reduced [azurin] +
CA   an aldehyde + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Occurs in Comamonas and Pseudomonas.
CC   -!- Does not require an amine activator.
CC   -!- Oxidizes a wide range of primary and secondary alcohols, and also
CC       aldehydes and large substrates such as sterols; methanol is not a
CC       substrate.
CC   -!- Usually assayed with phenazine methosulfate or ferricyanide.
CC   -!- Like all other quinoprotein alcohol dehydrogenases it has an 8-bladed
CC       'propeller' structure, a calcium ion bound to the PQQ in the active
CC       site and an unusual disulfide ring structure in close proximity to
CC       the PQQ.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.98.1.
DR   Q4W6G0, QGDA_PSEPU ;  Q46444, QHED_COMTE ;  Q8GR64, QHED_PSEPU ;
//
ID   1.1.98.1
DE   Transferred entry: 1.1.9.1.
//
ID   1.1.98.2
DE   glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (coenzyme-F420).
AN   coenzyme F420-dependent glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase.
AN   F420-dependent glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase.
CA   oxidized coenzyme F420-(gamma-L-Glu)(n) + D-glucose 6-phosphate + H(+) =
CA   6-phospho-D-glucono-1,5-lactone + reduced coenzyme F420-(gamma-L-Glu)(n).
CC   -!- The enzyme is very specific for D-glucose 6-phosphate.
CC   -!- No activity with NAD(+), NADP(+), flavin adenine dinucleotide and
CC       flavin mononucleotide.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.99.34.
