//
ID   1.2.1.60
DE   5-carboxymethyl-2-hydroxymuconic-semialdehyde dehydrogenase.
AN   carboxymethylhydroxymuconic semialdehyde dehydrogenase.
CA   2-hydroxy-5-carboxymethylmuconate semialdehyde + NAD(+) + H2O = (2E,4Z)-
CA   5-hydroxypenta-2,4-diene-1,2,5-tricarboxylate + NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Involved in the tyrosine degradation pathway in Arthrobacter sp.
DR   P42269, HPCC_ECOLX ;
//
ID   1.2.1.61
DE   4-hydroxymuconic-semialdehyde dehydrogenase.
CA   cis,trans-4-hydroxymuconate semialdehyde + NAD(+) + H2O = maleylacetate +
CA   NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Involved in the 4-nitrophenol degradation pathway.
//
ID   1.2.1.62
DE   4-formylbenzenesulfonate dehydrogenase.
CA   4-formylbenzenesulfonate + NAD(+) + H2O = 4-sulfobenzoate + NADH +
CA   2 H(+).
CC   -!- Involved in the toluene-4-sulfonate degradation pathway.
DR   P94681, TSAC_COMTE ;
//
ID   1.2.1.63
DE   6-oxohexanoate dehydrogenase.
CA   6-oxohexanoate + NADP(+) + H2O = hexanedioate + NADPH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Last step in the cyclohexanol degradation pathway in Acinetobacter
CC       sp.
DR   Q9R2F4, CHNE_ACIJO ;  Q9F7E3, CHNE_ACISS ;
//
ID   1.2.1.64
DE   4-hydroxybenzaldehyde dehydrogenase (NAD(+)).
AN   4-hydroxybenzaldehyde dehydrogenase.
AN   p-hydroxybenzaldehyde dehydrogenase.
CA   4-hydroxybenzaldehyde + NAD(+) + H2O = 4-hydroxybenzoate + NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The bacterial enzyme (characterized from an unidentified denitrifying
CC       bacterium) is involved in an anaerobic toluene degradation pathway.
CC   -!- The plant enzyme is involved in formation of 4-hydroxybenzoate,
CC       a cell wall-bound phenolic acid that plays a major role in plant
CC       defense against pathogens. cf. EC 1.2.1.96.
DR   Q04458, HFD1_YEAST ;  Q8NMB0, VDH_CORGL  ;
//
ID   1.2.1.65
DE   salicylaldehyde dehydrogenase.
CA   salicylaldehyde + NAD(+) + H2O = salicylate + NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Involved in the naphthalene degradation pathway in some bacteria.
DR   P0A391, NAHF_PSEPU ;  P0A390, NAHF_PSEU8 ;
//
ID   1.2.1.66
DE   Transferred entry: 1.1.1.306.
//
ID   1.2.1.67
DE   vanillin dehydrogenase.
CA   vanillin + NAD(+) + H2O = vanillate + NADH + 2 H(+).
DR   O06478, ALDH5_BACSU;  K9UV87, VDH_AMYS7  ;  Q8NMB0, VDH_CORGL  ;
DR   O69763, VDH_PSEFL  ;  O05619, VDH_PSEUH  ;
//
ID   1.2.1.68
DE   coniferyl-aldehyde dehydrogenase.
CA   (1) (E)-coniferaldehyde + NADP(+) + H2O = (E)-ferulate + NADPH + 2 H(+).
CA   (2) (E)-coniferaldehyde + NAD(+) + H2O = (E)-ferulate + NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Also oxidizes other aromatic aldehydes, but not aliphatic aldehydes.
DR   Q9A777, CALB_CAUVC ;  Q9I6C8, CALB_PSEAE ;  O86447, CALB_PSEUH ;
//
ID   1.2.1.69
DE   fluoroacetaldehyde dehydrogenase.
CA   fluoroacetaldehyde + NAD(+) + H2O = fluoroacetate + NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme from Streptomyces cattleya has a high affinity for
CC       fluoroacetate and glycolaldehyde but not for acetaldehyde.
DR   F8JX40, FLUDE_STREN;
//
ID   1.2.1.70
DE   glutamyl-tRNA reductase.
CA   (S)-4-amino-5-oxopentanoate + tRNA(Glu) + NADP(+) = L-glutamyl-tRNA(Glu)
CA   + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- Forms part of the pathway for the biosynthesis of 5-aminolevulinate
CC       from glutamate, known as the C5 pathway, which is used in most
CC       eubacteria, and in all archaebacteria, algae and plants.
CC   -!- However, in the alpha-proteobacteria EC 2.3.1.37 is used in an
CC       alternative route to produce the product 5-aminolevulinate from
CC       succinyl-CoA and glycine.
CC   -!- This route is found in the mitochondria of fungi and animals,
CC       organelles that are considered to be derived from an endosymbiotic
CC       alpha-proteobacterium.
CC   -!- Although higher plants do not possess EC 2.3.1.37, the protistan
CC       Euglena gracilis possesses both the C5 pathway and EC 2.3.1.37.
DR   Q2IJD4, HEM11_ANADE;  A7HA35, HEM11_ANADF;  P42804, HEM11_ARATH;
