ID   1.2.1.107
DE   glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (arsenate-transferring).
CA   arsenate + D-glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate + NAD(+) = 1-arsono-3-phospho-D-
CA   glycerate + NADH + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, discovered in bacteria, is very similar to EC 1.2.1.12.
CC   -!- However, the gene encoding it is located in arsenic resistance
CC       islands in the chromosome, next to a gene (arsJ) that encodes a
CC       transporter that removes the product, 1-arsono-3-phosphoglycerate,
CC       from the cell.
CC   -!- Together the two proteins form an arsenic detoxification system.
//
ID   1.2.1.108
DE   alcohol-forming fatty acyl-CoA reductase (NADH).
CA   a long-chain fatty acyl-CoA + 2 NADH + 2 H(+) = a long-chain primary
CA   fatty alcohol + 2 NAD(+) + CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme has been characterized from the photosynthetic flagellate
CC       Euglena gracilis.
CC   -!- The alcohol is formed by a four-electron reduction of fatty acyl-CoA
CC       by NADH.
CC   -!- Although the reaction proceeds through an aldehyde intermediate,
CC       a free aldehyde is not released.
CC   -!- cf.EC 1.2.1.84.
//
ID   1.2.1.n1
DE   Transferred entry: 1.2.1.77.
