ID   1.3.1.77
DE   anthocyanidin reductase [(2R,3R)-flavan-3-ol-forming].
AN   ANR.
AN   anthocyanidin reductase.
CA   (1) a (2R,3R)-flavan-3-ol + 2 NAD(+) = an anthocyanidin with a 3-hydroxy
CA   group + 2 NADH + 2 H(+).
CA   (2) a (2R,3R)-flavan-3-ol + 2 NADP(+) = an anthocyanidin with a 3-hydroxy
CA   group + 2 NADPH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme participates in the flavonoid biosynthesis pathway found
CC       in plants.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the double reduction of anthocyanidins, producing
CC       (2R,3R)-flavan-3-ol monomers required for the formation of
CC       proanthocyanidins.
CC   -!- While the enzyme from the legume Medicago truncatula can use both
CC       NADPH and NADH as reductant, that from the crucifer Arabidopsis
CC       thaliana uses only NADPH.
CC   -!- Also, while the substrate preference of the M. truncatula is
CC       cyanidin>pelargonidin>delphinidin, the reverse preference is found
CC       with the Arabidopsis enzyme. cf. EC 1.3.1.112.
DR   Q9SEV0, BAN_ARATH  ;
//
ID   1.3.1.78
DE   arogenate dehydrogenase (NADP(+)).
AN   arogenic dehydrogenase.
AN   pretyrosine dehydrogenase.
AN   TyrAa.
AN   TyrAAT1.
AN   TyrAAT2.
CA   L-arogenate + NADP(+) = L-tyrosine + CO2 + NADPH.
CC   -!- Arogenate dehydrogenases may utilize NAD(+) (EC 1.3.1.43), NADP(+)
CC       (EC 1.3.1.78), or both (EC 1.3.1.79).
CC   -!- NADP(+)-dependent enzymes usually predominate in higher plants.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the cyanobacterium Synechocystis sp. PCC 6803 and the
CC       TyrAAT1 isoform of the plant Arabidopsis thaliana cannot use
CC       prephenate as a substrate, while the Arabidopsis isoform TyrAAT2 can
CC       use it very poorly.
DR   Q944B6, TYRA1_ARATH;  Q9LMR3, TYRA2_ARATH;
//
ID   1.3.1.79
DE   arogenate dehydrogenase [NAD(P)(+)].
AN   arogenic dehydrogenase.
AN   cyclohexadienyl dehydrogenase.
AN   pretyrosine dehydrogenase.
CA   (1) L-arogenate + NADP(+) = L-tyrosine + CO2 + NADPH.
CA   (2) L-arogenate + NAD(+) = L-tyrosine + CO2 + NADH.
CC   -!- Arogenate dehydrogenases may utilize NAD(+) (EC 1.3.1.43), NADP(+)
CC       (EC 1.3.1.78), or both (EC 1.3.1.79).
CC   -!- Enzymes that can utilize both cofactors have been reported from some
CC       Proteobacteria, including Burkholderia caryophylli, Burkholderia
CC       cepacia, Pseudomonas marginata and Delftia acidovorans.
//
ID   1.3.1.80
DE   Transferred entry: 1.3.7.12.
//
ID   1.3.1.81
DE   (+)-pulegone reductase.
CA   (1) (1R,4S)-menthone + NADP(+) = (R)-pulegone + NADPH + H(+).
CA   (2) (2R,5R)-isomenthone + NADP(+) = (R)-pulegone + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- NADH cannot replace NADPH as reductant.
CC   -!- The Delta(8,9)-double bond of (+)-cis-isopulegone and the Delta(1,2)-
CC       double bond of (+-)-piperitone are not substrates.
CC   -!- The enzyme from peppermint (Mentha x piperita) converts (+)-pulegone
CC       into both (-)-menthone and (+)-isomenthone at a ratio of 70:30 for
CC       native enzyme but it does not catalyze the reverse reaction.
DR   Q6WAU0, PULR_MENPI ;
//
ID   1.3.1.82
DE   (-)-isopiperitenone reductase.
CA   (2R,5R)-isopulegone + NADP(+) = (6R)-isopiperitenone + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- The reaction occurs in the opposite direction to that shown above.
CC   -!- Participates in the menthol-biosynthesis pathway of Mentha plants.
CC   -!- (+)-Pulegone, (+)-cis-isopulegone and (-)-menthone are not
CC       substrates.
CC   -!- Has a preference for NADPH as the reductant, with NADH being a poor
CC       substitute.
CC   -!- Highly regioselective for the reduction of the endocyclic 1,2-double
CC       bond, and is stereoselective, producing only the 1R-configured
CC       product.
DR   Q6WAU1, IPIPR_MENPI;
//
ID   1.3.1.83
DE   geranylgeranyl diphosphate reductase.
AN   geranylgeranyl reductase.
CA   phytyl diphosphate + 3 NADP(+) = geranylgeranyl diphosphate + 3 NADPH +
CA   3 H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme also acts on geranylgeranyl-chlorophyll a.
CC   -!- The reaction occurs in three steps.
CC   -!- Which order the three double bonds are reduced is not known.
DR   P26172, BCHP_RHOCB ;  Q9CA67, CHLP_ARATH ;  Q6Z2T6, CHLP_ORYSJ ;
DR   Q55087, CHLP_SYNY3 ;  Q9ZS34, CHLP_TOBAC ;
//
ID   1.3.1.84
DE   acrylyl-CoA reductase (NADPH).
CA   propanoyl-CoA + NADP(+) = acryloyl-CoA + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- Catalyzes a step in the 3-hydroxypropanoate/4-hydroxybutyrate cycle,
CC       an autotrophic CO2 fixation pathway found in some thermoacidophilic
CC       archaea.
CC   -!- The reaction is catalyzed in the opposite direction to that shown.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Sulfolobus tokodaii does not act on either NADH or
CC       crotonyl-CoA.
CC   -!- Different from EC 1.3.1.8, which acts only on enoyl-CoA derivatives
CC       of carbon chain length 4 to 16.
DR   A4YGN2, ACAR_METS5 ;  Q975C8, ACAR_SULTO ;  Q3J6K9, ACUI_CERS4 ;
DR   P26646, ACUI_ECOLI ;  Q5LS56, ACUI_RUEPO ;
//
ID   1.3.1.85
DE   crotonyl-CoA carboxylase/reductase.
AN   CCR.
AN   crotonyl-CoA reductase (carboxylating).
CA   (2S)-ethylmalonyl-CoA + NADP(+) = (2E)-butenoyl-CoA + CO2 + NADPH.
CC   -!- The reaction is catalyzed in the reverse direction.
CC   -!- This enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Rhodobacter sphaeroides,
CC       catalyzes (E)-but-2-enoyl-CoA-dependent oxidation of NADPH in the
CC       presence of CO2.
CC   -!- When CO2 is absent, the enzyme catalyzes the reduction of (E)-but-2-
CC       enoyl-CoA to butanoyl-CoA, but with only 10% of maximal activity
CC       (relative to (E)-but-2-enoyl-CoA carboxylation).
DR   Q3IZ91, CCCR_CERS4 ;
