ID   1.4.1.26
DE   2,4-diaminopentanoate dehydrogenase (NAD(+)).
AN   DAPDH.
CA   (2R,4S)-2,4-diaminopentanoate + NAD(+) + H2O = (2R)-2-amino-4-
CA   oxopentanoate + NH4(+) + NADH + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from an unknown bacterium in an
CC       environmental sample, has some activity with (2R,4R)-2,4-
CC       diaminopentanoate.
CC   -!- It has very low activity with NADP(+) (cf. EC 1.4.1.12).
DR   C1FW05, ORD_UNKP   ;
//
ID   1.4.1.27
DE   glycine cleavage system.
AN   GCV.
CA   (6S)-5,6,7,8-tetrahydrofolate + glycine + NAD(+) = (6R)-5,10-methylene-
CA   5,6,7,8-tetrahydrofolate + NH4(+) + CO2 + NADH.
CC   -!- The glycine cleavage (GCV) system is a large multienzyme complex that
CC       belongs to the 2-oxoacid dehydrogenase complex family, which also
CC       includes EC 1.2.1.25, EC 1.2.1.105, EC 1.2.1.104 and EC 2.3.1.190.
CC   -!- The GCV system catalyzes the reversible oxidation of glycine,
CC       yielding carbon dioxide, ammonia, 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate and
CC       a reduced pyridine nucleotide.
CC   -!- Tetrahydrofolate serves as a recipient for one-carbon units generated
CC       during glycine cleavage to form the methylene group.
CC   -!- The GCV system consists of four protein components, the P protein
CC       (EC 1.4.4.2), T protein (EC 2.1.2.10), L protein (EC 1.8.1.4),
CC       and the non-enzyme H protein (lipoyl-carrier protein).
CC   -!- The P protein catalyzes the pyridoxal phosphate-dependent liberation
CC       of CO2 from glycine, leaving a methylamine moiety.
CC   -!- The methylamine moiety is transferred to the lipoic acid group of the
CC       H protein, which is bound to the P protein prior to decarboxylation
CC       of glycine.
CC   -!- The T protein catalyzes the release of ammonia from the methylamine
CC       group and transfers the remaining C1 unit to tetrahydrofolate,
CC       forming 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate.
CC   -!- The L protein then oxidizes the lipoic acid component of the H
CC       protein and transfers the electrons to NAD(+), forming NADH.
//
ID   1.4.1.28
DE   secondary-alkyl amine dehydrogenase [NAD(P)(+)].
AN   amine dehydrogenase.
CA   (1) a secondary alkyl amine + NAD(+) + H2O = a ketone + NH4(+) + NADH +
CA   H(+).
CA   (2) a secondary alkyl amine + NADP(+) + H2O = a ketone + NH4(+) + NADPH +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme has been shown to react preferentially with short-chain
CC       ketones such as cyclohexanone, primary amine groups attached to
CC       secondary alkyl groups, or D- and L-amino acids.
CC   -!- It also reduces aldehydes to primary amines.
CC   -!- Cosubstrate preference depends on the substrate.
DR   A9BHL2, AMDH_PETMO ;  A0A0L8M630, AMDH_STRVG ;
//
ID   1.4.2.1
DE   glycine dehydrogenase (cytochrome).
AN   glycine-cytochrome c reductase.
CA   2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] + glycine + H2O = 2 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c] +
CA   glyoxylate + NH4(+) + 2 H(+).
//
ID   1.4.2.2
DE   nicotine dehydrogenase.
CA   (S)-nicotine + 2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] = N-methylmyosmine + 2 Fe(II)-
CA   [cytochrome c] + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Pseudomonas putida S16,
CC       contains an FAD cofactor and belongs to the flavin-containing amine
CC       oxidase family.
CC   -!- The enzyme from this bacterium is specific for the c-type cytochrome
CC       CycN.
CC   -!- The product undergoes spontaneous hydrolysis to form
CC       pseudooxynicotine.
DR   F8G0P2, NICA2_PSEP6;
//
ID   1.4.2.3
