ID   1.7.1.9
DE   nitroquinoline-N-oxide reductase.
AN   4-nitroquinoline 1-oxide reductase.
AN   4NQO reductase.
AN   NAD(P)H:4-nitroquinoline-N-oxide oxidoreductase.
CA   (1) 4-(hydroxyamino)quinoline N-oxide + 2 NADP(+) + H2O =
CA   4-nitroquinoline N-oxide + 2 NADPH + 2 H(+).
CA   (2) 4-(hydroxyamino)quinoline N-oxide + 2 NAD(+) + H2O = 4-nitroquinoline
CA   N-oxide + 2 NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.6.6.10.
DR   P37261, FRM2_YEAST ;
//
ID   1.7.1.10
DE   hydroxylamine reductase (NADH).
AN   ammonium dehydrogenase.
AN   hydroxylamine reductase.
AN   NADH:hydroxylamine oxidoreductase.
AN   NADH-hydroxylamine reductase.
AN   N-hydroxy amine reductase.
CA   NH4(+) + NAD(+) + H2O = hydroxylamine + NADH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Also acts on some hydroxamates.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.6.6.11.
//
ID   1.7.1.11
DE   4-(dimethylamino)phenylazoxybenzene reductase.
AN   dimethylaminoazobenzene N-oxide reductase.
AN   N,N-dimethyl-p-aminoazobenzene oxide reductase.
AN   NADPH:4-(dimethylamino)phenylazoxybenzene oxidoreductase.
AN   NADPH-dependent DMAB N-oxide reductase.
CA   4-(dimethylamino)azobenzene + NADP(+) + H2O =
CA   4-(dimethylamino)phenylazoxybenzene + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.6.6.12.
//
ID   1.7.1.12
DE   N-hydroxy-2-acetamidofluorene reductase.
AN   N-hydroxy-2-acetylaminofluorene reductase.
CA   (1) 2-acetamidofluorene + NAD(+) + H2O = N-hydroxy-2-acetamidofluorene +
CA   NADH + H(+).
CA   (2) 2-acetamidofluorene + NADP(+) + H2O = N-hydroxy-2-acetamidofluorene +
CA   NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- Also acts, more slowly, on N-hydroxy-4-acetamidobiphenyl.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.6.6.13.
//
ID   1.7.1.13
DE   preQ1 synthase.
AN   7-cyano-7-deazaguanine reductase.
AN   preQ0 oxidoreductase.
AN   preQ0 reductase.
CA   7-aminomethyl-7-carbaguanine + 2 NADP(+) = 7-cyano-7-carbaguanine +
CA   2 NADPH + 3 H(+).
CC   -!- The reaction occurs in the reverse direction.
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes one of the early steps in the synthesis of
CC       queosine (Q-tRNA), and is followed by the action of EC 2.4.2.29.
CC   -!- Queosine is found in the wobble position of tRNAGUN in eukaryotes and
CC       bacteria and is thought to be involved in translational modulation.
