ID   1.7.2.5
DE   nitric-oxide reductase (cytochrome c).
CA   nitrous oxide + 2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] + H2O = 2 nitric oxide +
CA   2 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c] + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme from Pseudomonas aeruginosa contains a dinuclear center
CC       comprising a non-heme iron center and heme b3, plus heme c, heme b
CC       and calcium; the acceptor is cytochrome c551.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.7.99.7.
DR   Q59647, NORB_PSEAE ;  P98008, NORB_STUST ;
//
ID   1.7.2.6
DE   hydroxylamine dehydrogenase.
AN   hydroxylamine oxidase.
AN   hydroxylamine oxidoreductase.
CA   hydroxylamine + 4 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] + H2O = 4 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c]
CA   + nitrite + 5 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzymes from the nitrifying bacterium Nitrosomonas europaea and
CC       the methylotrophic bacterium Methylococcus capsulatus are
CC       hemoproteins with seven c-type hemes and one specialized P460-type
CC       heme per subunit.
CC   -!- The enzyme converts hydroxylamine to nitrite via an enzyme-bound
CC       nitroxyl intermediate.
CC   -!- While nitrite is the main product, the enzyme from Nitrosomonas
CC       europaea can also produce nitric oxide by catalyzing the activity of
CC       EC 1.7.2.9.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.7.3.4.
DR   Q50925, HAO_NITEU  ;  P0DV45, NRHAO_KUEST;
//
ID   1.7.2.7
DE   hydrazine synthase.
CA   hydrazine + 3 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] + H2O = nitric oxide + 3 Fe(II)-
CA   [cytochrome c] + NH4(+) + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from anaerobic ammonia oxidizers (anammox
CC       bacteria), is one of only a few enzymes that are known to form an N-N
CC       bond (other examples include EC 1.7.1.14 and EC 4.8.1.1).
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Candidatus Kuenenia stuttgartiensis is
CC       heterotrimeric and contains multiple c-type cytochromes.
DR   Q1Q0T2, HZSA_KUEST ;  Q1Q0T3, HZSG_KUEST ;
//
ID   1.7.2.8
DE   hydrazine dehydrogenase.
CA   hydrazine + 4 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] = N2 + 4 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c] +
CA   4 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, which is involved in the pathway of anaerobic ammonium
CC       oxidation in anammox bacteria, has been purified from the bacterium
CC       Candidatus Kuenenia stuttgartiensis.
CC   -!- The electrons derived from hydrazine are eventually transferred to
CC       the quinone pool.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.7.99.8.
DR   Q1PW30, HDH_KUEST  ;
//
ID   1.7.2.9
DE   hydroxylamine oxidase.
AN   HOX.
AN   HOX2.
CA   hydroxylamine + 3 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] = nitric oxide + 3 Fe(II)-
CA   [cytochrome c] + 3 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the anaerobic ammonium-oxidizing
CC       (anammox) bacterium Kuenenia stuttgartiensis, is very similar to
CC       EC 1.7.2.6.
CC   -!- Both enzymes are homotrimeric enzymes in which each subunit contains
CC       seven c-type hemes and one specialized P460-type heme that is bound
CC       to a tyrosine residue in an adjacent subunit.
CC   -!- However, this enzyme catalyzes only the 3 electron oxidation of
CC       hydroxylamine, forming nitric oxide, and is not capable of performing
CC       further oxidation to form nitrite.
DR   Q1PX48, HAO_KUEST  ;  Q50925, HAO_NITEU  ;
//
ID   1.7.3.1
DE   nitroalkane oxidase.
AN   NAO.
AN   nitroethane oxidase.
AN   nitroethane reductase.
CA   (1) a primary nitroalkane + O2 + H2O = an aldehyde + nitrite + H2O2 +
CA   H(+).
CA   (2) a secondary nitroalkane + O2 + H2O = a ketone + nitrite + H2O2 +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- While nitroethane may be the physiological substrate, the enzyme also
CC       acts on several other nitroalkanes, including 1-nitropropane,
CC       2-nitropropane, 1-nitrobutane, 1-nitropentane, 1-nitrohexane,
CC       nitrocyclohexane and some nitroalkanols.
CC   -!- Differs from EC 1.13.12.16 in that the preferred substrates are
CC       neutral nitroalkanes rather than anionic nitronates.
DR   Q8X1D8, NAO_FUSOX  ;  B2AM55, NAO_PODAN  ;
//
ID   1.7.3.2
DE   acetylindoxyl oxidase.
CA   N-acetylindoxyl + O2 = N-acetylisatin + H2O.
//
ID   1.7.3.3
DE   factor independent urate hydroxylase.
AN   urate oxidase.
AN   uric acid oxidase.
AN   uricase.
AN   uricase II.
CA   urate + O2 + H2O = 5-hydroxyisourate + H2O2.
CC   -!- Was previously thought to be a copper protein, but it is now known
CC       that the enzymes from soybean (Glycine max), the mold Aspergillus
CC       flavus and Bacillus subtilis contains no copper nor any other
CC       transition-metal ion.
CC   -!- The 5-hydroxyisourate formed decomposes spontaneously to form
CC       allantoin and CO2, although there is an enzyme-catalyzed pathway in
CC       which EC 3.5.2.17 catalyzes the first step.
CC   -!- The enzyme is different from EC 1.14.13.113.
