ID   1.8.1.16
DE   glutathione amide reductase.
AN   GAR.
CA   2 glutathione amide + NAD(+) = glutathione amide disulfide + NADH + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme restores glutathione amide disulfide, which is produced
CC       during the reduction of peroxide by EC 1.11.1.17 back to glutathione
CC       amide (it catalyzes the reaction in the opposite direction to that
CC       shown).
CC   -!- The enzyme belongs to the family of flavoprotein disulfide
CC       oxidoreductases, but unlike other members of the family, which are
CC       specific for NADPH, it prefers NADH.
DR   D0VWY5, GASHR_MARGR;
//
ID   1.8.1.17
DE   dimethylsulfone reductase.
CA   dimethyl sulfoxide + NAD(+) + H2O = dimethyl sulfone + NADH + H(+).
//
ID   1.8.1.18
DE   NAD(P)H sulfur oxidoreductase (CoA-dependent).
AN   coenzyme A-dependent NADPH sulfur oxidoreductase.
AN   S(0) reductase.
CA   (1) hydrogen sulfide + NADP(+) = sulfur + NADPH.
CA   (2) hydrogen sulfide + NAD(+) = sulfur + NADH.
CC   -!- This enzyme, characterized from the archaeon Pyrococcus furiosus,
CC       is responsible for NAD(P)H-linked sulfur reduction.
CC   -!- The activity with NADH is about half of that with NADPH.
CC   -!- The reaction is dependent on CoA, although the nature of this
CC       dependency is not well understood.
DR   Q9UYU5, NSR_PYRAB  ;  Q8U1M0, NSR_PYRFU  ;  Q5JGP4, NSR_THEKO  ;
//
ID   1.8.1.19
DE   sulfide dehydrogenase.
CA   n sulfur + hydrogen sulfide + NADP(+) = (n+1) sulfur + NADPH.
CC   -!- In the archaeon Pyrococcus furiosus the enzyme is involved in the
CC       oxidation of NADPH which is produced in peptide degradation.
CC   -!- The enzyme also catalyzes the reduction of sulfur with lower
CC       activity.
DR   Q8U195, SUDHA_PYRFU;  Q8U194, SUDHB_PYRFU;
//
ID   1.8.1.20
DE   4,4'-dithiodibutanoate disulfide reductase.
CA   2 4-sulfanylbutanoate + NAD(+) = 4,4'-disulfanyldibutanoate + NADH +
CA   H(+).
DR   A0A0N9HP11, NOX_RHOER  ;
//
ID   1.8.1.21
DE   dissimilatory dimethyldisulfide reductase.
CA   2 methanethiol + NAD(+) = dimethyl disulfide + NADH + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme's activity has been demonstrated in the bacterium
CC       Thiobacillus thioparus E6.
CC   -!- The methanethiol formed is eventually oxidized to sulfate and carbon
CC       dioxide, and the latter assimilated for autotrophic growth.
//
ID   1.8.1.22
DE   dissimilatory sulfite reductase.
AN   siroheme sulfite reductase.
CA   [DsrC protein]-trisulfide + NAD(+) + 3 H2O = [DsrC protein]-dithiol +
CA   sulfite + NADH + 3 H(+).
CC   -!- Contains siroheme.
CC   -!- The enzyme is essential in prokaryotic sulfur-based energy
CC       metabolism, including sulfate/sulfite reducing organisms, sulfur-
CC       oxidizing bacteria, and organosulfonate reducers.
CC   -!- The system comprises the DsrAB reductase and the DsrL protein, which
CC       form a tight complex.
CC   -!- The reaction involves the small protein DsrC, which is present in all
CC       the organisms that contain dissimilatory sulfite reductase.
CC   -!- In sulfite reducers the DsrL component transfers two electrons from
CC       NADH to the DsrAB component, which then reduces the sulfur in sulfite
CC       to an S(II) intermediate that forms (together with two cysteine
CC       residues of DsrC) a trisulfide.
CC   -!- In sulfur oxidizers the enzyme catalyzes the opposite reaction.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.8.99.5.
DR   O33998, DSRA_ALLVD ;  Q59109, DSRA_ARCFU ;  Q59110, DSRB_ARCFU ;
DR   P94694, DSVB_MEGG1 ;  P45575, DSVB_NITV2 ;  P45573, DSVC_NITV2 ;
//
ID   1.8.2.1
DE   sulfite dehydrogenase (cytochrome).
AN   sulfite dehydrogenase.
CA   2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] + sulfite + H2O = 2 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c] +
CA   sulfate + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Associated with cytochrome c-551. cf. EC 1.8.5.6.
//
ID   1.8.2.2
DE   thiosulfate dehydrogenase.
AN   tetrathionate synthase.
CA   2 thiosulfate + 2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] = tetrathionate + 2 Fe(II)-
CA   [cytochrome c] + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the reversible formation of a sulfur-sulfur bond
CC       between the sulfane atoms of two thiosulfate molecules, yielding
CC       tetrathionate and releasing two electrons.
CC   -!- In many bacterial species the enzyme is a diheme c-type cytochrome.
CC   -!- In a number of organisms, including Thiomonas intermedia and
CC       Sideroxydans lithotrophicus, a second diheme cytochrome (TsdB) acts
CC       as the electron acceptor.
CC   -!- However, some organisms, such as Allochromatium vinosum, lack TsdB.
CC   -!- The electron acceptor in these organisms may be the high-potential
CC       iron-sulfur protein (HiPIP).
DR   D3RVD4, TSDA_ALLVD ;  Q4FQB7, TSDA_PSYA2 ;  A4VND8, TSDA_STUS1 ;
DR   D5WYQ5, TSDA_THIK1 ;
