ID   1.8.2.5
DE   thiosulfate reductase (cytochrome).
CA   2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c3] + hydrogen sulfide + sulfite = 2 Fe(II)-
CA   [cytochrome c3] + thiosulfate.
CC   -!- The enzyme is found in sulfate-reducing bacteria.
CC   -!- The source of the electrons is molecular hydrogen, via EC 1.12.2.1.
CC   -!- The organisms utilize the sulfite that is produced for energy
CC       generation by EC 1.8.1.22.
//
ID   1.8.2.6
DE   S-disulfanyl-L-cysteine oxidoreductase.
AN   sulfur dehydrogenase.
CA   S-disulfanyl-L-cysteinyl-[SoxY protein] + 6 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] +
CA   3 H2O = S-sulfosulfanyl-L-cysteinyl-[SoxY protein] + 6 Fe(II)-[cytochrome
CA   c] + 7 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme is part of the Sox enzyme system, which participates in a
CC       bacterial thiosulfate oxidation pathway that produces sulfate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Paracoccus pantotrophus contains a
CC       molybdoprotein component and a diheme c-type cytochrome component.
CC   -!- The enzyme successively oxidizes the outer sulfur atom in [SoxY
CC       protein]-S-disulfanyl-L-cysteine, using three water molecules and
CC       forming [SoxY protein]-S-sulfosulfanyl-L-cysteine.
CC   -!- During the process, six electrons are transferred to the electron
CC       chain via cytochrome c.
//
ID   1.8.2.7
DE   thiocyanate desulfurase.
AN   thiocyanate dehydrogenase.
CA   thiocyanate + 2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] + H2O = cyanate + sulfur +
CA   2 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c] + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the haloalkaliphilic sulfur-oxidizing
CC       bacterium Thioalkalivibrio paradoxus, contains three copper ions in
CC       its active site.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the direct conversion of thiocyanate into cyanate and
CC       elemental sulfur without involvement of molecular oxygen.
//
