ID   1.8.4.12
DE   peptide-methionine (R)-S-oxide reductase.
AN   methionine S-oxide reductase.
AN   methionine S-oxide reductase (R-form oxidizing).
AN   methionine sulfoxide reductase.
AN   methionine sulfoxide reductase B.
AN   selenoprotein R.
CA   L-methionyl-[protein] + [thioredoxin]-disulfide + H2O = L-methionyl-(R)-
CA   S-oxide-[protein] + [thioredoxin]-dithiol.
CC   -!- The reaction occurs in the reverse direction to that shown above.
CC   -!- Exhibits high specificity for reduction of the R-form of methionine
CC       S-oxide, with higher activity being observed with L-methionine
CC       S-oxide than with D-methionine S-oxide.
CC   -!- While both free and protein-bound methionine (R)-S-oxide act as
CC       substrates, the activity with the peptide-bound form is far greater.
CC   -!- Plays a role in preventing oxidative-stress damage caused by reactive
CC       oxygen species by reducing the oxidized form of methionine back to
CC       methionine and thereby reactivating peptides that had been damaged.
CC   -!- The reaction proceeds via a sulfenic-acid intermediate.
CC   -!- For MsrB2 and MsrB3, thioredoxin is a poor reducing agent but
CC       thionein works well.
