ID   1.8.5.3
DE   respiratory dimethylsulfoxide reductase.
AN   dimethylsulfoxide reductase.
AN   DMSO reductase.
CA   dimethyl sulfide + a menaquinone + H2O = dimethyl sulfoxide +
CA   a menaquinol.
CC   -!- The enzyme participates in bacterial electron transfer pathways in
CC       which dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO) is the terminal electron acceptor.
CC   -!- It is composed of three subunits - DmsA contains a bis(guanylyl
CC       molybdopterin) cofactor and a [4Fe-4S] cluster, DmsB is an iron-
CC       sulfur protein, and DmsC is a transmembrane protein that anchors the
CC       enzyme and accepts electrons from the quinol pool.
CC   -!- The electrons are passed through DmsB to DmsA and on to DMSO.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also reduce pyridine-N-oxide and trimethylamine
CC       N-oxide to the corresponding amines with lower activity.
DR   P18775, DMSA_ECOLI ;  P45004, DMSA_HAEIN ;  Q9HR74, DMSA_HALSA ;
DR   Q57366, DSTOR_CERSP;  Q52675, DSTOR_RHOCA;
//
ID   1.8.5.4
DE   bacterial sulfide:quinone reductase.
AN   sulfide:quinone reductase.
CA   n a quinone + n hydrogen sulfide + n H(+) = polysulfur(n-2) + n a quinol.
CC   -!- Ubiquinone, plastoquinone or menaquinone can act as acceptor in
CC       different species.
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes the formation of sulfur globules.
CC   -!- It repeats the catalytic cycle without releasing the product,
CC       producing a polysulfide of up to 10 sulfur atoms.
CC   -!- The reaction stops when the maximum length of the polysulfide that
CC       can be accommodated in the sulfide oxidation pocket is achieved.
CC   -!- The enzyme also plays an important role in anoxygenic bacterial
CC       photosynthesis. cf. EC 1.8.5.8.
DR   Q7ZAG8, SQRD_ACIAM ;  B7JBP8, SQRD_ACIF2 ;  O67931, SQRD_AQUAE ;
//
ID   1.8.5.5
DE   thiosulfate reductase (quinone).
CA   a quinone + hydrogen sulfide + sulfite + 2 H(+) = thiosulfate + a quinol.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Salmonella enterica,
CC       is similar to EC 1.17.5.3.
CC   -!- The reaction occurs in vivo in the direction of thiosulfate
CC       disproportionation, which is highly endergonic.
CC   -!- It is driven by the proton motive force that occurs across the
CC       cytoplasmic membrane.
DR   P37600, PHSA_SALTY ;
//
ID   1.8.5.6
DE   sulfite dehydrogenase (quinone).
CA   a quinone + sulfite + H2O = a quinol + sulfate.
CC   -!- This membrane-bound bacterial enzyme catalyzes the direct oxidation
CC       of sulfite to sulfate in the cytoplasm.
CC   -!- Characterized from the bacteria Ruegeria pomeroyi and Allochromatium
CC       vinosum. cf. EC 1.8.2.1.
DR   D3RNN8, SOEA_ALLVD ;
//
ID   1.8.5.7
DE   glutathionyl-hydroquinone reductase.
CA   2-(glutathione-S-yl)-hydroquinone + glutathione = hydroquinone +
CA   glutathione disulfide.
CC   -!- This type of enzymes, which are found in bacteria, halobacteria,
CC       fungi, and plants, catalyze the glutathione-dependent reduction of
CC       glutathionyl-hydroquinones.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Sphingobium chlorophenolicum can act on
CC       halogenated substrates such as 2,6-dichloro-3-(glutathione-S-yl)-
CC       hydroquinone and 2,3,5-trichloro-6-(glutathione-S-yl)-hydroquinone.
CC   -!- Substrates for these enzymes are often formed spontaneously by
CC       interaction of benzoquinones with glutathione.
DR   Q8KN33, PCPF_SPHCR ;  P42620, YQJG_ECOLI ;
//
ID   1.8.5.8
DE   eukaryotic sulfide quinone oxidoreductase.
AN   SQOR.
CA   a quinone + hydrogen sulfide + glutathione + H(+) = S-sulfanylglutathione
CA   + a quinol.
CC   -!- This eukaryotic enzyme, located at the inner mitochondrial membrane,
CC       catalyzes the first step in the metabolism of sulfide.
CC   -!- While both sulfite and glutathione have been shown to act as sulfane
CC       sulfur acceptors in vitro, it is thought that the latter acts as the
CC       main acceptor in vivo.
CC   -!- The electrons are transferred via FAD and quinones to the electron
CC       transfer chain.
CC   -!- Unlike the bacterial homolog (EC 1.8.5.4), which repeats the
CC       catalytic cycle without releasing the product, producing a
CC       polysulfide, the eukaryotic enzyme transfers the persulfide to an
CC       acceptor at the end of each catalytic cycle.
DR   Q54DK1, SQOR_DICDI ;  Q9Y6N5, SQOR_HUMAN ;  Q9R112, SQOR_MOUSE ;
//
ID   1.8.5.9
DE   protein dithiol:quinone oxidoreductase DsbB.
CA   [DsbA protein]-dithiol + a quinone = [DsbA protein]-disulfide + a quinol.
CC   -!- DsbB is a protein found in Gram-negative bacteria that functions
CC       within a pathway for protein disulfide bond formation.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the oxidation of the DsbA protein by generating
CC       disulfide bonds de novo via the reduction of membrane quinones. cf.
CC       EC 1.8.4.15.
//
ID   1.8.5.10
DE   [DsrC]-trisulfide reductase.
CA   [DsrC protein]-dithiol + 2 a quinone + hydrogen sulfide + H(+) =
CA   [DsrC protein]-trisulfide + 2 a quinol.
CC   -!- This enzyme complex is present in both sulfate-reducing bacteria and
CC       sulfur-oxidizing bacteria, and acts in opposite directions during the
CC       reductive and oxidative pathways, respectively.
CC   -!- DsrM and DsrP contain b-type hemes, DsrJ contains c-type hemes, DsrO
CC       is a ferredoxin-like protein, and DsrK is the catalytic subunit that
CC       acts as a disulfide reductase on DsrC proteins that contain a
CC       trisulfide bridge.
CC   -!- The complex receives the electrons from the membrane quinone pool.
//
ID   1.8.6.1
DE   Transferred entry: 2.5.1.18.
//
ID   1.8.7.1
DE   assimilatory sulfite reductase (ferredoxin).
AN   sulfite reductase (ferredoxin).
CA   hydrogen sulfide + 6 oxidized [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + 3 H2O = sulfite +
CA   6 reduced [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + 7 H(+).
CC   -!- An iron protein.
CC   -!- The enzyme participates in sulfate assimilation.
CC   -!- While it is usually found in cyanobacteria, plants and algae, it has
CC       also been reported in bacteria.
CC   -!- Different from EC 1.8.1.22 which is involved in prokaryotic sulfur-
CC       based energy metabolism. cf. EC 1.8.1.2.
DR   Q73YC1, SIR1_MYCPA ;  O82802, SIR1_TOBAC ;  Q73XV0, SIR2_MYCPA ;
DR   Q9LZ66, SIR_ARATH  ;  O23813, SIR_MAIZE  ;  Q7TYP6, SIR_MYCBO  ;
DR   P9WJ02, SIR_MYCTO  ;  P9WJ03, SIR_MYCTU  ;  Q75NZ0, SIR_PEA    ;
DR   Q9AWB2, SIR_SOYBN  ;  P30008, SIR_SYNE7  ;  P72854, SIR_SYNY3  ;
//
ID   1.8.7.2
DE   ferredoxin:thioredoxin reductase.
CA   [thioredoxin]-disulfide + 2 reduced [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + 2 H(+) =
CA   [thioredoxin]-dithiol + 2 oxidized [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin].
CC   -!- The enzyme contains a [4Fe-4S] cluster and internal disulfide.
CC   -!- It forms a mixed disulfide with thioredoxin on one side, and docks
CC       ferredoxin on the other side, enabling two one-electron transfers.
CC   -!- The reduced thioredoxins generated by the enzyme activate the Calvin
CC       cycle enzymes EC 3.1.3.11, EC 3.1.3.37 and EC 2.7.1.19 as well as
CC       other chloroplast enzymes by disulfide reduction.
DR   P41348, FTRC1_SPIOL;  P41349, FTRC2_SPIOL;  Q9SJ89, FTRC_ARATH ;
DR   Q9TM25, FTRC_CYACA ;  O78461, FTRC_GUITH ;  P41347, FTRC_MAIZE ;
DR   Q6K471, FTRC_ORYSJ ;  P51386, FTRC_PORPU ;  Q1XDA1, FTRC_PYRYE ;
DR   O49856, FTRC_SOYBN ;  Q55389, FTRC_SYNY3 ;
