ID   1.10.3.15
DE   grixazone synthase.
CA   2 3-amino-4-hydroxybenzoate + N-acetyl-L-cysteine + 2 O2 + H(+) =
CA   grixazone B + CO2 + 4 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Streptomyces griseus,
CC       catalyzes an 8 electron oxidation.
CC   -!- Activation of the enzyme requires a copper chaperone (GriE).
CC   -!- It also acts on 3-amino-4-hydroxybenzaldehyde, giving grixazone A.
CC   -!- The second aldehyde group is presumably lost as formate.
CC   -!- The enzyme also catalyzes the reaction of EC 1.10.3.4.
DR   B1VTI5, GRIF_STRGG ;
//
ID   1.10.3.16
DE   dihydrophenazinedicarboxylate synthase.
CA   (1) (1R,6R)-1,4,5,5a,6,9-hexahydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + O2 =
CA   (1R,10aS)-1,4,10,10a-tetrahydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + H2O2.
CA   (2) (1R,10aS)-1,4,10,10a-tetrahydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + O2 =
CA   (5aS)-5,5a-dihydrophenazine-1,6-dicarboxylate + H2O2.
CA   (3) (1R,10aS)-1,4,10,10a-tetrahydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + O2 = (10aS)-
CA   10,10a-dihydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + H2O2.
CA   (4) (1R)-1,4,5,10-tetrahydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + O2 = (10aS)-10,10a-
CA   dihydrophenazine-1-carboxylate + H2O2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the bacteria Pseudomonas fluorescens 2-79
CC       and Burkholderia lata 383, is involved in biosynthesis of the reduced
CC       forms of phenazine, phenazine-1-carboxylate, and phenazine-1,6-
CC       dicarboxylate, where it catalyzes multiple reactions.
DR   Q51521, PHZD_PSECL ;  Q396C5, PHZG_BURL3 ;  Q51793, PHZG_PSEFL ;
//
ID   1.10.3.17
DE   superoxide oxidase.
AN   cytochrome b561.
CA   a ubiquinol + 2 O2 = 2 superoxide + a ubiquinone + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- This membrane-bound, di-heme containing enzyme, identified in the
CC       bacterium Escherichia coli, is responsible for the detoxification of
CC       superoxide in the periplasm.
CC   -!- In vivo the reaction proceeds in the opposite direction of that shown
CC       and produces oxygen.
CC   -!- Superoxide production was only observed when the enzyme was incubated
CC       in vitro with an excess of ubiquinol.
DR   P0ABE5, C561_ECOLI ;  P0ABE6, C561_SHIFL ;  Q9X6B2, C561_YERPE ;
//
ID   1.10.5.1
DE   ribosyldihydronicotinamide dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   NQO2.
AN   NRH:quinone oxidoreductase 2.
AN   N-ribosyldihydronicotinamide dehydrogenase (quinone).
AN   QR2.
AN   quinone reductase 2.
CA   1-(beta-D-ribofuranosyl)-1,4-dihydronicotinamide + a quinone + H(+) =
CA   beta-nicotinamide D-riboside + a quinol.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 1.6.5.2, this quinone reductase cannot use NADH or NADPH;
CC       instead it uses N-ribosyl- and N-alkyldihydronicotinamides.
CC   -!- Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, such as benz[a]anthracene, and the
CC       estrogens 17beta-estradiol and diethylstilbestrol are potent
CC       inhibitors, but dicoumarol is only a very weak inhibitor.
CC   -!- This enzyme can catalyze both 2-electron and 4-electron reductions,
CC       but one-electron acceptors, such as potassium ferricyanide, cannot be
CC       reduced.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.10.99.2.
DR   P16083, NQO2_HUMAN ;  Q9JI75, NQO2_MOUSE ;  Q5RBB9, NQO2_PONAB ;
DR   Q6AY80, NQO2_RAT   ;
