ID   1.11.1.13
DE   manganese peroxidase.
AN   Mn-dependent peroxidase.
CA   2 Mn(2+) + H2O2 + 2 H(+) = 2 Mn(3+) + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme from white rot basidiomycetes is involved in the oxidative
CC       degradation of lignin.
CC   -!- The enzyme oxidizes a bound Mn(2+) ion to Mn(3+) in the presence of
CC       hydrogen peroxide.
CC   -!- The product, Mn(3+), is released from the active site in the presence
CC       of a chelator (mostly oxalate and malate) that stabilizes it against
CC       disproportionation to Mn(2+) and insoluble Mn(4+).
CC   -!- The complexed Mn(3+) ion can diffuse into the lignified cell wall,
CC       where it oxidizes phenolic components of lignin and other organic
CC       substrates.
CC   -!- It is inactive with veratryl alcohol or nonphenolic substrates.
DR   Q02567, PEM1_PHACH ;  Q70LM3, PEM2_PHLRA ;  P78733, PEM3_PHACH ;
DR   Q96TS6, PEM3_PHLRA ;  P19136, PEM4_PHACH ;  Q9URB1, PEM5_PHACH ;
DR   P83918, PEM_IRPLA  ;
//
ID   1.11.1.14
DE   lignin peroxidase.
AN   diarylpropane oxygenase.
AN   diarylpropane peroxidase.
AN   ligninase I.
AN   LiP.
CA   (1) 1-(3,4-dimethoxyphenyl)-2-(2-methoxyphenoxy)propane-1,3-diol + H2O2 =
CA   3,4-dimethoxybenzaldehyde + guaiacol + glycolaldehyde + H2O.
CA   (2) 2 (3,4-dimethoxyphenyl)methanol + H2O2 = 2 (3,4-
CA   dimethoxyphenyl)methanol radical + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- Involved in the oxidative breakdown of lignin by white-rot
CC       basidiomycete fungi.
CC   -!- The reaction involves an initial oxidation of the heme iron by
CC       hydrogen peroxide, forming compound I (Fe(IV)=O radical cation) at
CC       the active site.
CC   -!- A single one-electron reduction of compound I by an electron derived
CC       from a substrate molecule yields compound II (Fe(IV)=O non-radical
CC       cation), followed by a second one-electron transfer that returns the
CC       enzyme to the ferric oxidation state.
CC   -!- The electron transfer events convert the substrate molecule into a
CC       transient cation radical intermediate that fragments spontaneously.
CC   -!- The enzyme can act on a wide range of aromatic compounds, including
CC       methoxybenzenes and nonphenolic beta-O-4 linked arylglycerol beta-
CC       aryl ethers, but cannot act directly on the lignin molecule, which is
CC       too large to fit into the active site.
CC   -!- However larger lignin molecules can be degraded in the presence of
CC       veratryl alcohol.
CC   -!- It has been suggested that the free radical that is formed when the
CC       enzyme acts on veratryl alcohol can diffuse into the lignified cell
CC       wall, where it oxidizes lignin and other organic substrates.
CC   -!- In the presence of high concentration of hydrogen peroxide and lack
CC       of substrate, the enzyme forms a catalytically inactive form
CC       (compound III).
CC   -!- This form can be rescued by interaction with two molecules of the
CC       free radical products.
CC   -!- In the case of veratryl alcohol, such an interaction yields two
CC       molecules of veratryl aldehyde.
DR   P49012, LIG2_PHACH ;  P21764, LIG3_PHACH ;  P11542, LIG4_PHACH ;
DR   P11543, LIG5_PHACH ;  P50622, LIG6_PHACH ;  P06181, LIG8_PHACH ;
DR   P31837, LIGA_PHACH ;  P20011, LIGA_TRAVE ;  P31838, LIGB_PHACH ;
DR   P20012, LIGB_TRAVE ;  P20013, LIGC_TRAVE ;  P20010, LIG_PHLRA  ;
//
ID   1.11.1.15
DE   Transferred entry: 1.11.1.24, 1.11.1.25, 1.11.1.26, 1.11.1.27, 1.11.1.28
DE   and 1.11.1.29.
//
ID   1.11.1.16
DE   versatile peroxidase.
AN   hybrid peroxidase.
AN   polyvalent peroxidase.
AN   VP.
CA   (1) 1-(4-hydroxy-3-methoxyphenyl)-2-(2-methoxyphenoxy)propane-1,3-diol +
CA   H2O2 = guaiacol + vanillin + glycolaldehyde + H2O.
CA   (2) 2 Mn(2+) + H2O2 + 2 H(+) = 2 Mn(3+) + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- This ligninolytic peroxidase combines the substrate-specificity
CC       characteristics of the two other ligninolytic peroxidases,
CC       EC 1.11.1.13 and EC 1.11.1.14.
CC   -!- Unlike these two enzymes, it is also able to oxidize phenols,
CC       hydroquinones and both low- and high-redox-potential dyes, due to a
CC       hybrid molecular architecture that involves multiple binding sites
CC       for substrates.
DR   K7N5M8, DYP2_AMYS7 ;  Q9UR19, VPL1_PLEER ;  O94753, VPL2_PLEER ;
DR   Q9UVP6, VPS1_PLEER ;
//
ID   1.11.1.17
DE   glutathione amide-dependent peroxidase.
CA   2 glutathione amide + H2O2 = glutathione amide disulfide + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- This enzyme, which has been characterized from the proteobacterium
CC       Marichromatium gracile, is a chimeric protein, containing a
CC       peroxiredoxin-like N-terminus and a glutaredoxin-like C terminus.
CC   -!- The enzyme has peroxidase activity toward hydrogen peroxide and
CC       several small alkyl hydroperoxides, and is thought to represent an
CC       early adaptation for fighting oxidative stress.
CC   -!- The glutathione amide disulfide produced by this enzyme can be
CC       restored to glutathione amide by EC 1.8.1.16.
DR   B3EWI1, GSPRX_MARGR;
//
ID   1.11.1.18
DE   bromide peroxidase.
AN   bromoperoxidase.
AN   eosinophil peroxidase.
CA   RH + Br(-) + H2O2 = RBr + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- Bromoperoxidases of red and brown marine algae (Rhodophyta and
CC       Phaeophyta) contain vanadate.
CC   -!- They catalyze the bromination of a range of organic molecules such as
CC       sesquiterpenes, forming stable C-Br bonds.
CC   -!- Bromoperoxidases also oxidize iodides.
DR   P81701, PRXV_ASCNO ;  Q8LLW7, PRXV_COROI ;  O81959, PRXV_CORPI ;
//
ID   1.11.1.19
DE   dye decolorizing peroxidase.
AN   DyP.
AN   DyP-type peroxidase.
CA   Reactive Blue 5 + 2 H2O2 = 2,2'-disulfonyl azobenzene + 3-[(4-amino-6-
CA   chloro-1,3,5-triazin-2-yl)amino]benzenesulfonate + phthalate + 2 H2O +
CA   2 H(+).
CC   -!- Heme proteins with proximal histidine secreted by basidiomycetous
CC       fungi and eubacteria.
CC   -!- They are similar to EC 1.11.1.16 versatile peroxidase (oxidation of
CC       Reactive Black 5, phenols, veratryl alcohol), but differ from the
CC       latter in their ability to efficiently oxidize a number of
CC       recalcitrant anthraquinone dyes, and inability to oxidize Mn(II).
CC   -!- The model substrate Reactive Blue 5 is converted with high efficiency
CC       via a so far unique mechanism that combines oxidative and hydrolytic
CC       steps and leads to the formation of phthalic acid.
CC   -!- Bacterial TfuDyP catalyzes sulfoxidation.
DR   K7N5M8, DYP2_AMYS7 ;  I2DBY1, DYP_AURAJ  ;  I2DBY2, DYP_EXIGL  ;
DR   I2DBY3, DYP_MYCEP  ;  Q47KB1, DYP_THEFY  ;  B0BK71, MSP1_MYCSO ;
//
ID   1.11.1.20
DE   prostamide/prostaglandin F2alpha synthase.
AN   prostamide/PGF synthase.
AN   prostamide/prostaglandin F synthase.
AN   prostamide F synthase.
AN   TPGF synthase.
CA   prostaglandin H2 + [thioredoxin]-dithiol = prostaglandin F2alpha +
CA   [thioredoxin]-disulfide.
CC   -!- The enzyme contains a thioredoxin-type disulfide as a catalytic
CC       group.
CC   -!- Prostamide H2 and prostaglandin H2 are the best substrates; the
CC       latter is converted to prostaglandin F2alpha.
CC   -!- The enzyme also reduces tert-butyl hydroperoxide, cumene
CC       hydroperoxide and H2O2, but not prostaglandin D2 or prostaglandin E2.
DR   C1C416, PXL2B_AQUCT;  Q58CY6, PXL2B_BOVIN;  Q6NV24, PXL2B_DANRE;
DR   Q8TBF2, PXL2B_HUMAN;  Q9DB60, PXL2B_MOUSE;  A9CQL8, PXL2B_PIG  ;
DR   Q5R7S9, PXL2B_PONAB;  D3ZVR7, PXL2B_RAT  ;  B5X9L9, PXL2B_SALSA;
DR   Q6AZG8, PXL2B_XENLA;  Q28IJ3, PXL2B_XENTR;
//
ID   1.11.1.21
DE   catalase peroxidase.
CA   (1) H2O2 + AH2 = A + 2 H2O.
CA   (2) 2 H2O2 = O2 + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- Differs from EC 1.11.1.7, peroxidase, in having a relatively high
CC       catalase (EC 1.11.1.6) activity with H2O2 as donor, releasing O2;
