ID   1.11.1.29
DE   mycoredoxin-dependent peroxiredoxin.
CA   [mycoredoxin]-L-dithiol + a hydroperoxide = [mycoredoxin]-L-disulfide +
CA   an alcohol + H2O.
CC   -!- Peroxiredoxins (Prxs) are a ubiquitous family of antioxidant
CC       proteins.
CC   -!- They can be divided into three classes: typical 2-Cys, atypical 2-Cys
CC       and 1-Cys peroxiredoxins.
CC   -!- The peroxidase reaction comprises two steps centered around a redox-
CC       active cysteine called the peroxidatic cysteine.
CC   -!- All three peroxiredoxin classes have the first step in common,
CC       in which the peroxidatic cysteine attacks the peroxide substrate and
CC       is oxidized to S-hydroxycysteine (a sulfenic acid).
CC   -!- The second step of the peroxidase reaction, the regeneration of
CC       cysteine from S-hydroxycysteine, distinguishes the three
CC       peroxiredoxin classes.
CC   -!- For typical 2-Cys Prxs, in the second step, the peroxidatic
CC       S-hydroxycysteine from one subunit is attacked by the 'resolving'
CC       cysteine located in the C-terminus of the second subunit, to form an
CC       intersubunit disulfide bond, which is then reduced by one of several
CC       cell-specific thiol-containing reductants completing the catalytic
CC       cycle.
CC   -!- In the atypical 2-Cys Prxs, both the peroxidatic cysteine and its
CC       resolving cysteine are in the same polypeptide, so their reaction
CC       forms an intrachain disulfide bond.
CC   -!- The 1-Cys Prxs conserve only the peroxidatic cysteine, so its
CC       regeneration involves direct interaction with a reductant molecule.
CC   -!- Mycoredoxin-dependent enzymes are found in Mycobacteria.
CC   -!- Following the reduction of the substrate, the sulfenic acid
CC       derivative of the peroxidatic cysteine forms a protein mixed
CC       disulfide with the N-terminal cysteine of mycoredoxin, which is then
CC       reduced by the C-terminal cysteine of mycoredoxin, restoring the
CC       peroxiredoxin to active state and resulting in an intra-protein
CC       disulfide in mycoredoxin.
CC   -!- The disulfide is eventually reduced by mycothiol.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.11.1.15.
DR   P65689, AHPE_MYCBO ;  P9WIE2, AHPE_MYCTO ;  P9WIE3, AHPE_MYCTU ;
//
ID   1.11.2.1
DE   unspecific peroxygenase.
AN   haloperoxidase-peroxygenase.
CA   RH + H2O2 = ROH + H2O.
CC   -!- Enzymes of this type include glycoproteins secreted by agaric
CC       basidiomycetes.
CC   -!- They catalyze the insertion of an oxygen atom from H2O2 into a wide
CC       variety of substrates, including aromatic rings such as naphthalene,
CC       toluene, phenanthrene, pyrene and p-nitrophenol, recalcitrant
CC       heterocycles such as pyridine, dibenzofuran, various ethers
CC       (resulting in O-dealkylation) and alkanes such as propane, hexane and
CC       cyclohexane.
CC   -!- Reactions catalyzed include hydroxylation, epoxidation, N-oxidation,
CC       sulfooxidation, O- and N-dealkylation, bromination and one-electron
CC       oxidations.
CC   -!- They have little or no activity toward chloride.
CC   -!- Mechanistically, the catalytic cycle of unspecific (mono)-
CC       peroxygenases combines elements of the 'shunt' pathway of cytochrome
CC       P450s (a side activity that utilizes a peroxide in place of dioxygen
CC       and NAD[P]H) and the classic heme peroxidase cycle.
DR   B9W4V6, APO1_CYCAE ;
//
ID   1.11.2.2
DE   myeloperoxidase.
AN   MPO.
AN   verdoperoxidase.
CA   chloride + H2O2 + H(+) = hypochlorous acid + H2O.
CC   -!- Is present in phagosomes of neutrophils and monocytes, where the
CC       hypochlorite produced is strongly bactericidal.
CC   -!- It differs from EC 1.11.1.10 in its preference for formation of
CC       hypochlorite over the chlorination of organic substrates under
CC       physiological conditions (pH 5-8).
CC   -!- Hypochlorite in turn forms a number of antimicrobial products (Cl2,
CC       chloramines, hydroxyl radical, singlet oxygen).
CC   -!- MPO also oxidizes bromide, iodide and thiocyanate.
CC   -!- In the absence of halides, it oxidizes phenols and has a moderate
CC       peroxygenase activity toward styrene.
DR   P05164, PERM_HUMAN ;  P11247, PERM_MOUSE ;
//
ID   1.11.2.3
DE   plant seed peroxygenase.
AN   plant peroxygenase.
AN   soybean peroxygenase.
CA   RH + ROOH = ROH + ROH.
CC   -!- A heme protein with calcium binding motif (caleosin-type).
CC   -!- Enzymes of this type include membrane-bound proteins found in seeds
CC       of different plants.
CC   -!- They catalyze the direct transfer of one oxygen atom from an organic
CC       hydroperoxide, which is reduced into its corresponding alcohol to a
CC       substrate which will be oxidized.
CC   -!- Reactions catalyzed include hydroxylation, epoxidation and
CC       sulfoxidation.
CC   -!- Preferred substrate and cosubstrate are unsaturated fatty acids and
CC       fatty acid hydroperoxides, respectively.
CC   -!- Plant seed peroxygenase is involved in the synthesis of cutin.
DR   O81270, PXG1_ARATH ;  G1JSL4, PXG1_AVESA ;  Q9FLN9, PXG2_ARATH ;
DR   O22788, PXG3_ARATH ;  Q9CAB7, PXG4_ARATH ;  Q9CAB8, PXG5_ARATH ;
DR   B3H7A9, PXG7_ARATH ;  A8B479, PXG_LILLO  ;  A2XVG1, PXG_ORYSI  ;
DR   Q7FAX1, PXG_ORYSJ  ;  A0A1I9R3Y5, PXG_PINEL  ;  A0A060LAL9, PXG_PINMS  ;
DR   Q9SQ57, PXG_SESIN  ;
//
ID   1.11.2.4
DE   fatty-acid peroxygenase.
AN   CYP152A1.
AN   fatty acid hydroxylase.
AN   P450 peroxygenase.
AN   P450SPalpha.
CA   (1) a 1,2-saturated fatty acid + H2O2 = a 2-hydroxy fatty acid + H2O.
CA   (2) a 2,3-saturated fatty acid + H2O2 = a 3-hydroxy fatty acid + H2O.
CC   -!- A cytosolic heme-thiolate protein with sequence homology to P450
CC       monooxygenases.
CC   -!- Unlike the latter, it needs neither NAD(P)H, dioxygen nor specific
CC       reductases for function.
CC   -!- Enzymes of this type are produced by bacteria (e.g. Sphingomonas
CC       paucimobilis, Bacillus subtilis).
CC   -!- Catalytic turnover rates are high compared with those of
CC       monooxygenation reactions as well as peroxide shunt reactions
CC       catalyzed by the common P450s.
CC   -!- A model substrate is myristate, but other saturated and unsaturated
CC       fatty acids are also hydroxylated.
CC   -!- Oxidizes the peroxidase substrate 3,3,5,5-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB)
CC       and peroxygenates aromatic substrates in a fatty-acid-dependent
CC       reaction.
DR   I3DZK9, CYPC_BACMM ;  O31440, CYPC_BACSU ;
//
ID   1.11.2.5
DE   3-methyl-L-tyrosine peroxygenase.
AN   3-methyl-L-tyrosine peroxidase.
AN   3-methyltyrosine peroxidase.
CA   3-methyl-L-tyrosine + H2O2 = 5-hydroxy-3-methyl-L-tyrosine + H2O.
CC   -!- The heme-containing peroxygenase from the bacterium Streptomyces
