ID   1.14.13.178
DE   methylxanthine N(1)-demethylase.
CA   (1) caffeine + NADH + O2 + H(+) = theobromine + formaldehyde + NAD(+) +
CA   H2O.
CA   (2) caffeine + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = theobromine + formaldehyde + NADP(+) +
CA   H2O.
CA   (3) theophylline + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = 3-methylxanthine + formaldehyde +
CA   NADP(+) + H2O.
CA   (4) theophylline + NADH + O2 + H(+) = 3-methylxanthine + formaldehyde +
CA   NAD(+) + H2O.
CA   (5) 1,7-dimethylxanthine + NADH + O2 + H(+) = 7-methylxanthine +
CA   formaldehyde + NAD(+) + H2O.
CA   (6) 1,7-dimethylxanthine + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = 7-methylxanthine +
CA   formaldehyde + NADP(+) + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Pseudomonas putida shares an
CC       NAD(P)H-FMN reductase subunit with EC 1.14.13.179, and has a 5-fold
CC       higher activity with NADH than with NADPH.
CC   -!- Also demethylates 1-methylxantine with lower efficiency.
CC   -!- Forms part of the degradation pathway of methylxanthines.
DR   H9N289, NDMA_PSEPU ;
//
ID   1.14.13.179
DE   methylxanthine N(3)-demethylase.
CA   (1) theobromine + NADH + O2 + H(+) = 7-methylxanthine + formaldehyde +
CA   NAD(+) + H2O.
CA   (2) theobromine + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = 7-methylxanthine + formaldehyde +
CA   NADP(+) + H2O.
CA   (3) 3-methylxanthine + NADH + O2 + H(+) = xanthine + formaldehyde +
CA   NAD(+) + H2O.
CA   (4) 3-methylxanthine + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = xanthine + formaldehyde +
CA   NADP(+) + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Pseudomonas putida shares an
CC       NAD(P)H-FMN reductase subunit with EC 1.14.13.178, and has higher
CC       activity with NADH than with NADPH.
CC   -!- Also demethylates caffeine and theophylline with lower efficiency.
CC   -!- Forms part of the degradation pathway of methylxanthines.
DR   H9N290, NDMB_PSEPU ;
//
ID   1.14.13.180
DE   aklavinone 12-hydroxylase.
AN   aklavinone 11-hydroxylase.
CA   aklavinone + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = epsilon-rhodomycinone + NADP(+) + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzymes from the Gram-positive bacteria Streptomyces peucetius
CC       and Streptomyces purpurascens participate in the biosynthesis of
CC       daunorubicin, doxorubicin and rhodomycins.
DR   P72495, DNRF_STRC0 ;  Q54530, DNRF_STREF ;  P32009, DNRF_STRPE ;
//
ID   1.14.13.181
DE   13-deoxydaunorubicin hydroxylase.
CA   (1) 13-deoxydaunorubicin + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = 13-dihydrodaunorubicin +
CA   NADP(+) + H2O.
CA   (2) 13-dihydrodaunorubicin + NADPH + O2 + H(+) = daunorubicin + NADP(+) +
CA   2 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzymes from the Gram-positive bacteria Streptomyces sp. C5 and
CC       Streptomyces peucetius show broad substrate specificity for
CC       structures based on an anthracycline aglycone, but have a strong
CC       preference for 4-methoxy anthracycline intermediates
CC       (13-deoxydaunorubicin and 13-dihydrodaunorubicin) over their
CC       4-hydroxy analogs (13-deoxycarminomycin and 13-dihydrocarminomycin),
CC       as well as a preference for substrates hydroxylated at the C-13
CC       rather than the C-14 position.
DR   Q93MI2, DOXA_STRC0 ;  Q9ZAU3, DOXA_STRPE ;  Q59971, DOXA_STRS5 ;
//
