ID   1.14.14.32
DE   17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone deacetylase.
AN   17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone 17,20-lyase.
AN   17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone acetaldehyde-lyase.
AN   CYP17.
AN   CYP17A1.
CA   (1) 17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone + reduced [NADPH--hemoprotein reductase]
CA   + O2 = androst-4-ene-3,17-dione + acetate + oxidized [NADPH--hemoprotein
CA   reductase] + H2O + 2 H(+).
CA   (2) 17alpha-hydroxypregnenolone + reduced [NADPH--hemoprotein reductase]
CA   + O2 = 3beta-hydroxyandrost-5-en-17-one + acetate + oxidized [NADPH-
CA   -hemoprotein reductase] + H2O + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- A microsomal protein that catalyzes two independent reactions at the
CC       same active site - the 17-hydroxylation of pregnenolone and
CC       progesterone, which is part of glucocorticoid hormones biosynthesis
CC       (EC 1.14.14.19), and the conversion of the 17-hydroxylated products
CC       via a 17,20-lyase reaction to form androstenedione and 3beta-
CC       hydroxyandrost-5-en-17-one, leading to sex hormone biosynthesis.
CC   -!- The activity of this reaction is dependent on the allosteric
CC       interaction of the enzyme with cytochrome b5 without any transfer of
CC       electrons from the cytochrome.
CC   -!- The enzymes from different organisms differ in their substrate
CC       specificity; while the enzymes from pig, hamster, and rat accept both
CC       17alpha-hydroxyprogesterone and 17alpha-hydroxypregnenolone,
CC       the enzymes from human, bovine, sheep, goat, and bison do not accept
CC       the former, and the enzyme from guinea pig does not accept the
CC       latter.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.1.2.30.
