ID   1.14.18.1
DE   tyrosinase.
AN   cresolase.
AN   monophenol monooxygenase.
AN   monophenol oxidase.
AN   N-acetyl-6-hydroxytryptophan oxidase.
AN   phenolase.
AN   tyrosine-dopa oxidase.
CA   (1) L-tyrosine + O2 = L-dopaquinone + H2O.
CA   (2) 2 L-dopa + O2 = 2 L-dopaquinone + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- Found in a broad variety of bacteria, fungi, plants, insects,
CC       crustaceans, and mammals, which is involved in the synthesis of
CC       betalains and melanin.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which is activated upon binding molecular oxygen,
CC       can catalyze both a monophenolase reaction cycle or a diphenolase
CC       reaction cycle.
CC   -!- During the monophenolase cycle, one of the bound oxygen atoms is
CC       transferred to a monophenol (such as L-tyrosine), generating an
CC       O-diphenol intermediate, which is subsequently oxidized to an
CC       o-quinone and released, along with a water molecule.
CC   -!- The enzyme remains in an inactive deoxy state, and is restored to the
CC       active oxy state by the binding of a new oxygen molecule.
CC   -!- During the diphenolase cycle the enzyme binds an external diphenol
CC       molecule (such as L-dopa) and oxidizes it to an O-quinone that is
CC       released along with a water molecule, leaving the enzyme in the
CC       intermediate met state.
CC   -!- The enzyme then binds a second diphenol molecule and repeats the
CC       process, ending in a deoxy state.
CC   -!- The second reaction is identical to that catalyzed by the related
CC       enzyme catechol oxidase (EC 1.10.3.1).
CC   -!- However, the latter can not catalyze the hydroxylation or
CC       monooxygenation of monophenols.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.14.17.2.
