ID   1.14.99.53
DE   lytic chitin monooxygenase.
AN   chitin oxidohydrolase.
AN   LPMO.
CA   [(1->4)-N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosaminyl]n+m + reduced acceptor + O2 =
CA   [(1->4)-N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosaminyl]m-1-(1->4)-2-(acetylamino)-2-deoxy-
CA   D-glucono-1,5-lactone + [(1->4)-N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosaminyl]n + acceptor
CA   + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme cleaves chitin in an oxidative manner, releasing fragments
CC       of chitin with an N-acetylamino-D-glucono-1,5-lactone at the reducing
CC       end.
CC   -!- The initially formed lactone at the reducing end of the shortend
CC       chitin chain quickly hydrolyzes spontaneously to the aldonic acid.
CC   -!- In vitro ascorbate can serve as reducing agent.
DR   Q838S1, LCHMO_ENTFA;  A3KIM2, LCHMO_STRA7;
//
ID   1.14.99.54
DE   lytic cellulose monooxygenase (C1-hydroxylating).
AN   LPMO.
AN   lytic polysaccharide monooxygenase.
CA   [(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]n+m + reduced acceptor + O2 = [(1->4)-beta-D-
CA   glucosyl]m-1-(1->4)-D-glucono-1,5-lactone + [(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]n +
CA   acceptor + H2O.
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in fungi and bacteria, cleaves cellulose in an
CC       oxidative manner.
CC   -!- The cellulose fragments that are formed contain a D-glucono-1,5-
CC       lactone residue at the reducing end, which hydrolyzes quickly and
CC       spontaneously to the aldonic acid.
CC   -!- The electrons are provided in vivo by the cytochrome b domain of
CC       EC 1.1.99.18.
CC   -!- Ascorbate can serve as the electron donor in vitro.
DR   A3KKC4, LCEMO_STRA7;
//
ID   1.14.99.55
DE   lytic starch monooxygenase.
AN   LPMO.
CA   starch + reduced acceptor + O2 = D-glucono-1,5-lactone-terminated malto-
CA   oligosaccharides + short-chain malto-oligosaccharides + acceptor + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme cleaves starch in an oxidative manner.
CC   -!- It releases fragments of starch with a D-glucono-1,5-lactone at the
CC       reducing end.
CC   -!- The initially formed alpha-D-glucono-1,5-lactone at the reducing end
CC       of the shortend amylose chain quickly hydrolyzes spontaneously to the
CC       aldonic acid.
CC   -!- In vitro ascorbate has been found to be able to serve as reducing
CC       agent.
DR   Q0CGA6, AA13_ASPTN ;  Q5B1W7, AA13_EMENI ;  Q7SCE9, AA13_NEUCR ;
DR   A0A151V4J3, AA13_PYRO7 ;  Q5B027, AASB_EMENI ;
//
ID   1.14.99.56
DE   lytic cellulose monooxygenase (C4-dehydrogenating).
CA   [(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]n+m + reduced acceptor + O2 = 4-dehydro-beta-D-
CA   glucosyl-[(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]n-1 + [(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]m +
CA   acceptor + H2O.
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in fungi and bacteria, cleaves cellulose in an
CC       oxidative manner.
