ID   1.14.99.63
DE   beta-carotene 4-ketolase.
AN   beta-C-4 oxygenase.
AN   beta-carotene ketolase.
CA   (1) all-trans-beta-carotene + 2 AH2 + 2 O2 = echinenone + 2 A + 3 H2O.
CA   (2) echinenone + 2 AH2 + 2 O2 = canthaxanthin + 2 A + 3 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, studied from algae, plants, fungi, and bacteria, adds an
CC       oxo group at position 4 of a carotenoid beta ring.
CC   -!- It is involved in the biosynthesis of carotenoids such as astaxanthin
CC       and flexixanthin.
CC   -!- The enzymes does not act on beta rings that are hydroxylated at
CC       position 3, such as in zeaxanthin (cf. EC 1.14.99.64).
CC   -!- The enzyme from the yeast Xanthophyllomyces dendrorhous is
CC       bifuntional and also catalyzes the activity of EC 1.14.15.24.
DR   Q6J3N5, BKT3_HAELA ;  Q4VKB4, BKT_CHLRE  ;  G3F5K2, BKT_PROBT  ;
DR   Q39982, CRTW_HAELA ;  Q44261, CRTW_PARS1 ;  P54972, CRTW_PARSN ;
//
ID   1.14.99.64
DE   zeaxanthin 4-ketolase.
CA   (1) all-trans-zeaxanthin + 2 AH2 + 2 O2 = all-trans-adonixanthin + 2 A +
CA   3 H2O.
CA   (2) all-trans-adonixanthin + 2 AH2 + 2 O2 = all-trans-(3S,3'S)-
CA   astaxanthin + 2 A + 3 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme has a similar activity to that of EC 1.14.99.63,
CC       but unlike that enzyme is able to also act on zeaxanthin.
DR   Q4VKB4, BKT_CHLRE  ;
//
ID   1.14.99.65
DE   4-amino-L-phenylalanyl-[CmlP-peptidyl-carrier-protein] 3-hydroxylase.
CA   4-amino-L-phenylalanyl-[peptidyl-carrier protein] + AH2 + O2 = (2R)-2-
CA   (4-aminophenyl)-L-seryl-[peptidyl-carrier protein] + A + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Streptomyces venezuelae,
CC       participates in the biosynthesis of the antibiotic chloramphenicol.
CC   -!- It carries an oxygen-bridged dinuclear iron cluster.
CC   -!- The native electron donor remains unknown, and the enzyme was assayed
CC       in vitro using sodium dithionite.
CC   -!- The enzyme only acts on its substrate when it is loaded onto the
CC       peptidyl-carrier domain of the CmlP non-ribosomal peptide synthase.
DR   F2RB80, CMLA_STRVP ;
//
ID   1.14.99.66
DE   [histone-H3]-N(6),N(6)-dimethyl-L-lysine(4) FAD-dependent demethylase.
AN   lysine-specific histone demethylase 1.
CA   N(6),N(6)-dimethyl-L-lysyl(4)-[histone H3] + 2 A + 2 H2O = L-lysyl(4)-
CA   [histone H3] + 2 formaldehyde + 2 AH2.
CC   -!- The enzyme specifically removes methyl groups from mono- and
CC       dimethylated lysine(4) of histone 3.
CC   -!- During the reaction the substrate is oxidized by the FAD cofactor of
CC       the enzyme to generate the corresponding imine, which is subsequently
CC       hydrolyzed in the form of formaldehyde.
CC   -!- The enzyme is similar to flavin amine oxidases, and differs from all
CC       other known histone lysine demethylases, which are iron(II)- and
CC       2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenases.
CC   -!- The physiological electron acceptor is not known with certainty. In
CC       vitro the enzyme can use oxygen, which is reduced to hydrogen
CC       peroxide, but generation of hydrogen peroxide in the chromatin
CC       environment is unlikely as it will result in oxidative damage of DNA.
DR   Q9XWP6, KDM1A_CAEEL;  O60341, KDM1A_HUMAN;  Q6ZQ88, KDM1A_MOUSE;
DR   A0A8I6G2K8, KDM1A_RAT  ;  Q21988, KDM1B_CAEEL;  Q8NB78, KDM1B_HUMAN;
DR   Q8CIG3, KDM1B_MOUSE;  Q9Y802, LSD1_SCHPO ;  Q10135, LSD2_SCHPO ;
//
ID   1.14.99.67
DE   alpha-N-dichloroacetyl-p-aminophenylserinol N-oxygenase.
CA   alpha-N-dichloroacetyl-p-aminophenylserinol + AH2 + 2 O2 =
CA   chloramphenicol + A + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Streptomyces venezuelae,
CC       is involved in the biosynthesis of the antibiotic chloramphenicol.
CC   -!- It contains a carboxylate-bridged binuclear non-heme iron cluster.
CC   -!- The components of the native electron chain have not been identified,
CC       although the immediate donor is likely to be an iron-sulfur protein.
CC   -!- The reaction mechanism involves formation of an extremely stable
CC       peroxo intermediate that catalyzes three individual two-electron
CC       oxidations via a hydroxylamine and a nitroso intermediates without
CC       releasing the intermediates. cf. EC 1.14.99.68.
DR   F2RB83, CMLI_STRVP ;
//
ID   1.14.99.68
DE   4-aminobenzoate N-oxygenase.
CA   4-aminobenzoate + AH2 + 2 O2 = 4-nitrobenzoate + A + 2 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Streptomyces thioluteus,
CC       catalyzes an early step in the biosynthesis of the antibiotic
CC       aureothin.
CC   -!- It contains a carboxylate-bridged binuclear non-heme iron cluster.
CC   -!- The native electron donor has not been identified, but is likely an
CC       iron-sulfur protein.
CC   -!- The reaction mechanism involves formation of an extremely stable
CC       peroxo intermediate that catalyzes three two-electron oxidations via
CC       a hydroxylamine and dihydroxylamine intermediates. cf. EC 1.14.99.67.
DR   Q70KH9, AURF_STRTU ;
//
ID   1.14.99.69
DE   tRNA 2-(methylsulfanyl)-N(6)-isopentenyladenosine(37) hydroxylase.
AN   tRNA 2-(methylsulfanyl)-N(6)-dimethylallyladenosine(37) hydroxylase.
AN   tRNA 2-methylthio-N(6)-isopentenyl adenosine(37) hydroxylase.
CA   2-methylsulfanyl-N(6)-dimethylallyladenosine(37) in tRNA + AH2 + O2 =
CA   N(6)-[(2E)-4-hydroxy-3-methylbut-2-en-1-yl]-2-
CA   (methylsulfanyl)adenosine(37) in tRNA + A + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, found only within a small subset of facultative anaerobic
CC       bacteria, belongs to the nonheme diiron family.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Salmonella typhimurium was shown to catalyze a
CC       stereoselective (E) hydroxylation at the terminal C(4)-position of
CC       the prenyl group.
DR   Q88KV1, MIAE_PSEPK ;  Q08015, MIAE_SALTY ;
