ID   1.16.9.1
DE   iron:rusticyanin reductase.
CA   Fe(II) + rusticyanin = Fe(III) + reduced rusticyanin.
CC   -!- The enzyme in Acidithiobacillus ferrooxidans is a component of an
CC       electron transfer chain from Fe(II), comprising this enzyme,
CC       the copper protein rusticyanin, cytochrome c4, and cytochrome c
CC       oxidase (EC 7.1.1.9).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.16.98.1.
//
ID   1.16.98.1
DE   Transferred entry: 1.16.9.1.
//
ID   1.16.99.1
DE   [Co(II) methylated amine-specific corrinoid protein] reductase.
CA   (1) 2 Co(II)-[methylamine-specific corrinoid protein] + AH2 + ATP + H2O =
CA   2 Co(I)-[methylamine-specific corrinoid protein] + A + ADP + phosphate +
CA   3 H(+).
CA   (2) 2 Co(II)-[dimethylamine-specific corrinoid protein] + AH2 + ATP +
CA   H2O = 2 Co(I)-[dimethylamine-specific corrinoid protein] + A + ADP +
CA   phosphate + 3 H(+).
CA   (3) 2 Co(II)-[trimethylamine-specific corrinoid protein] + AH2 + ATP +
CA   H2O = 2 Co(I)-[trimethylamine-specific corrinoid protein] + A + ADP +
CA   phosphate + 3 H(+).
CC   -!- Methyltrophic corrinoid proteins must have the cobalt atom in the
CC       active cobalt(I) state to become methylated.
CC   -!- Because the cobalt(I)/cobalt(II) transformation has a very low redox
CC       potential the corrinoid cofactor is subject to adventitious oxidation
CC       to the cobalt(II) state, which renders the proteins inactive.
CC   -!- This enzyme, characterized from the methanogenic archaeon
CC       Methanosarcina barkeri, reduces cobalt(II) back to cobalt(I),
CC       restoring activity.
CC   -!- The enzyme acts on the corrinoid proteins involved in methanogenesis
CC       from methylamine, dimethylamine, and trimethylamine, namely MtmC,
CC       MtbC, and MttC, respectively.
CC   -!- While in vitro the enzyme can use Ti(III)-citrate as the electron
CC       donor, the in vivo donor is not known.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Methanosarcina barkeri contains a C-terminal [4Fe-4S]
CC       ferredoxin-like domain.
DR   B8Y445, RAMA_METBA ;
//
ID   1.17.1.1
DE   CDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxyglucose reductase.
AN   CDP-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose-3-dehydrogenase system.
AN   CDP-4-keto-6-deoxyglucose reductase.
AN   CDP-4-keto-deoxy-glucose reductase.
AN   cytidine diphosphate 4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose-3-dehydrogenase.
AN   cytidine diphospho-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose reductase.
AN   NAD(P)H:CDP-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose oxidoreductase.
CA   (1) CDP-4-dehydro-3,6-dideoxy-alpha-D-glucose + NAD(+) + H2O = CDP-4-
CA   dehydro-6-deoxy-alpha-D-glucose + NADH + H(+).
CA   (2) CDP-4-dehydro-3,6-dideoxy-alpha-D-glucose + NADP(+) + H2O = CDP-4-
CA   dehydro-6-deoxy-alpha-D-glucose + NADPH + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme consists of two proteins.
CC   -!- One forms an enzyme-bound adduct of the CDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxyglucose
CC       with pyridoxamine phosphate, in which the 3-hydroxy group has been
CC       removed.
CC   -!- The second catalyzes the reduction of this adduct by NAD(P)H and
CC       release of the CDP-4-dehydro-3,6-dideoxy-D-glucose and pyridoxamine
CC       phosphate.
//
ID   1.17.1.2
DE   Transferred entry: 1.17.7.4.
//
ID   1.17.1.3
DE   leucoanthocyanidin reductase.
AN   leucocyanidin reductase.
CA   (2R,3S)-catechin + NADP(+) + H2O = (2R,3S,4S)-leucocyanidin + NADPH +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- Catalyzes the synthesis of catechin, catechin-4beta-ol
CC       (leucocyanidin) and the related flavan-3-ols afzelechin and
CC       gallocatechin, which are initiating monomers in the synthesis of
CC       plant polymeric proanthocyanidins or condensed tannins.
CC   -!- While 2,3-trans-3,4-cis-leucocyanidin is the preferred flavan-3,4-
CC       diol substrate, 2,3-trans-3,4-cis-leucodelphinidin and 2,3-trans-3,
CC       4-cis-leucopelargonidin can also act as substrates, but more slowly.
CC   -!- NADH can replace NADPH but is oxidized more slowly.
DR   Q84V83, LAR_DESUN  ;
//
ID   1.17.1.4
DE   xanthine dehydrogenase.
AN   NAD-xanthine dehydrogenase.
AN   xanthine/NAD(+) oxidoreductase.
AN   xanthine-NAD oxidoreductase.
AN   xanthine oxidoreductase.
CA   xanthine + NAD(+) + H2O = urate + NADH + H(+).
CC   -!- Acts on a variety of purines and aldehydes, including hypoxanthine.
CC   -!- The mammalian enzyme can also convert all-trans retinol to all-trans-
CC       retinoate, while the substrate is bound to a retinoid-binding
CC       protein.
CC   -!- The enzyme from eukaryotes contains [2Fe-2S], FAD and a molybdenum
CC       center.
CC   -!- The mammallian enzyme predominantly exists as the NAD-dependent
CC       dehydrogenase (EC 1.17.1.4).
CC   -!- During purification the enzyme is largely converted to an
CC       O2-dependent form, EC 1.17.3.2.
CC   -!- The conversion can be triggered by several mechanisms, including the
CC       oxidation of cysteine thiols to form disulfide bonds (which can be
CC       catalyzed by EC 1.8.4.7 in the presence of glutathione disulfide) or
CC       limited proteolysis, which results in irreversible conversion.
CC   -!- The conversion can also occur in vivo.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.1.204 and EC 1.2.1.37.
