//
ID   1.17.1.10
DE   formate dehydrogenase (NADP(+)).
CA   formate + NADP(+) = CO2 + NADPH.
CC   -!- Characterized from the bacterium Moorella thermoacetica.
CC   -!- It is extremely sensitive to oxygen.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.2.1.43.
DR   P77908, FDHA_NEOTH ;  P77907, FDHB_NEOTH ;
//
ID   1.17.1.11
DE   formate dehydrogenase (NAD(+), ferredoxin).
AN   electron-bifurcating formate dehydrogenase.
CA   2 formate + 2 oxidized [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + NAD(+) = 2 reduced [2Fe-
CA   2S]-[ferredoxin] + 2 CO2 + NADH + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme complex, isolated from the bacterium Gottschalkia
CC       acidurici, couples the reduction of NAD(+) and the reduction of
CC       ferredoxin with formate via flavin-based electron bifurcation.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.2.1.93.
//
ID   1.17.2.1
DE   nicotinate dehydrogenase (cytochrome).
AN   nicotinate hydroxylase.
AN   nicotinic acid hydroxylase.
CA   2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome] + nicotinate + H2O = 2 Fe(II)-[cytochrome] +
CA   6-hydroxynicotinate + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- This two-component enzyme from Pseudomonas belongs to the family of
CC       xanthine dehydrogenases, but differs from most other members of this
CC       family.
CC   -!- While most members contain an FAD cofactor, the large subunit of this
CC       enzyme contains three c-type cytochromes, enabling it to interact
CC       with the electron transfer chain, probably by delivering the
CC       electrons to a cytochrome oxidase.
DR   Q88FX9, NICA_PSEPK ;  Q88FX8, NICB_PSEPK ;
//
ID   1.17.2.2
DE   lupanine 17-hydroxylase (cytochrome c).
AN   lupanine dehydrogenase (cytochrome c).
CA   lupanine + 2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] + H2O = 17-hydroxylupanine +
CA   2 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c] + 2 H(+).
DR   Q934G0, LUH_PSESP  ;
//
ID   1.17.2.3
DE   formate dehydrogenase (cytochrome c-553).
CA   2 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c553] + formate = 2 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c553] +
CA   CO2 + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme has been characterized from the bacterium Desulfovibrio
CC       vulgaris.
CC   -!- In vitro, yeast cytochrome c, ferricyanide and phenazine methosulfate
CC       can act as acceptors.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.2.2.3.
DR   Q727P3, FDNGA_NITV2;
//
ID   1.17.3.1
DE   pteridine oxidase.
CA   2-amino-4-hydroxypteridine + O2 = 2-amino-4,7-dihydroxypteridine +
CA   chemical entity.
CC   -!- Does not act on hypoxanthine.
CC   -!- Different from EC 1.17.3.2.
//
ID   1.17.3.2
DE   xanthine oxidase.
AN   hypoxanthine oxidase.
AN   hypoxanthine-xanthine oxidase.
AN   Schardinger enzyme.
AN   xanthine oxidoreductase.
CA   xanthine + O2 + H2O = urate + H2O2.
CC   -!- Also oxidizes hypoxanthine, some other purines and pterins,
CC       and aldehydes, but is distinct from EC 1.2.3.1.
CC   -!- Under some conditions the product is mainly superoxide rather than
CC       peroxide: R-H + H2O + 2 O2 = ROH + 2 O2(.-) + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The mammallian enzyme predominantly exists as an NAD-dependent
CC       dehydrogenase (EC 1.17.1.4).
CC   -!- During purification the enzyme is largely converted to the
CC       O2-dependent xanthine oxidase form (EC 1.17.3.2).
CC   -!- The conversion can be triggered by several mechanisms, including the
CC       oxidation of cysteine thiols to form disulfide bonds (which can be
CC       catalyzed by EC 1.8.4.7 in the presence of glutathione disulfide) or
CC       limited proteolysis, which results in irreversible conversion.
CC   -!- The conversion can also occur in vivo.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.3.22 and EC 1.2.3.2.
DR   P80457, XDH_BOVIN  ;  P47990, XDH_CHICK  ;  Q9MYW6, XDH_FELCA  ;
DR   P47989, XDH_HUMAN  ;  Q00519, XDH_MOUSE  ;  P22985, XDH_RAT    ;
//
ID   1.17.3.3
DE   6-hydroxynicotinate dehydrogenase.
AN   6-hydroxynicotinate hydroxylase.
AN   6-hydroxynicotinic acid dehydrogenase.
AN   6-hydroxynicotinic acid hydroxylase.
CA   6-hydroxynicotinate + O2 + H2O = 2,6-dihydroxynicotinate + H2O2.
CC   -!- The enzyme also has a catalytically essential, labile selenium that
CC       can be removed by reaction with cyanide.
CC   -!- In Bacillus niacini, this enzyme is required for growth on nicotinic
CC       acid.
//
ID   1.17.3.4
DE   juglone 3-hydroxylase.
AN   naphthoquinone hydroxylase.
CA   2 juglone + O2 = 2 3,5-dihydroxy-1,4-naphthoquinone + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- Even though oxygen is consumed, molecular oxygen is not incorporated
CC       into the product.
CC   -!- Catalysis starts by incorporation of an oxygen atom from a water
CC       molecule into the substrate.
CC   -!- The naphthalene-1,2,4,8-tetrol intermediate is then oxidized by
CC       molecular oxygen, which is reduced to water.
CC   -!- Also acts on 1,4-naphthoquinone, naphthazarin and 2-chloro-1,4-
CC       naphthoquinone.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.14.99.27.
//
ID   1.17.3.5
DE   4-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate 2-dehydrogenase.
AN   4-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate 2-desaturase.
CA   4-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylate + O2 = 4-oxocyclohex-2-ene-1-carboxylate +
CA   H2O2.
CC   -!- Contains FAD.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Corynebacterium
CC       cyclohexanicum, participates in a cyclohexane-1-carboxylate
CC       degradation pathway.
DR   P0DXE2, CHCC1_SINCY;
//
ID   1.17.4.1
DE   ribonucleoside-diphosphate reductase.
AN   ribonucleotide reductase.
CA   a 2'-deoxyribonucleoside 5'-diphosphate + [thioredoxin]-disulfide + H2O =
CA   a ribonucleoside 5'-diphosphate + [thioredoxin]-dithiol.
CC   -!- This enzyme is responsible for the de novo conversion of
CC       ribonucleoside diphosphates into deoxyribonucleoside diphosphates,
CC       which are essential for DNA synthesis and repair.
CC   -!- There are three types of this enzyme differing in their cofactors.
CC   -!- Class Ia enzymes contain a diiron(III)-tyrosyl radical, class Ib
CC       enzymes contain a dimanganese-tyrosyl radical, and class II enzymes
CC       contain adenosylcobalamin.
CC   -!- In all cases the cofactors are involved in generation of a transient
CC       thiyl (sulfanyl) radical on a cysteine residue, which attacks the
CC       substrate, forming a ribonucleotide 3'-radical, followed by water
CC       loss to form a ketyl (alpha-oxoalkyl) radical.
CC   -!- The ketyl radical is reduced to 3'-keto-deoxynucleotide concomitant
CC       with formation of a disulfide anion radical between two cysteine
CC       residues.
CC   -!- A proton-coupled electron-transfer from the disulfide radical to the
CC       substrate generates a 3'-deoxynucleotide radical, and the the final
CC       product is formed when the hydrogen atom that was initially removed
CC       from the 3'-position of the nucleotide by the thiyl radical is
CC       returned to the same position.
CC   -!- The disulfide bridge is reduced by the action of thioredoxin. cf.
CC       EC 1.1.98.6 and EC 1.17.4.2.
