ID   1.17.98.4
DE   formate dehydrogenase (acceptor).
AN   formate dehydrogenase H.
AN   formate dehydrogenase O.
CA   formate + A + H(+) = AH2 + CO2.
CC   -!- Formate dehydrogenase H is a cytoplasmic enzyme that oxidizes formate
CC       without oxygen transfer, transferring electrons to a hydrogenase.
CC   -!- The two enzymes form the formate-hydrogen lyase complex.
CC   -!- The enzyme contains a selenocysteine residue.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.99.33 and EC 1.17.99.7.
DR   P07658, FDHF_ECOLI ;
//
ID   1.17.98.5
DE   hydrogen-dependent carbon dioxide reductase.
CA   formate + H(+) = H2 + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, originally characterized from the acetogenic bacterium
CC       Acetobacterium woodii, catalyzes the reduction of CO2 to formate with
CC       electrons provided by the oxidation of molecular hydrogen.
CC   -!- It consists of two catalytic subunits - a hydrogenase and a formate
CC       dehydrogenase, which are connected by two types of electron transfer
CC       subunits.
CC   -!- The enzyme forms membrane-anchored nanowires in which the electron-
CC       transfer subunits oligomerize through their C-terminal helices to
CC       form the backbone of the filament.
CC   -!- The rapid electron transfer via the filaments enhances the activity
CC       of the enzyme, resulting in a very high catalytic turnover rate.
CC   -!- While the reaction is similar to that catalyzed by the formate
CC       hydrogenlyase complex, which is formed by EC 1.17.98.4 and
CC       EC 1.12.7.2, this enzyme is not biased toward formate oxidation,
CC       and does not require ferredoxin in order to complete the reaction
CC       (although it is able to use reduced ferredoxin instead of dihydrogen
CC       for the reduction of CO2).
//
ID   1.17.99.1
DE   Transferred entry: 1.17.9.1.
//
ID   1.17.99.2
DE   ethylbenzene hydroxylase.
AN   ethylbenzene dehydrogenase.
CA   ethylbenzene + A + H2O = (S)-1-phenylethanol + AH2.
CC   -!- Involved in the anaerobic catabolism of ethylbenzene by denitrifying
CC       bacteria.
CC   -!- Ethylbenzene is the preferred substrate; the enzyme from some strains
CC       oxidizes propylbenzene, 1-ethyl-4-fluorobenzene, 3-methylpent-2-ene
CC       and ethylidenecyclohexane.
CC   -!- Toluene is not oxidized.
CC   -!- p-benzoquinone or ferrocenium can act as electron acceptor.
//
ID   1.17.99.3
DE   3alpha,7alpha,12alpha-trihydroxy-5beta-cholestanoyl-CoA 24-hydroxylase.
AN   3alpha,7alpha,12alpha-trihydroxy-5beta-cholestan-26-oate 24-hydroxylase.
AN   3alpha,7alpha,12alpha-trihydroxy-5beta-cholestanoyl-CoA oxidase.
AN   THCA-CoA oxidase.
AN   THC-CoA oxidase.
AN   trihydroxycoprostanoyl-CoA oxidase.
CA   (25R)-3alpha,7alpha,12alpha-trihydroxy-5beta-cholestan-26-oyl-CoA + A +
CA   H2O = (24R,25R)-3alpha,7alpha,12alpha,24-tetrahydroxy-5beta-cholestan-26-
CA   oyl-CoA + AH2.
CC   -!- Requires ATP.
CC   -!- The reaction in mammals possibly involves dehydrogenation to give a
CC       24(25)-double bond followed by hydration.
CC   -!- However, in amphibians such as the Oriental fire-bellied toad
CC       (Bombina orientalis), it is probable that the product is formed via
CC       direct hydroxylation of the saturated side chain of (25R)-
CC       3alpha,7alpha,12alpha-trihydroxy-5beta-cholestan-26-oate and not via
CC       hydration of a 24(25) double bond.
CC   -!- In microsomes, the free acid is preferred to the coenzyme A ester,
CC       whereas in mitochondria, the coenzyme A ester is preferred to the
CC       free-acid form of the substrate.
DR   Q99424, ACOX2_HUMAN;  Q9QXD1, ACOX2_MOUSE;  O02767, ACOX2_RABIT;
DR   P97562, ACOX2_RAT  ;
//
ID   1.17.99.4
DE   uracil/thymine dehydrogenase.
AN   uracil dehydrogenase.
AN   uracil oxidase.
AN   uracil-thymine oxidase.
CA   (1) uracil + A + H2O = barbiturate + AH2 + H(+).
CA   (2) thymine + A + H2O = 5-methylbarbiturate + AH2 + H(+).
CC   -!- Forms part of the oxidative pyrimidine-degrading pathway in some
CC       microorganisms, along with EC 3.5.2.1 and EC 3.5.1.95.
CC   -!- Mammals, plants and other microorganisms utilize the reductive
CC       pathway, comprising EC 1.3.1.1 or EC 1.3.1.2, EC 3.5.2.2 and
CC       EC 3.5.1.6, with the ultimate degradation products being an L-amino
CC       acid, NH3 and CO2.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.1.99.19 and EC 1.2.99.1.
//
ID   1.17.99.5
DE   Transferred entry: 1.1.1.395.
