ID   1.19.1.1
DE   flavodoxin--NADP(+) reductase.
AN   FPR.
CA   reduced [flavodoxin] + NADP(+) = oxidized [flavodoxin] + NADPH + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- This activity occurs in some prokaryotes and algae that possess
CC       flavodoxin, and provides low-potential electrons for a variety of
CC       reactions such as nitrogen fixation, sulfur assimilation and amino
CC       acid biosynthesis.
CC   -!- In photosynthetic organisms it is involved in the photosynthetic
CC       electron transport chain.
CC   -!- The enzyme also catalyzes EC 1.18.1.2.
DR   P57641, FENR_BUCAI ;  Q9Z615, FENR_BUCAP ;  Q89A28, FENR_BUCBP ;
DR   P28861, FENR_ECOLI ;  Q9L6V3, FENR_RHOCA ;  P28901, FENR_SHIFL ;
//
ID   1.19.6.1
DE   nitrogenase (flavodoxin).
CA   N2 + 4 reduced [flavodoxin] + 16 ATP + 16 H2O = 4 oxidized [flavodoxin] +
CA   H2 + 2 NH4(+) + 16 ADP + 16 phosphate + 18 H(+).
CC   -!- Composed of two components, dinitrogen reductase and dinitrogenase,
CC       that can be separated but are both required for nitrogenase activity.
CC   -!- Dinitrogen reductase is a [4Fe-4S] protein, which, at the expense of
CC       ATP, transfers electrons from a dedicated flavodoxin to
CC       dinitrogenase.
CC   -!- Dinitrogenase is a protein complex that contains either a molybdenum-
CC       iron cofactor, a vanadium-iron cofactor, or an iron-iron cofactor,
CC       that reduces dinitrogen in three succesive two-electron reductions
CC       from nitrogen to diimine to hydrazine to two molecules of ammonia.
CC   -!- The reduction is initiated by formation of hydrogen.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also reduce acetylene to ethylene (but only very
CC       slowly to ethane), azide to nitrogen and ammonia, and cyanide to
CC       methane and ammonia.
CC   -!- In the absence of a suitable substrate, hydrogen is slowly formed.
CC   -!- Some enzymes utilize ferredoxin rather than flavodoxin as the
CC       electron donor (see EC 1.18.6.1).
//
ID   1.20.1.1
DE   phosphite dehydrogenase.
AN   NAD-dependent phosphite dehydrogenase.
CA   phosphite + NAD(+) + H2O = phosphate + NADH + 3 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme has been characterized from the bacterium Pseudomonas
CC       stutzeri WM88.
CC   -!- NADP(+) is a poor substitute for NAD(+).
DR   O69054, PTXD_STUST ;
