ID   2.1.1.326
DE   N-acetyl-demethylphosphinothricin P-methyltransferase.
AN   P-methylase.
CA   N-acetyl-demethyl-L-phosphinothricin + AH2 + 2 S-adenosyl-L-methionine =
CA   N-acetyl-L-phosphinothricin + 5'-deoxyadenosine + L-methionine + A +
CA   S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme was originally characterized from bacteria that produce
CC       the tripeptides bialaphos and phosalacine, which inhibit plant and
CC       bacterial glutamine synthetases.
CC   -!- It is a radical S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAM) enzyme.
CC   -!- According to the proposed mechanism, the reduced iron-sulfur center
CC       donates an electron to SAM, resulting in homolytic cleavage of the
CC       carbon-sulfur bond to form a 5'-deoxyadenosyl radical that abstracts
CC       the hydrogen atom from the P-H bond of the substrate, forming a
CC       phosphinate-centered radical.
CC   -!- This radical reacts with methylcob(III)alamin to produce the
CC       methylated product and cob(II)alamin, which is reduced by an unknown
CC       donor to cob(I)alamin.
CC   -!- A potential route for restoring the latter back to
CC       methylcob(III)alamin is a nucleophilic attack on a second SAM
CC       molecule.
CC   -!- The enzyme acts in vivo on N-acetyl-demethylphosphinothricin-L-
CC       alanyl-L-alanine or N-acetyl-demethylphosphinothricin-L-alanyl-L-
CC       leucine, the intermediates in the biosynthesis of bialaphos and
CC       phosalacine, respectively.
CC   -!- This transformation produces the only example of a carbon-phosphorus-
CC       carbon linkage known to occur in nature.
//
ID   2.1.1.327
DE   phenazine-1-carboxylate N-methyltransferase.
CA   phenazine-1-carboxylate + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = 5-methyl-phenazine-1-
CA   carboxylate + S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Pseudomonas aeruginosa,
CC       is involved in the biosynthesis of pyocyanine, a toxin produced and
CC       secreted by the organism.
CC   -!- The enzyme is active in vitro only in the presence of EC 1.14.13.218.
DR   Q9HWH2, PHZM_PSEAE ;
//
ID   2.1.1.328
DE   N-demethylindolmycin N-methyltransferase.
CA   N-demethylindolmycin + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = indolmycin + S-adenosyl-
CA   L-homocysteine + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Streptomyces griseus,
CC       catalyzes the ultimate reaction in the biosynthesis of indolmycin,
CC       an antibacterial drug that inhibits the bacterial EC 6.1.1.2.
DR   A0A0D4BSP3, IND7_STRGR ;
//
ID   2.1.1.329
DE   demethylphylloquinol methyltransferase.
AN   2-phytyl-1,4-naphthoquinol methyltransferase.
CA   demethylphylloquinol + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = phylloquinol +
CA   S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in plants and cyanobacteria, catalyzes the final
CC       step in the biosynthesis of phylloquinone (vitamin K1), an electron
