ID   2.1.3.7
DE   3-hydroxymethylcephem carbamoyltransferase.
CA   carbamoyl phosphate + a 3-hydroxymethylceph-3-em-4-carboxylate =
CA   phosphate + a 3-carbamoyloxymethylcephem.
CC   -!- Acts on a wide range of 3-hydroxymethylcephems (a subclass of the
CC       cephalosporin antibiotics).
CC   -!- Activated by ATP.
//
ID   2.1.3.8
DE   lysine carbamoyltransferase.
AN   lysine transcarbamylase.
CA   carbamoyl phosphate + L-lysine = L-homocitrulline + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Not identical with EC 2.1.3.3.
//
ID   2.1.3.9
DE   N-acetylornithine carbamoyltransferase.
AN   acetylornithine transcarbamylase.
AN   AOTC.
AN   N-acetylornithine transcarbamylase.
CA   N(2)-acetyl-L-ornithine + carbamoyl phosphate = N(2)-acetyl-L-citrulline
CA   + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Differs from EC 2.1.3.3.
CC   -!- This enzyme replaces EC 2.1.3.3 in the canonic arginine biosynthetic
CC       pathway of several eubacteria and has no catalytic activity with
CC       L-ornithine as substrate.
DR   Q8PK25, AOTC_XANAC ;  Q8P8J2, AOTC_XANCP ;  Q9PEN0, AOTC_XYLFA ;
DR   Q87EL4, AOTC_XYLFT ;
//
ID   2.1.3.10
DE   malonyl-S-ACP:biotin-protein carboxyltransferase.
CA   N(6)-biotinyl-L-lysyl-[protein] + malonyl-[ACP] = N(6)-carboxybiotinyl-L-
CA   lysyl-[protein] + acetyl-[ACP].
CC   -!- Derived from the components MadC and MadD of the anaerobic bacterium
CC       Malonomonas rubra, this enzyme is a component of EC 7.2.4.4.
CC   -!- The carboxy group is transferred from malonate to the cofactor of the
CC       biotin protein (MadF) with retention of configuration.
CC   -!- Similar to EC 4.1.1.87, which forms part of the biotin-independent
CC       EC 4.1.1.88, this enzyme also follows on from EC 2.3.1.187,
CC       and results in the regeneration of the acetyl-[acyl-carrier protein].
DR   O06926, MADC_MALRU ;  O06927, MADD_MALRU ;
//
ID   2.1.3.11
DE   N-succinylornithine carbamoyltransferase.
AN   N-succinyl-L-ornithine transcarbamylase.
AN   SOTCase.
AN   succinylornithine transcarbamylase.
CA   N(2)-succinyl-L-ornithine + carbamoyl phosphate = N(2)-succinyl-L-
CA   citrulline + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Specific for N-succinyl-L-ornithine and cannot use either L-ornithine
CC       (see EC 2.1.3.3) or N-acetyl-L-ornithine (see EC 2.1.3.9) as
CC       substrate.
CC   -!- However, a single amino-acid substitution (Pro90Glu) is sufficient to
CC       switch the enzyme to one that uses N-acetyl-L-ornithine as substrate.
CC   -!- It is essential for de novo arginine biosynthesis in the obligate
CC       anaerobe Bacteroides fragilis, suggesting that this organism uses an
CC       alternative pathway for synthesizing arginine.
DR   E1WKT5, SOTC_BACF6 ;  Q8A1E9, SOTC_BACTN ;
//
ID   2.1.3.12
DE   decarbamoylnovobiocin carbamoyltransferase.
CA   descarbamoylnovobiocin + carbamoyl phosphate = novobiocin + phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the last step in the biosynthesis of the
CC       aminocoumarin antibiotic novobiocin.
CC   -!- The reaction is activated by ATP.
DR   Q9L9F4, NOVN_STRNV ;
//
ID   2.1.3.13
DE   Transferred entry: 6.1.2.2.
//
ID   2.1.3.14
DE   Transferred entry: 6.1.2.2.
//
ID   2.1.3.15
DE   acetyl-CoA carboxytransferase.
CA   N(6)-carboxybiotinyl-L-lysyl-[protein] + acetyl-CoA = N(6)-biotinyl-L-
CA   lysyl-[protein] + malonyl-CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the transfer of a carboxyl group carried on a
CC       biotinylated biotin carboxyl carrier protein (BCCP) to acetyl-CoA,
CC       forming malonyl-CoA.
CC   -!- In some organisms this activity is part of a multi-domain polypeptide
CC       that includes the carrier protein and EC 6.3.4.14 (see EC 6.4.1.2).
CC   -!- Some enzymes can also carboxylate propanonyl-CoA and butanoyl-CoA
CC       (Cf. EC 6.4.1.3).
