//
ID   2.3.1.181
DE   lipoyl(octanoyl) transferase.
AN   lipoate/octanoate transferase.
AN   lipoyl (octanoyl)-[acyl-carrier-protein]-protein N-lipoyltransferase.
AN   lipoyl(octanoyl)transferase.
AN   lipoyltransferase.
AN   octanoyl-[acyl-carrier-protein]-protein N-octanoyltransferase.
CA   octanoyl-[ACP] + L-lysyl-[protein] = N(6)-octanoyl-L-lysyl-[protein] +
CA   holo-[ACP] + H(+).
CC   -!- The first committed step in the biosynthesis of lipoyl cofactor.
CC   -!- The lipoyl cofactor is essential for the function of several key
CC       enzymes involved in oxidative metabolism, as it converts apoprotein
CC       into the biologically active holoprotein.
CC   -!- Examples of such lipoylated proteins include pyruvate dehydrogenase
CC       (E2 domain), 2-oxoglutarate dehydrogenase (E2 domain), the branched-
CC       chain 2-oxoacid dehydrogenases and the glycine cleavage system (H
CC       protein).
CC   -!- Lipoyl-ACP can also act as a substrate although octanoyl-ACP is
CC       likely to be the true substrate.
CC   -!- The other enzyme involved in the biosynthesis of lipoyl cofactor is
CC       EC 2.8.1.8.
CC   -!- An alternative lipoylation pathway involves EC 2.7.7.63, which can
CC       lipoylate apoproteins using exogenous lipoic acid (or its analogs).
