DE   N-terminal L-serine N(alpha)-acetyltransferase NatD.
CA   (1) N-terminal L-seryl-[histone H4] + acetyl-CoA = N-terminal N(alpha)-
CA   acetyl-L-seryl-[histone H4] + CoA + H(+).
CA   (2) N-terminal L-seryl-[histone H2A] + acetyl-CoA = N-terminal N(alpha)-
CA   acetyl-L-seryl-[histone H2A] + CoA + H(+).
CC   -!- N-terminal-acetylases (NATs) catalyze the covalent attachment of an
CC       acetyl moiety from acetyl-CoA to the free alpha-amino group at the
CC       N-terminus of a protein.
CC   -!- This irreversible modification neutralizes the positive charge at the
CC       N-terminus and makes the N-terminal residue larger and more
CC       hydrophobic.
CC   -!- NatD is found in all eukaryotic organisms, and acetylates solely the
CC       serine residue at the N-terminus of histones H2A or H4.
