ID   2.3.1.260
DE   tetracycline polyketide synthase.
CA   malonamoyl-[OxyC ACP] + 8 malonyl-CoA + 8 H(+) = 18-carbamoyl-
CA   3,5,7,9,11,13,15,17-octaoxooctadecanoyl-[OxyC ACP] + 8 CO2 + 8 CoA.
CC   -!- The synthesis, in the bacterium Streptomyces rimosus of the
CC       tetracycline antibiotics core skeleton requires a minimal polyketide
CC       synthase (PKS) consisting of a ketosynthase (KS), a chain length
CC       factor (CLF), and an acyl-carrier protein (ACP).
CC   -!- Initiation involves an amide-containing starter unit that becomes the
CC       C-2 amide that is present in the tetracycline compounds.
CC   -!- Following the initiation, the PKS catalyzes the iterative
CC       condensation of 8 malonyl-CoA molecules to yield the polyketide
CC       backbone of tetracycline.
CC   -!- Throughout the proccess, the nascent chain is attached to the
CC       OxyC acyl-carrier protein.
//
ID   2.3.1.261
DE   (4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate synthase.
CA   (1) 4-hydroxyphenyl-[4-hydroxyphenylalkanoate synthase] + 8 malonyl-CoA +
CA   16 NADPH + 24 H(+) = 17-(4-hydroxyphenyl)heptadecanoyl-
CA   [4-hydroxyphenylalkanoate synthase] + 8 CO2 + 16 NADP(+) + 8 CoA + 8 H2O.
CA   (2) 4-hydroxyphenyl-[4-hydroxyphenylalkanoate synthase] + 9 malonyl-CoA +
CA   18 NADPH + 27 H(+) = 19-(4-hydroxyphenyl)nonadecanoyl-
CA   [4-hydroxyphenylalkanoate synthase] + 9 CO2 + 18 NADP(+) + 9 CoA + 9 H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme is part of the biosynthetic pathway of phenolphthiocerol,
CC       a lipid that serves as a virulence factor of pathogenic mycobacteria.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the elongation of 4-hydroxybenzoate that is loaded on
CC       its acyl-carrier domain to form (4-hydroxyphenyl)alkanoate
CC       intermediates.
CC   -!- The enzyme adds either 8 or 9 malonyl-CoA units, resulting in
CC       formation of 17-(4-hydroxyphenyl)heptadecanoate or
CC       19-(4-hydroxyphenyl)nonadecanoate, respectively.
CC   -!- As the enzyme lacks a thioesterase domain, the product remains loaded
CC       on the acyl-carrier domain at the end of catalysis, and has to be
CC       hydrolyzed by an as-yet unknown mechanism.
//
ID   2.3.1.262
DE   anthraniloyl-CoA anthraniloyltransferase.
CA   anthraniloyl-CoA + malonyl-CoA + H(+) = (2-aminobenzoyl)acetyl-CoA +
CA   CO2 + CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Pseudomonas aeruginosa,
CC       participates in the synthesis of the secondary metabolites 2-heptyl-
CC       3-hydroxy-4(1H)-quinolone and 4-hydroxy-2(1H)-quinolone.
CC   -!- The enzyme transfers an anthraniloyl group from anthraniloyl-CoA to
CC       an internal L-cysteine residue, followed by its transfer to malonyl-
CC       CoA to produce a short-lived product that can cyclize spontaneously
CC       to form 4-hydroxy-2(1H)-quinolone.
CC   -!- However, when EC 3.1.2.32 is present, it removes the CoA moiety from
CC       the product, forming the stable 2-aminobenzoylacetate.
DR   P20582, PQSD_PSEAE ;
//
ID   2.3.1.263
DE   2-amino-4-oxopentanoate thiolase.
AN   2-amino-4-ketopentanoate thiolase.
AN   AKP thiolase.
CA   D-alanine + acetyl-CoA = (2R)-2-amino-4-oxopentanoate + CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Clostridium sticklandii,
CC       is part of a degradation pathway of ornithine.
CC   -!- It is specific for acetyl-CoA and D-alanine.
DR   E3PY98, ORTA_ACESD ;  C1FW06, ORTA_UNKP  ;  E3PY97, ORTB_ACESD ;
DR   C1FW07, ORTB_UNKP  ;
//
ID   2.3.1.264
DE   beta-lysine N(6)-acetyltransferase.
CA   (3S)-3,6-diaminohexanoate + acetyl-CoA = (3S)-6-acetamido-3-
CA   aminohexanoate + CoA + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme is found in some methanogenic archaea and bacteria.
CC   -!- In archaea it is induced under salt stress.
CC   -!- The product, N(6)-acetyl-beta-L-lysine, serves as a compatible
CC       solute, conferring high salt resistance on the producing organisms.
DR   Q8PYC8, ABLB_METMA ;  Q6LYX3, ABLB_METMP ;  O34895, YODP_BACSU ;
//
ID   2.3.1.265
DE   phosphatidylinositol dimannoside acyltransferase.
AN   PIM2 acyltransferase.
CA   (1) a 2,6-O-bis(alpha-D-mannopyranosyl)-1-phosphatidyl-1D-myo-inositol +
CA   an acyl-CoA = a 2-O-(alpha-D-mannosyl)-6-O-(6-O-acyl-alpha-D-mannosyl)-1-
CA   phosphatidyl-1D-myo-inositol + CoA.
CA   (2) a 1,2-diacyl-sn-glycero-3-phospho-[alpha-D-mannopyranosyl-(1<->6)-D-
CA   myo-inositol] + an acyl-CoA = a 1,2-diacyl-sn-glycero-3-phospho-[alpha-D-
CA   6-acyl-mannopyranosyl-(1<->6)-D-myo-inositol] + CoA.
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in Corynebacteriales, is involved in the
CC       biosynthesis of phosphatidyl-myo-inositol mannosides (PIMs).
DR   A0QWG5, ACYLT_MYCS2;  P9WMB4, ACYLT_MYCTO;  P9WMB5, ACYLT_MYCTU;
//
ID   2.3.1.266
DE   [ribosomal protein bS18]-alanine N-acetyltransferase.
CA   N-terminal L-alanyl-[ribosomal protein bS18] + acetyl-CoA = N-terminal
CA   N(alpha)-acetyl-L-alanyl-[ribosomal protein bS18] + CoA + H(+).
