ID   2.3.3.18
DE   2-phosphinomethylmalate synthase.
CA   3-(hydrohydroxyphosphoryl)pyruvate + acetyl-CoA + H2O =
CA   phosphinomethylmalate + CoA + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Streptomyces
CC       hygroscopicus, participates in the pathway for bialaphos
CC       biosynthesis.
CC   -!- It requires a divalent metal ion and can also act on oxaloacetate.
DR   Q9LCB4, PMMS_STRHY ;
//
ID   2.3.3.19
DE   2-phosphonomethylmalate synthase.
AN   2-phosphinomethylmalic acid synthase.
AN   PMM synthase.
CA   3-phosphonopyruvate + acetyl-CoA + H2O = (R)-2-(phosphonomethyl)malate +
CA   CoA + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from several Streptomyces species, participates
CC       in the biosynthesis of certain phosphonate antibiotics.
CC   -!- The enzyme is analogous to EC 2.3.3.1.
DR   Q0ZQ46, FRBC_STRR3 ;
//
ID   2.3.3.20
DE   acyl-CoA:acyl-CoA alkyltransferase.
CA   a 1,2-saturated acyl-CoA + an acyl-CoA + H2O = an (R)-2-alkyl-3-
CA   oxoalkanoate + 2 CoA + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in certain bacterial species, catalyzes a head-
CC       to-head non-decarboxylative Claisen condensation of two acyl-CoA
CC       molecules, resulting in formation of a 2-alkyl-3-oxoalkanoic acid.
CC   -!- It is part of a pathway for the production of olefins.
DR   Q8EG66, OLEA_SHEON ;  Q8PDX2, OLEA_XANCP ;
//
ID   2.3.3.21
DE   (R)-citramalate synthase.
CA   pyruvate + acetyl-CoA + H2O = (3R)-citramalate + CoA + H(+).
CC   -!- One of the enzymes involved in a pyruvate-derived pathway for
CC       isoleucine biosynthesis that is found in some bacterial and archaeal
CC       species.
CC   -!- The enzyme can be inhibited by isoleucine, the end-product of the
CC       pathway, but not by leucine.
CC   -!- The enzyme is highly specific for pyruvate as substrate, as the 2-oxo
CC       acids 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoate, 2-oxobutanoate, 4-methyl-2-
CC       oxopentanoate, 2-oxohexanoate and 2-oxoglutarate cannot act as
CC       substrate.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.3.1.182.
