DE   3-O-beta-D-glucopyranosyl-beta-D-glucuronide phosphorylase.
CA   a 3-O-beta-D-glucosyl-beta-D-glucuronoside + phosphate = a beta-D-
CA   glucuronoside + alpha-D-glucose 1-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Paenibacillus borealis,
CC       catalyzes a reversible reaction, transferring a glucosyl residue
CC       attached by a beta(1,3) linkage to a D-glucuronate residue (either
CC       free or as a part of a beta-D-glucuronide) to a free phosphate,
CC       generating alpha-D-glucopyranose 1-phosphate.
DR   A0A089MMR2, BGBGP_PAEBO;
//
ID   2.4.1.393
DE   MMP alpha-(1->4)-mannosyltransferase.
CA   [3-O-methyl-alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)](n)-3-O-methyl-D-mannose + GDP-alpha-
CA   D-mannose = alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)-[3-O-methyl-alpha-D-mannosyl-
CA   (1->4)](n)-3-O-methyl-D-mannose + GDP + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, present in mycobacterial species that produce a 3-O-
CC       methylmannose polysaccharide (MMP), is involved in recycling and
CC       biosynthesis of the polymer.
CC   -!- The enzyme has the highest activity with 3-O-methylated mannosides
CC       with 4-6 residues.
CC   -!- The residue at the reducing end of the substrate is often
CC       dimethylated, with the second methyl group attached at the O-1
CC       position.
DR   K5BE02, MANT_MYCHD ;
//
ID   2.4.1.394
DE   4,6-alpha-glucanotransferase (linear substrates/linear products).
CA   formation of a linear isomalto/malto-polysaccharide from linear malto-
CA   oligosaccharides.
CC   -!- The enzyme, originally discovered in lactic acid bacteria but later
CC       found in other organisms, is similar to EC 2.4.1.395 yet is not able
CC       to act on sucrose.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which belongs to the glycoside hydrolase 70 (GH70)
CC       family, possesses both hydrolase and transglycosylase activities,
CC       cleaving alpha(1->4) linkages from the non-reducing end of linear
CC       maltooligosaccharides and synthesizing linear alpha(1->6)-glucan
CC       chains.
CC   -!- It also possesses an endo-alpha(1->4)-glycosidase activity.
CC   -!- Due to its narrow binding groove, it is not able to act on branched
CC       substrates. cf. EC 2.4.1.396.
//
ID   2.4.1.395
DE   reuteransucrase.
CA   formation of reuteran from sucrose.
CC   -!- The glucansucrases transfer a D-glucosyl residue from sucrose to a
CC       glucan chain.
CC   -!- They are classified based on the linkage of the transferred glucosyl
CC       residue.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the lactic acid bacterium
CC       Limosilactobacillus reuteri strain 121, catalyzes the hydrolysis of
CC       sucrose and the transfer of the D-glucose moiety to suitable
CC       acceptors (inclduing sucrose), forming the glucan reuteran, which is
CC       typical for these strains.
CC   -!- The enzyme forms mostly alpha(1->4) glucosidic linkages, but also
CC       alpha(1->6) linkages.
CC   -!- The presence of maltose significantly accelerate the initial rate of
CC       the reaction. See EC 2.4.1.5.
//
ID   2.4.1.396
DE   4,6-alpha-glucanotransferase (linear and branched substrates, branched
DE   products).
CA   formation of a branched isomalto/malto-polysaccharide from branched
CA   malto-oligosaccharides.
CC   -!- The enzyme, discovered in several bacterial species, is similar to
CC       EC 2.4.1.395 yet is not able to act on sucrose.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which belongs to the glycoside hydrolase 70 (GH70)
CC       family, possesses both hydrolase and transglycosylase activities,
CC       cleaving endo alpha(1->4) linkages from the non-reducing end of
CC       maltooligosaccharides and adding the resulting oligosaccharides to
CC       the non-reducing end of alpha-D-glucan chains that terminate with a
CC       residue linked by an alpha-(1->4) linkage, forming an alpha(1->6)
CC       linkage.
CC   -!- The enzyme is not able to form successive alpha(1->6) linkages.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 2.4.1.394, which can only act on linear substrates, this
CC       enzyme is able to act on both linear and branched substrates, and can
CC       form the branched reuteran type of alpha-glucan.
//
ID   2.4.1.397
DE   cyclic beta-1,2-glucan glucanotransferase.
CA   Cyclizes part of a (1->2)-beta-D-glucan chain by formation of a (1->2)-
CA   beta-D-glucosidic bond.
CC   -!- This enzyme is the cyclization domain of cyclic beta-1,2-glucan
CC       synthase.
CC   -!- Enzymes from Brucella abortus and Thermoanaerobacter italicus were
CC       characterized.
CC   -!- The cyclization domain of cyclic beta-1,2-glucan synthase is flanked
CC       by an N-terminal beta-1,2-glucosyltransferase domain (cf.
CC       EC 2.4.1.391) and a C-terminal beta-1,2-glucoside phosphorylase
CC       domain (cf. EC 2.4.1.333), with the former responsible for elongation
CC       and the latter for chain length control.
CC   -!- The cyclization domain of T. italicus cyclizes linear
CC       oligosaccharides with a degree of polymerization (DP) of 21 or higher
CC       to produce cyclic glucans with DP 17 or higher.
CC   -!- The cyclization domain also disproportionates linear beta-1,2-
CC       glucooligosaccharides without cycling.
CC   -!- The entire cyclic beta-1,2-glucan synthase from B. abortus
CC       synthesizes cyclic beta-1,2-glucans with DP 17-22.
//
ID   2.4.1.398
DE   beta-galactofuranoside beta-1,5-galactofuranosyltransferase.
CA   a beta-D-galactofuranoside + UDP-alpha-D-galactofuranose = a beta-D-Galf-
CA   (1->5)-beta-D-galactofuranoside + UDP + H(+).
CC   -!- Requires Mn(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the flilamentous fungi Aspergillus
CC       nidulans and Aspergillus fumigatus, participates in the synthesis of
CC       fungal-type galactomannan and O-mannose-type galactomannan, which are
CC       embedded in the fungal cell walls.
CC   -!- While the reaction shown here describes the transfer of one
CC       galactofuransyl residue, the enzyme can add up to 5 more
CC       galactofuranosyl residues.
//
ID   2.4.1.399
DE   phenyllactate glucosyltransferase.
CA   (R)-3-phenyllactate + UDP-alpha-D-glucose = (R)-3-phenyllactyl-beta-D-
CA   glucose + UDP.
CC   -!- Isolated from the plant Atropa belladonna (deadly nightshade).
CC   -!- Involved in the biosynthesis of littorine.
//
ID   2.4.1.400
DE   littorine synthase.
CA   (R)-3-phenyllactyl-beta-D-glucose + tropine = (R)-littorine + D-glucose.
CC   -!- Isolated from the plant Atropa belladonna (deadly nightshade).
CC   -!- Involved in the biosynthesis of the alkaloid L-hyoscyamine
CC       (S-atropine).
//
ID   2.4.1.n1
DE   Transferred entry: 2.4.1.245.
//
ID   2.4.1.n2
DE   loliose synthase.
CA   sucrose + UDP-alpha-D-galactose = loliose + UDP + H(+).
//
ID   2.4.1.n3
DE   Transferred entry: 2.4.1.250.
//
ID   2.4.1.n4
DE   Transferred entry: 2.4.1.131.
//
ID   2.4.1.n5
DE   Transferred entry: 2.4.99.16.
//
ID   2.4.2.1
DE   purine-nucleoside phosphorylase.
AN   inosine phosphorylase.
AN   PNPase.
CA   (1) a purine D-ribonucleoside + phosphate = a purine nucleobase + alpha-
CA   D-ribose 1-phosphate.
CA   (2) a purine 2'-deoxy-D-ribonucleoside + phosphate = a purine nucleobase
CA   + 2-deoxy-alpha-D-ribose 1-phosphate.
CC   -!- Specificity not completely determined.
CC   -!- Can also catalyze ribosyltransferase reactions of the type catalyzed
CC       by EC 2.4.2.5.
