ID   2.5.1.2
DE   thiamine pyridinylase.
AN   pyrimidine transferase.
AN   thiaminase.
AN   thiaminase I.
AN   thiamine:base 2-methyl-4-aminopyrimidine-5-methenyltransferase.
AN   thiamine hydrolase.
AN   thiamine pyridinolase.
CA   pyridine + thiamine = heteropyrithiamine + 5-(2-hydroxyethyl)-4-
CA   methylthiazole.
CC   -!- Various bases and thiol compounds can act instead of pyridine.
DR   P45741, THI1_PANTH ;
//
ID   2.5.1.3
DE   thiamine phosphate synthase.
AN   thiamine-phosphate diphosphorylase.
AN   thiamine-phosphate pyrophosphorylase.
AN   thiamine-phosphate synthase.
AN   TMP diphosphorylase.
AN   TMP pyrophosphorylase.
CA   (1) 2-[(2R,5Z)-2-carboxy-4-methylthiazol-5(2H)-ylidene]ethyl phosphate +
CA   4-amino-2-methyl-5-(diphosphooxymethyl)pyrimidine + 2 H(+) = thiamine
CA   phosphate + CO2 + diphosphate.
CA   (2) 2-(2-carboxy-4-methylthiazol-5-yl)ethyl phosphate + 4-amino-2-methyl-
CA   5-(diphosphooxymethyl)pyrimidine + 2 H(+) = thiamine phosphate + CO2 +
CA   diphosphate.
CA   (3) 4-methyl-5-(2-phosphooxyethyl)-thiazole + 4-amino-2-methyl-5-
CA   (diphosphooxymethyl)pyrimidine + H(+) = thiamine phosphate + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the penultimate reaction in thiamine de novo
CC       biosynthesis, condensing the pyrimidine and thiazole components.
CC   -!- The enzyme is thought to accept the product of EC 2.8.1.10 as its
CC       substrate.
CC   -!- However, it has been shown that in some bacteria, such as Bacillus
CC       subtilis, an additional enzyme, EC 5.3.99.10 converts that compound
CC       into its tautomer 2-(2-carboxy-4-methylthiazol-5-yl)ethyl phosphate,
CC       and that it is the latter that serves as the substrate for the
CC       synthase.
CC   -!- In addition to this activity, the enzyme participates in a salvage
CC       pathway, acting on 4-methyl-5-(2-phosphono-oxyethyl)thiazole, which
CC       is produced from thiamine degradation products.
CC   -!- In yeast this activity is found in a bifunctional enzyme and in the
CC       plant Arabidopsis thaliana the activity is part of a trifunctional
CC       enzyme.
