ID   2.5.1.148
DE   lycopaoctaene synthase.
CA   2 (2E,6E,10E)-geranylgeranyl diphosphate + NADPH + H(+) = all-trans-
CA   lycopaoctaene + 2 diphosphate + NADP(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the green microalga Botryococcus
CC       braunii race L, in involved in biosynthesis of (14E,18E)-lycopadiene.
CC   -!- In vitro, the enzyme can accept (2E,6E)-farnesyl diphosphate and
CC       phytyl diphosphate as substrates, and is also able to catalyze the
CC       condensation of two different substrate molecules, forming chimeric
CC       products.
CC   -!- However, the use of these alternative substrates is not significant
CC       in vivo.
DR   A0A142ZC57, LOS_BOTBR  ;
//
ID   2.5.1.149
DE   lycopene elongase/hydratase (flavuxanthin-forming).
CA   (1) all-trans-lycopene + dimethylallyl diphosphate + A + H2O =
CA   nonaflavuxanthin + AH2 + diphosphate.
CA   (2) nonaflavuxanthin + dimethylallyl diphosphate + A + H2O = flavuxanthin
CA   + AH2 + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Corynebacterium
CC       glutamicum, is bifunctional.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the elongation of the C40 carotenoid all-trans-lycopene
CC       by attaching an isoprene unit at C-2, as well as the hydroxylation of
CC       the new isoprene unit.
CC   -!- The enzyme acts at both ends of the substrate, forming the C50
CC       carotenoid flavuxanthin via the C45 intermediate nonaflavuxanthin.
CC       cf. EC 2.5.1.150.
DR   Q8NSP8, CRTEB_CORGL;  Q93QX2, CRTEB_CORGT;
//
ID   2.5.1.150
DE   lycopene elongase/hydratase (dihydrobisanhydrobacterioruberin-forming).
CA   (1) all-trans-lycopene + dimethylallyl diphosphate + H2O =
CA   dihydroisopentenyldehydrorhodopin + diphosphate.
CA   (2) isopentenyldehydrorhodopin + dimethylallyl diphosphate + H2O =
CA   dihydrobisanhydrobacterioruberin + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Dietzia sp. CQ4 and the
CC       halophilic archaea Halobacterium salinarum and Haloarcula japonica,
CC       is bifunctional.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the elongation of the C40 carotenoid all-trans-lycopene
CC       by attaching an isoprene unit at C-2 as well as the hydroxylation of
CC       the previous end of the molecule.
CC   -!- The enzyme acts at both ends of the substrate, and combined with the
CC       action of EC 1.3.99.37, it forms the C50 carotenoid
CC       dihydrobisanhydrobacterioruberin. cf. EC 2.5.1.149.
DR   A1XI30, LBTBC_DIESC;  M0L7V9, LYEL_HALJT ;  Q9HPD9, LYEL_HALSA ;
DR   D4GTV9, LYEL_HALVD ;
//
ID   2.5.1.151
DE   alkylcobalamin dealkylase.
AN   alkylcobalamin reductase.
CA   (1) apo-[alkylcobalamin reductase] + methylcob(III)alamin + glutathione =
CA   S-methyl glutathione + cob(I)alamin-[alkylcobalamin reductase] + H(+).
CA   (2) apo-[alkylcobalamin reductase] + adenosylcob(III)alamin + glutathione
CA   = S-adenosylglutathione + cob(I)alamin-[alkylcobalamin reductase] + H(+).
CC   -!- This mammalian enzyme, which is cytosolic, can bind internalized
CC       alkylcobalamins and process them to cob(I)alamin using the thiolate
CC       of glutathione for nucleophilic displacement.
CC   -!- The product remains bound to the protein, and, following its
CC       oxidation to cob(II)alamin, is transferred by the enzyme, together
CC       with its interacting partner MMADHC, directly to downstream enzymes
CC       involved in adenosylcobalamin and methylcobalamin biosynthesis.
CC   -!- In addition to its dealkylase function, the enzyme also catalyze an
CC       entirely different decyanase reaction with cyanocobalamin (cf.
CC       EC 1.16.1.6).
DR   Q5E9C8, MMAC_BOVIN ;  Q5ZL21, MMAC_CHICK ;  Q5RFU5, MMAC_DANRE ;
DR   Q9Y4U1, MMAC_HUMAN ;  Q9CZD0, MMAC_MOUSE ;
//
ID   2.5.1.152
DE   D-histidine 2-aminobutanoyltransferase.
CA   D-histidine + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = (2S)-2-amino-4-{[(1R)-1-carboxy-
CA   2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethyl]amino}butanoate + S-methyl-5'-thioadenosine +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Staphylococcus aureus,
CC       participates in the biosynthesis of the metallophore staphylopine,
CC       which is involved in the acquisition of nickel, copper, and cobalt.
DR   A0A0H3JXA8, NASLD_STAAM;
//
ID   2.5.1.153
DE   adenosine tuberculosinyltransferase.
CA   tuberculosinyl diphosphate + adenosine + H(+) =
CA   1-tuberculosinyladenosine + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterial pathogen Mycobacterium
CC       tuberculosis, produces 1-tuberculosinyladenosine, an unusual terpene
CC       nucleoside that acts as a phagolysosome disruptor by neutralizing the
CC       pH, resulting in swelling of the lysosome and obliteration of its
CC       multilamellar structure.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.1.7.8.
DR   P9WJ60, TUBAT_MYCTO;  P9WJ61, TUBAT_MYCTU;
//
ID   2.5.1.154
DE   corrinoid adenosyltransferase EutT.
CA   2 cob(II)alamin + reduced [electron-transfer flavoprotein] + 2 ATP +
CA   2 H2O = 2 adenosylcob(III)alamin + oxidized [electron-transfer
CA   flavoprotein] + 2 phosphate + 2 diphosphate + 3 H(+).
CC   -!- The corrinoid adenosylation pathway comprises three steps.
CC   -!- (i) reduction of Co(III) within the corrinoid to Co(II) by a one-
CC       electron transfer. This can occur non-enzymically in the presence of
CC       dihydroflavin nucleotides or reduced flavoproteins.
CC   -!- (ii) Co(II) is bound by corrinoid adenosyltransferase, resulting in
CC       displacement of the lower axial ligand by an aromatic residue. The
CC       reduction potential of the 4-coordinate Co(II) intermediate is raised
CC       by ~250 mV compared with the free compound, bringing it to within
CC       physiological range.
CC   -!- This is followed by a second single-electron transfer from either
CC       free dihydroflavins or the reduced flavin cofactor of flavoproteins,
CC       resulting in reduction to Co(I).
CC   -!- (iii) the Co(I) conducts a nucleophilic attack on the adenosyl moiety
CC       of ATP, resulting in transfer of the deoxyadenosyl group and
CC       oxidation of the cobalt atom to Co(III) state.
CC   -!- Three types of corrinoid adenosyltransferases, not related by
CC       sequence, have been described. In the anaerobic bacterium Salmonella
CC       enterica they are encoded by the cobA gene (a housekeeping enzyme
CC       involved in both the de novo biosynthesis and the salvage of
CC       adenosylcobalamin), the pduO gene (involved in (S)-propane-1,2-diol
CC       utilization), and the eutT gene (involved in ethanolamine
CC       utilization).
CC   -!- The first two types, which produce triphosphate, are classified as
CC       EC 2.5.1.17, while the EutT type hydrolyzes triphosphate to
CC       diphosphate and phosphate during catalysis and is thus classified
CC       separately.
DR   P65644, EUTT_ECOL6 ;  P65643, EUTT_ECOLI ;  Q9ZFV4, EUTT_SALTY ;
//
ID   2.5.1.155
DE   phosphoglycerol geranylfarnesyltransferase.
AN   GFGP synthase.
CA   sn-glycerol 1-phosphate + (2E,6E,10E,14E)-geranylfarnesyl diphosphate =
CA   sn-3-(O-farnesylgeranyl)glycerol 1-phosphate + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the archaeon Aeropyrum pernix,
CC       catalyzes the first pathway-specific step in the biosynthesis of the
CC       core membrane C25,C25-diether lipids in some archaea.
CC   -!- It does not act on geranylgeranyl diphosphate. cf. EC 2.5.1.41.
//
ID   2.5.1.156
DE   geranylfarnesylglycerol-phosphate geranylfarnesyltransferase.
AN   2,3-bis-O-(farnesylgeranyl)-sn-glycerol 1-phosphate synthase.
AN   2,3-di-O-farnesylgeranylglyceryl synthase.
AN   DGFGP synthase.
CA   sn-3-(O-farnesylgeranyl)glycerol 1-phosphate + (2E,6E,10E,14E)-
CA   geranylfarnesyl diphosphate = 2,3-bis-O-(geranylfarnesyl)-sn-glycerol
CA   1-phosphate + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the archaeon Aeropyrum pernix, carries
CC       out the second prenyltransfer reaction involved in the formation of
CC       C25,C25 membrane diether-lipids in some archaea.
CC   -!- Requires a divalent metal cation, such as Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme cannot accept sn-3-(O-geranylgeranyl)glycerol 1-phosphate
CC       as the prenyl donor. cf. EC 2.5.1.42.
//
ID   2.5.1.157
DE   rRNA small subunit aminocarboxypropyltransferase.
CA   an N(1)-methylpseudouridine in rRNA + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = N(1)-
CA   methyl-N(3)-[(3S)-3-amino-3-carboxypropyl]pseudouridine in rRNA +
CA   S-methyl-5'-thioadenosine + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in all eukaryotes and some archaea, catalyzes the
CC       final step in production of the modified rRNA nucleotide N(1)-methyl-
CC       N(3)-[(3S)-aminocarboxypropyl]-pseudouridine (m(1)acp(3)psi).
CC   -!- This modified nucleotide is present in the small subunit of ribosomal
CC       RNA (18S in eukaryotes and 16S in archaea). cf. EC 2.5.1.114 and
CC       EC 2.5.1.108.
