ID   2.7.7.100
DE   SAMP-activating enzyme.
AN   SAMP-activating enzyme E1.
CA   [small archaeal modifier protein]-C-terminal Gly-Gly + ATP + H(+) =
CA   [small archaeal modifier protein]-C-terminal Gly-Gly-AMP + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the activation of SAMPs (Small Archaeal Modifier
CC       Proteins), which are ubiquitin-like proteins found only in the
CC       Archaea, by catalyzing the ATP-dependent formation of a SAMP
CC       adenylate in which the C-terminal glycine of SAMP is bound to AMP via
CC       an acyl-phosphate linkage.
CC   -!- The product of this activity can accept a sulfur atom to form a
CC       thiocarboxylate moiety that acts as a sulfur carrier involved in
CC       thiolation of tRNA and other metabolites such as molybdopterin.
CC   -!- Alternatively, the enzyme can also catalyze the transfer of SAMP from
CC       its activated form to an internal cysteine residue, leading to a
CC       process termed SAMPylation (see EC 6.2.1.55).
//
