//
ID   2.7.10.3
DE   bacterial tyrosine kinase.
AN   bacterial protein tyrosine kinase.
AN   BY-kinase.
CA   L-tyrosyl-[protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-tyrosyl-[protein] + ADP + H(+).
CC   -!- This family of enzymes includes most of the bacterial tyrosine
CC       kinases.
CC   -!- These enzymes do not share sequence or structural homology with
CC       eukaryotic tyrosine kinases, and exploit ATP/GTP-binding Walker
CC       motifs to catalyze autophosphorylation and substrate phosphorylation
CC       on tyrosine.
CC   -!- Two subfamilies have been defined: P-type enzymes contain an
CC       N-terminal transmembrane portion and an extracellular hairpin loop
CC       domain. The intracellular portion comprises the catalytic domain and
CC       a tyrosine-rich C-terminal domain that contains the site for
CC       autophosphorylation.
CC   -!- In F-type enzymes the extracellular transmembrane domain and the
CC       intracellular catalytic domain are two independent proteins encoded
CC       by two separate genes.
CC   -!- The majority of characterized bacterial tyrosine kinases regulate the
CC       production and export of capsular and extracellular polysaccharides,
CC       but other members are involved in many other functions.
//
ID   2.7.10.4
DE   [Src-family] C-terminal protein kinase.
AN   C-terminal Src kinase.
CA   ATP + [a protein]-(L-tyrosine) = ADP + [a protein]-(L-tyrosine)-
CA   phosphate. The optimal peptide substrate contains the sequence (relative
CA   to the phosphorylated tyrosine): Glu-Glu-Asp/Glu-Ile-Tyr-Phe-Phe-Phe-Phe.
CA   Physiological substrates are C-terminal tail of Src-family protein
CA   kinases.
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- Specifically phosphorylates a C-terminal tyrosine in Src family of
CC       non-receptor protein kinases (Tyr 527 chicken c-Src numbering,
CC       Tyr 530 human c-Src numbering).
CC   -!- The enzyme engages in particular binding mode with its substrate
CC       proteins to present the C-terminal sequence in the active site. A
CC       shortened activation loop disrupts the conventional substrate peptide
CC       docking observed in most tyrosine kinases.
CC   -!- It can be activated by docking of the SH2 and linking regions to the
CC       kinase domain. Removal of the SH3/SH2 domain decreases kinase
CC       activity by 100-fold.
CC   -!- Phosphorylation of Ser(364) by EC 2.7.11.1 activates the enzyme in
CC       the presence of the Csk SH3 domain.
CC   -!- The SH3 domain lacks a tyrosine for autophosphorylation in its
CC       activation loop and requires a specific binding mode to its substrate
CC       proteins for catalysis.
//
ID   2.7.11.1
DE   non-specific serine/threonine protein kinase.
AN   protein phosphokinase.
AN   protein serine kinase.
AN   protein-serine kinase.
AN   protein serine-threonine kinase.
AN   serine/threonine protein kinase.
AN   serine(threonine) protein kinase.
AN   serine kinase.
AN   serine protein kinase.
AN   serine-specific protein kinase.
AN   threonine-specific protein kinase.
CA   (1) L-seryl-[protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-seryl-[protein] + ADP + H(+).
CA   (2) L-threonyl-[protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-threonyl-[protein] + ADP +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- This is a heterogeneous group of serine/threonine protein kinases
CC       that do not have an activating compound and are either non-specific
CC       or their specificity has not been analyzed to date.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.7.1.37 and EC 2.7.1.70.
