ID   2.7.11.8
DE   Fas-activated serine/threonine kinase.
AN   FAST.
AN   FASTK.
CA   (1) L-seryl-[Fas-activated protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-seryl-[Fas-
CA   activated protein] + ADP + H(+).
CA   (2) L-threonyl-[Fas-activated protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-threonyl-[Fas-
CA   activated protein] + ADP + H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme is activated during Fas-mediated apoptosis.
CC   -!- Following Fas ligation, the enzyme, which is constitutively
CC       phosphorylated, is dephosphorylated, and it is the dephosphorylated
CC       form that causes phosphorylation of TIA-1, a nuclear RNA-binding
CC       protein.
CC   -!- Phosphorylation of TIA-1 precedes the onset of DNA fragmentation.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.7.1.37.
DR   Q14296, FASTK_HUMAN;  Q9JIX9, FASTK_MOUSE;
//
ID   2.7.11.9
DE   [Goodpasture-antigen-binding protein] kinase.
AN   Goodpasture antigen-binding protein kinase.
AN   GPBPK.
AN   GPBP kinase.
CA   (1) L-seryl-[Goodpasture antigen-binding protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-
CA   seryl-[Goodpasture antigen-binding protein] + ADP + H(+).
CA   (2) L-threonyl-[Goodpasture antigen-binding protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-
CA   threonyl-[Goodpasture antigen-binding protein] + ADP + H(+).
CC   -!- This serine/threonine kinase specifically binds to and phosphorylates
CC       the N-terminal region of the human Goodpasture antigen, which is
CC       located on the alpha3 chain of collagen IV and is involved in
CC       autoimmune disease.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.7.1.37.
//
ID   2.7.11.10
DE   IkappaB kinase.
AN   CHUK.
AN   IKBKA.
AN   IKBKB.
AN   IKK.
AN   IKK-1.
AN   IKK-2.
AN   inhibitor of NFkappaB kinase.
AN   TANK-binding kinase 1.
AN   TBK1.
CA   L-seryl-[I-kappa-B protein] + ATP = O-phospho-L-seryl-[I-kappa-B protein]
CA   + ADP + H(+).
CC   -!- Phosphorylates IkappaB proteins at specific serine residues, which
CC       marks them for destruction via the ubiquitination pathway.
CC   -!- Subsequent degradation of the IkappaB complex (IKK) activates
CC       NF-kappaB, a translation factor that plays an important role in
CC       inflammation, immunity, cell proliferation and apoptosis.
CC   -!- If the serine residues are replaced by threonine residues,
CC       the activity of the enzyme is decreased considerably.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.7.1.37.
DR   Q95KV1, IKKA_BOVIN ;  Q5ZJB4, IKKA_CHICK ;  Q4G3H4, IKKA_DANRE ;
