ID   2.8.1.2
DE   3-mercaptopyruvate sulfurtransferase.
CA   2-oxo-3-sulfanylpropanoate + [thioredoxin]-dithiol = [thioredoxin]-
CA   disulfide + hydrogen sulfide + pyruvate + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes a transsulfuration reaction from
CC       3-mercaptopyruvate to an internal cysteine residue.
CC   -!- In the presence of a dithiol such as reduced thioredoxin or
CC       dihydrolipoate, the sulfanyl sulfur is released as hydrogen sulfide.
CC   -!- The enzyme participates in a sulfur relay process that leads to the
CC       2-thiolation of some tRNAs and to protein urmylation by transferring
CC       sulfur between the NFS1 cysteine desulfurase (EC 2.8.1.7) and the
CC       MOCS3 sulfurtransferase (EC 2.8.1.11).
DR   O64530, STR1_ARATH ;  Q24JL3, STR2_ARATH ;  P58388, THTM_ECO57 ;
DR   P31142, THTM_ECOLI ;  P25325, THTM_HUMAN ;  Q99J99, THTM_MOUSE ;
DR   Q9I452, THTM_PSEAE ;  P97532, THTM_RAT   ;  Q9USJ1, THTM_SCHPO ;
DR   Q08686, THTR_YEAST ;
//
ID   2.8.1.3
DE   thiosulfate--thiol sulfurtransferase.
AN   glutathione-dependent thiosulfate reductase.
AN   sulfane reductase.
CA   thiosulfate + 2 glutathione = glutathione disulfide + hydrogen sulfide +
CA   sulfite + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The primary product is glutathione hydrodisulfide, which reacts with
CC       glutathione to give glutathione disulfide and sulfide.
CC   -!- L-Cysteine can also act as acceptor.
DR   Q8NFU3, TSTD1_HUMAN;
//
ID   2.8.1.4
DE   tRNA uracil 4-sulfurtransferase.
AN   tRNA sulfurtransferase.
CA   [ThiI sulfur-carrier protein]-S-sulfanyl-L-cysteine + a uridine in tRNA +
CA   2 reduced [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + ATP + H(+) = [ThiI sulfur-carrier
CA   protein]-L-cysteine + a 4-thiouridine in tRNA + 2 oxidized [2Fe-2S]-
CA   [ferredoxin] + AMP + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in bacteria and archaea, is activated by
CC       EC 2.8.1.7, which transfers a sulfur atom to an internal L-cysteine
CC       residue, forming a cysteine persulfide.
CC   -!- The activated enzyme then transfers the sulfur to a uridine in a tRNA
CC       chain in a reaction that requires ATP.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Escherichia coli forms 4-thiouridine
CC       only at position 8 of tRNA.
CC   -!- The enzyme also participates in the biosynthesis of the thiazole
CC       moiety of thiamine, but different domains are involved in the two
CC       processes.
