ID   3.1.1.82
DE   pheophorbidase.
AN   phedase.
AN   PPD.
CA   pheophorbide a + H2O + H(+) = pyropheophorbide a + methanol + CO2.
CC   -!- Forms part of the chlorophyll degradation pathway, and is found in
CC       higher plants and in algae.
CC   -!- In higher plants it participates in de-greening processes such as
CC       fruit ripening, leaf senescence, and flowering.
CC   -!- Exists in two forms: type 1 is induced by senescence whereas type 2
CC       is constitutively expressed.
CC   -!- Highly specific for pheophorbide as substrate (with a preference for
CC       pheophorbide a over pheophorbide b) as other chlorophyll derivatives
CC       such as protochlorophyllide a, pheophytin a and c, chlorophyll a and
CC       b, and chlorophyllide a cannot act as substrates.
CC   -!- Another enzyme, called pheophorbide demethoxycarbonylase (PDC),
CC       produces pyropheophorbide a from pheophorbide a without forming an
CC       intermediate although the precise reaction is not yet known.
DR   Q2V0W1, PPD_RAPSA  ;
//
ID   3.1.1.83
DE   monoterpene epsilon-lactone hydrolase.
AN   MLH.
CA   (1) 4-isopropenyl-7-methyloxepan-2-one + H2O = 6-hydroxy-3-
CA   isopropenylheptanoate + H(+).
CA   (2) 7-isopropyl-4-methyloxepan-2-one + H2O = 6-hydroxy-3,7-
CA   dimethyloctanoate + H(+).
CC   -!- Catalyzes the ring opening of epsilon-lactones which are formed
CC       during degradation of dihydrocarveol by the Gram-positive bacterium
CC       Rhodococcus erythropolis DCL14.
CC   -!- Also acts on ethyl caproate, indicating that it is an esterase with a
CC       preference for lactones (internal cyclic esters).
CC   -!- Not stereoselective.
DR   Q9EX73, MLHB_RHOER ;
