ID   3.1.1.120
DE   L-fucono-1,5-lactonase.
CA   L-fucono-1,5-lactone + H2O = L-fuconate + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Burkholderia
CC       multivorans, participates in an L-fucose degradation pathway.
CC   -!- The enzyme exhibits catalytic activity for the hydrolysis of several
CC       lactones, including L-fucono-1,4-lactone, D-arabinono-1,4-lactone,
CC       L-xylono-1,4-lactone, and L-galactono-1,4-lactone, but L-fucono-1,5-
CC       lactone is the best substrate.
DR   Q0BGE1, FCNLN_BURCM;  A0A0H3KNC4, FCNLN_BURM1;  P0DX22, FCNLN_PSEA6;
//
ID   3.1.1.121
DE   ergosteryl-3beta-O-L-aspartate hydrolase.
CA   1-(ergostan-3beta-yl)-L-aspartate + H2O = ergosterol + L-aspartate +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme has been detected in fungal species that belong to the
CC       Ascomycota and Basidiomycota phyla, and has been characterized from
CC       the fungus Aspergillus fumigatus.
//
ID   3.1.1.122
DE   carbendazim hydrolysing esterase.
CA   carbendazim + H2O = 2-aminobenzimidazole + methanol + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which is inducible in the soil bacterium Nocardioides sp.
CC       (strain SG-4G), catalyzes the degradation of the fungicide
CC       carbendazim.
CC   -!- Following hydrolysis of the carbamate ester, the carbamate
CC       decarboxylates spontaneously.
DR   C8CP46, MHEI_NOCS4 ;
//
ID   3.1.1.123
DE   dehydropreakuammicine esterase.
AN   norfluorocurarine synthase.
CA   17-dehydropreakuammicine + H2O = norfluorocurarine + methanol + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the tree Strychnos nux-vomica,
CC       participates in the biosynthesis of strychnine.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the hydrolysis of a methyl ester, which is
CC       followed by the spontaneous decarboxylation of the resulting carboxyl
CC       group.
DR   P0DO80, NS1_STRNX  ;  P0DO89, NS1_STRYX  ;  P0DO81, NS2_STRNX  ;
DR   P0DO90, NS2_STRYX  ;
//
ID   3.1.1.124
DE   ellagitannin acyl hydrolase.
AN   ellagitannase.
AN   ellagitannin acyl esterase.
CA   punicalagin = punicalin + ellagic acid.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which has been isolated from the fungus Aspergillus
CC       niger, catalyzes a step in the degradation of ellagitannins such as
CC       punicalagin, which are phenolic compounds occurring in pomegranates
CC       and some tropical trees.
CC   -!- It hydrolyzes ester bonds between the D-glucose core and the
CC       hexahydroxydiphenic acid groups of ellagitannins, releasing
CC       hexahydroxydiphenic acid that undergoes spontaneous lactonization to
CC       form ellagic acid.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 3.1.1.20, it does not act on gallotannins.
CC   -!- More recent studies suggest that the activity may be catalyzed by
CC       EC 3.1.4.3.
//
ID   3.1.1.n1
DE   Transferred entry: 3.5.1.103.
