ID   3.1.6.20
DE   S-sulfosulfanyl-L-cysteine sulfohydrolase.
CA   (1) S-sulfosulfanyl-L-cysteinyl-[SoxY protein] + H2O = S-sulfanyl-L-
CA   cysteinyl-[SoxY protein] + sulfate + H(+).
CA   (2) S-(2-sulfodisulfanyl)-L-cysteinyl-[SoxY protein] + H2O =
CA   S-disulfanyl-L-cysteinyl-[SoxY protein] + sulfate + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme is part of the Sox enzyme system, which participates in a
CC       bacterial thiosulfate oxidation pathway that produces sulfate.
CC   -!- It catalyzes two reactions in the pathway.
CC   -!- In both cases the enzyme hydrolyzes a sulfonate moiety that is bound
CC       (either directly or via a sulfane) to a cysteine residue of a SoxY
CC       protein, releasing sulfate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Paracoccus pantotrophus contains a pyroglutamate
CC       (cycloglutamate) at its N-terminus.
//
ID   3.1.6.21
DE   linear primary-alkylsulfatase.
AN   type III linear primary-alkylsulfatase.
CA   a primary linear alkyl sulfate ester + H2O = a primary alcohol + sulfate
CA   + H(+).
CC   -!- Sulfatase enzymes are classified as type I, in which the key
CC       catalytic residue is 3-oxo-L-alanine, type II, which are non-heme
CC       iron-dependent dioxygenases, or type III, whose catalytic domain
CC       adopts a metallo-beta-lactamase fold and binds two zinc ions as
CC       cofactors.
CC   -!- This enzyme belongs to the type III sulfatase family.
CC   -!- The enzyme is active against linear primary-alkyl sulfate esters,
CC       such as dodecyl sulfate, decyl sulfate, octyl sulfate, and hexyl
CC       sulfate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Pseudomonas aeruginosa is secreted out of the cell.
CC   -!- The catalytic mechanism begins with activation of a water molecule by
CC       the binuclear Zn(2+) cluster, resulting in a nucleophilic attack on
CC       the carbon atom. cf. EC 3.1.6.19 and EC 3.1.6.22.
DR   P32717, LPAKS_ECOLI;  Q9I5I9, LPAKS_PSEAE;  Q52556, LPAKS_PSES9;
DR   F2WP51, LPAKS_PSESP;
//
ID   3.1.6.22
DE   branched primary-alkylsulfatase.
AN   type III branched primary-alkylsulfatase.
CA   2-butyloctyl sulfate + H2O = 2-butyloctan-1-ol + sulfate + H(+).
CC   -!- Sulfatase enzymes are classified as type I, in which the key
CC       catalytic residue is 3-oxo-L-alanine, type II, which are non-heme
CC       iron-dependent dioxygenases, or type III, whose catalytic domain
CC       adopts a metallo-beta-lactamase fold and binds two zinc ions as
CC       cofactors.
CC   -!- This enzyme belongs to the type III family.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from a Pseudomonas strain, is specific for
CC       branched primary-alkyl sulfate esters and does not act on linear
CC       substrates such as dodecyl sulfate. cf. EC 1.14.11.77, EC 3.1.6.1,
CC       EC 3.1.6.19, and EC 3.1.6.21.
//
ID   3.1.7.1
DE   prenyl-diphosphatase.
AN   prenyl-pyrophosphatase.
CA   dimethylallyl diphosphate + H2O = prenol + diphosphate.
CC   -!- Farnesyl diphosphate is the best substrate so far tested.
//
