ID   3.2.1.156
DE   oligosaccharide reducing-end xylanase.
AN   reducing end xylose-releasing exo-oligoxylanase.
AN   Rex.
CA   Hydrolysis of (1->4)-beta-D-xylose residues from the reducing end of
CA   oligosaccharides.
CC   -!- The enzyme, originally isolated from the bacterium Bacillus
CC       halodurans C-125, releases the xylose unit at the reducing end of
CC       oligosaccharides ending with the structure beta-D-xylopyranosyl-
CC       (1->4)-beta-D-xylopyranosyl-(1->4)-beta-D-xylopyranose, leaving the
CC       new reducing end in the alpha configuration.
CC   -!- It is specific for the beta anomers of xylooligosaccharides whose
CC       degree of polymerization is equal to or greater than 3.
CC   -!- The penultimate residue must be beta-D-xylopyranose, but replacing
CC       either of the flanking residues with glucose merely slows the rate
CC       greatly.
DR   A1A048, REOX_BIFAA ;  Q9KB30, REOX_HALH5 ;  A0A0S2UQQ5, REX8A_PAEBA;
//
ID   3.2.1.157
DE   iota-carrageenase.
CA   Endohydrolysis of 1,4-beta-D-linkages between D-galactose 4-sulfate and
CA   3,6-anhydro-D-galactose-2-sulfate in iota-carrageenans.
CC   -!- The main products of hydrolysis are iota-neocarratetraose sulfate and
CC       iota-neocarrahexaose sulfate.
CC   -!- Iota-neocarraoctaose is the shortest substrate oligomer that can be
CC       cleaved.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 3.2.1.81 and EC 3.2.1.83, this enzyme proceeds with
CC       inversion of the anomeric configuration.
CC   -!- Iota-carrageenan differs from kappa-carrageenan by possessing a sulfo
CC       group on O-2 of the 3,6-anhydro-D-galactose residues, in addition to
CC       that present in the kappa-compound on O-4 of the D-galactose
CC       residues.
DR   Q9F5I8, CGIA_ALTMA ;  Q9F284, CGIA_ZOBGA ;
//
ID   3.2.1.158
DE   alpha-agarase.
AN   agarase A33.
CA   Endohydrolysis of 1,3-alpha-L-galactosidic linkages in agarose, yielding
CA   agarotetraose as the major product.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Thalassomonas sp. can use agarose, agarohexaose and
CC       neoagarohexaose as substrate.
CC   -!- The products of agarohexaose hydrolysis are dimers and tetramers,
CC       with agarotetraose being the predominant product, whereas hydrolysis
CC       of neoagarohexaose gives rise to two types of trimer.
CC   -!- While the enzyme can also hydrolyze the highly sulfated agarose
CC       porphyran very efficiently, it cannot hydrolyze kappa-carrageenan and
CC       iota-carrageenan.
DR   Q9LAP7, AAGAR_ALTAG;  A1IGV8, AAGAR_THASX;  P0DY28, BAF96_AQUS2;
//
ID   3.2.1.159
DE   alpha-neoagaro-oligosaccharide hydrolase.
AN   alpha-NAOS hydrolase.
AN   alpha-neoagarooligosaccharide hydrolase.
CA   Hydrolysis of the 1,3-alpha-L-galactosidic linkages of neoagaro-
CA   oligosaccharides that are smaller than a hexamer, yielding 3,6-anhydro-L-
CA   galactose and D-galactose.
CC   -!- When neoagarohexaose is used as a substrate, the oligosaccharide is
CC       cleaved at the non-reducing end to produce 3,6-anhydro-L-galactose
CC       and agaropentaose, which is further hydrolyzed to agarobiose and
CC       agarotriose.
CC   -!- With neoagarotetraose as substrate, the products are predominantly
CC       agarotriose and 3,6-anhydro-L-galactose.
CC   -!- In Vibrio sp. the actions of EC 3.2.1.81 and EC 3.2.1.159 can be used
CC       to degrade agarose to 3,6-anhydro-L-galactose and D-galactose.
//
