ID   3.2.1.191
DE   ginsenosidase type III.
CA   a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with two glucosyl residues at position
CA   3 + 2 H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with no glycosidic
CA   modification at position 3 + 2 D-glucopyranose.
CC   -!- Ginsenosidase type III catalyzes the sequential hydrolysis of the
CC       3-O-beta-D-(1->2)-glucopyranosyl bond followed by hydrolysis of the
CC       3-O-beta-D-glucopyranosyl bond of protopanaxadiol ginsenosides.
CC   -!- When acting for example on ginsenoside Rb1 the enzyme first generates
CC       ginsenoside XVII, and subsequently ginsenoside LXXV.
//
ID   3.2.1.192
DE   ginsenoside Rb1 beta-glucosidase.
CA   (20S)-ginsenoside Rb1 + 2 H2O = (20S)-ginsenoside Rg3 + 2 D-glucose.
CC   -!- Ginsenosidases catalyze the hydrolysis of glycosyl moieties attached
CC       to the C-3, C-6 or C-20 position of ginsenosides.
CC   -!- They are specific with respect to the nature of the glycosidic
CC       linkage, the position and the order in which the linkages are
CC       cleaved.
CC   -!- Ginsenoside Rb1 beta-glucosidase specifically and sequentially
CC       hydrolyzes the 20-[beta-D-glucopyranosyl-(1->6)-beta-D
CC       glucopyranosyloxy] residues attached to position 20 by first
CC       hydrolyzing the (1->6)-glucosidic bond to generate ginsenoside Rd as
CC       an intermediate, followed by hydrolysis of the remaining 20-O-beta-D-
CC       glucosidic bond.
//
ID   3.2.1.193
DE   ginsenosidase type I.
CA   (1) a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with two glucosyl residues at
CA   position 3 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with one glucosyl
CA   residue at position 3 + D-glucopyranose.
CA   (2) a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with one glucosyl residue at
CA   position 3 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with no glycosidic
CA   modifications at position 3 + D-glucopyranose.
CA   (3) a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with two glycosyl residues at
CA   position 20 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with a single
CA   glucosyl residue at position 20 + a monosaccharide.
CC   -!- The enzyme hydrolyzes the 3-O-beta-D-(1->2)-glucosidic bond, the 3-O-
CC       beta-D-glucopyranosyl bond and the 20-O-beta-D-(1->6)-glycosidic bond
CC       of protopanaxadiol-type ginsenosides.
CC   -!- It usually leaves a single glucosyl residue attached at position 20
CC       and one or no glucosyl residues at position 3.
CC   -!- Starting with a ginsenoside that is glycosylated at both positions
CC       (e.g. ginsenoside Rb1, Rb2, Rb3, Rc or Rd), the most common products
CC       are ginsenoside F2 and ginsenoside C-K, with low amounts of
CC       ginsenoside Rh2.
//
ID   3.2.1.194
DE   ginsenosidase type IV.
CA   a protopanaxatriol-type ginsenoside with two glycosyl residues at
CA   position 6 + 2 H2O = a protopanaxatriol-type ginsenoside with no
CA   glycosidic modification at position 6 + D-glucopyranose +
CA   a monosaccharide.
CC   -!- Ginsenosidase type IV catalyzes the sequential hydrolysis of the 6-O-
CC       beta-D-(1->2)-glycosidic bond or the 6-O-alpha-D-(1->2)-glycosidic
CC       bond in protopanaxatriol-type ginsenosides with a disacchride
CC       attached to the C6 position, followed by the hydrolysis of the
CC       remaining 6-O-beta-D-glycosidic bond (e.g. ginsenoside Re ->
CC       ginsenoside Rg1 -> ginsenoside F1).
//
ID   3.2.1.195
DE   20-O-multi-glycoside ginsenosidase.
CA   a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with two glycosyl residues at position
CA   20 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with a single glucosyl
CA   residue at position 20 + a monosaccharide.
CC   -!- The 20-O-multi-glycoside ginsenosidase catalyzes the hydrolysis of
CC       the 20-O-alpha-(1->6)-glycosidic bond and the 20-O-beta-(1->6)-
CC       glycosidic bond of protopanaxadiol-type ginsenosides.
CC   -!- The enzyme usually leaves a single glucosyl residue attached at
CC       position 20, although it can cleave the remaining glucosyl residue
CC       with a lower efficiency.
CC   -!- Starting with a ginsenoside that is glycosylated at positions 3 and
CC       20, such as ginsenosides Rb1, Rb2, Rb3 and Rc, the most common
CC       product is ginsenoside Rd, with a low amount of ginsenoside Rg3 also
CC       formed.
//
ID   3.2.1.196
DE   limit dextrin alpha-1,6-maltotetraose-hydrolase.
AN   glycogen debranching enzyme.
CA   Hydrolysis of (1->6)-alpha-D-glucosidic linkages to branches with degrees
CA   of polymerization of three or four glucose residues in limit dextrin.
CC   -!- This bacterial enzyme catalyzes a reaction similar to EC 3.2.1.33.
CC   -!- However, while EC 3.2.1.33 removes single glucose residues linked by
CC       1,6-alpha-linkage, and thus requires the additional activity of
