ID   3.3.2.10
DE   soluble epoxide hydrolase.
AN   arene-oxide hydratase.
AN   aryl epoxide hydrase.
AN   cytosolic epoxide hydrolase.
AN   epoxide hydrase.
AN   epoxide hydratase.
AN   trans-stilbene oxide hydrolase.
CA   an epoxide + H2O = an ethanediol.
CC   -!- Catalyzes the hydrolysis of trans-substituted epoxides, such as
CC       trans-stilbene oxide, as well as various aliphatic epoxides derived
CC       from fatty-acid metabolism.
CC   -!- It is involved in the metabolism of arachidonic epoxides
CC       (epoxyeicosatrienoic acids; EETs) and linoleic acid epoxides.
CC   -!- The enzyme from mammals is a bifunctional enzyme: the C-terminal
CC       domain exhibits epoxide-hydrolase activity and the N-terminal domain
CC       has the activity of EC 3.1.3.76.
CC   -!- Like EC 3.3.2.9, it is probable that the reaction involves the
CC       formation of an hydroxyalkyl-enzyme intermediate.
CC   -!- The enzyme can also use leukotriene A4, the substrate of EC 3.3.2.6,
CC       but it forms 5,6-dihydroxy-7,9,11,14-eicosatetraenoic acid rather
CC       than leukotriene B4 as the product.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.3.2.3, EC 4.2.1.63 and EC 4.2.1.64.
