ID   3.4.22.61
DE   caspase-8.
AN   apoptotic cysteine protease.
AN   apoptotic protease Mch-5.
AN   CAP4.
AN   CASP-8.
AN   FADD-homologous ICE/CED-3-like protease.
AN   FADD-like ICE.
AN   FLICE.
AN   ICE-like apoptotic protease 5.
AN   MACH.
AN   mammalian Ced-3 homolog 5.
AN   Mch5.
AN   MORT1-associated CED-3 homolog.
CA   Strict requirement for Asp at position P1 and has a preferred cleavage
CA   sequence of (Leu/Asp/Val)-Glu-Thr-Asp-|-(Gly/Ser/Ala).
CC   -!- Caspase-8 is an initiator caspase, as are caspase-2 (EC 3.4.22.55),
CC       caspase-9 (EC 3.4.22.62) and caspase-10 (EC 3.4.22.63).
CC   -!- Apical activator of the extrinsic (death receptor) apoptosis pathway,
CC       triggered by death receptor ligation.
CC   -!- Contains two tandem death effector domains (DEDs) in its N-terminal
CC       prodomain, which play a role in procaspase activation.
CC   -!- Linked to cell surface death receptors such as Fas.
CC   -!- When Fas is aggregated by the Fas ligand, procaspase-8 is recruited
CC       to the death receptor where it is activated.
CC   -!- Has a preference for Glu at P3 and prefers small residues, such as
CC       Gly, Ser and Ala at the P1' position.
CC   -!- Has very broad P4 specificity, tolerating substrates with Asp, Val or
CC       Leu in this position.
CC   -!- Endogenous substrates for caspase-8 include procaspase-3, the pro-
CC       apoptotic Bcl-2 family member Bid, RIP, PAK2 and the caspase-8
CC       activity modulator FLIPL.
CC   -!- Belongs to peptidase family C14.
DR   Q8IRY7, CASP8_DROME;  Q29IM7, CASP8_DROPS;  Q14790, CASP8_HUMAN;
DR   O89110, CASP8_MOUSE;  Q9JHX4, CASP8_RAT  ;
//
ID   3.4.22.62
DE   caspase-9.
AN   APAF-3.
AN   apoptotic protease-activating factor 3.
AN   apoptotic protease Mch-6.
AN   CASP-9.
AN   ICE-LAP6.
AN   ICE-like apoptotic protease 6.
CA   Strict requirement for an Asp residue at position P1 and with a marked
CA   preference for His at position P2. It has a preferred cleavage sequence
CA   of Leu-Gly-His-Asp-|-Xaa.
CC   -!- Caspase-9 is an initiator caspase, as are caspase-2 (EC 3.4.22.55),
CC       caspase-8 (EC 3.4.22.61) and caspase-10 (EC 3.4.22.63).
CC   -!- Contains a caspase-recruitment domain (CARD) in its N-terminal
CC       prodomain, which plays a role in procaspase activation.
CC   -!- An alternatively spliced version of caspase-9 also exists, caspase-
CC       9S, that inhibits apoptosis, similar to the situation found with
CC       caspase-2.
CC   -!- Phosphorylation of caspase-9 from some species by Akt, a serine-
CC       threonine protein kinase, inhibits caspase activity in vitro and
CC       caspase activation in vivo.
CC   -!- The activity of caspase-9 is increased dramatically upon association
CC       with the apoptosome but the enzyme can be activated without
CC       proteolytic cleavage.
CC   -!- Procaspase-3 is the enzyme's physiological substrate.
CC   -!- Belongs to peptidase family C14.
DR   P55211, CASP9_HUMAN;  Q8C3Q9, CASP9_MOUSE;  Q9XYF4, DRONC_DROME;
//
ID   3.4.22.63
DE   caspase-10.
AN   apoptotic protease Mch-4.
AN   CASP-10.
AN   FAS-associated death domain protein interleukin-1beta-converting enzyme
AN   2.
AN   FLICE2.
AN   ICE-like apoptotic protease 4.
AN   Mch4.
CA   Strict requirement for Asp at position P1 and has a preferred cleavage
CA   sequence of Leu-Gln-Thr-Asp-|-Gly.
CC   -!- Caspase-10 is an initiator caspase, as are caspase-2 (EC 3.4.22.55),
CC       caspase-8 (EC 3.4.22.61) and caspase-9 (EC 3.4.22.62).
CC   -!- Like caspase-8, it contains two tandem death effector domains (DEDs)
CC       in its N-terminal prodomain, which play a role in procaspase
CC       activation.
CC   -!- Has many overlapping substrates in common with caspase-8, such as RIP
CC       (the cleavage of which impairs NF-kappaB survival signaling and
CC       starts the cell-death process) and PAK2 (associated with some of the
CC       morphological features of apoptosis, such as cell rounding and
CC       apoptotic body formation).
CC   -!- Bid, a Bcl2 protein, can be cleaved by caspase-3 (EC 3.4.22.56),
CC       caspase-8 and caspase-10 at Lys-Gln-Thr-Asp-|- to yield the pro-
CC       apoptotic p15 fragment.
CC   -!- The p15 fragment is N-myristoylated and enhances cytochrome c release
CC       from mitochondria (which initiatiates the intrinsic apoptosis
CC       pathway).
CC   -!- Bid can be further cleaved by caspase-10 and granzyme B but not by
CC       caspase-3 and caspase-8 at Ile-Glu-Thr-Asp-|- to yield a p13 fragment
CC       that is not N-myristoylated.
CC   -!- Belongs to peptidase family C14.
DR   Q92851, CASPA_HUMAN;
//
ID   3.4.22.64
DE   caspase-11.
AN   CASP-11.
CA   Strict requirement for Asp at the P1 position and has a preferred
CA   cleavage sequence of (Ile/Leu/Val/Phe)-Gly-His-Asp-|-.
CC   -!- Part of the family of inflammatory-caspases, which also includes
CC       caspase-1 (EC 3.4.22.36), caspase-4 (EC 3.4.22.57) and caspase-5
CC       (EC 3.4.22.58) in humans and caspase-12, caspase-13 and caspase-14 in
CC       mice.
CC   -!- Contains a caspase-recruitment domain (CARD) in its N-terminal
CC       prodomain, which plays a role in procaspase activation.
CC   -!- Like caspase-5, but unlike caspase-4, this enzyme can be induced by
CC       lipopolysaccharide.
CC   -!- Not only activates caspase-1, which is required for the maturation of
CC       proinflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-1beta (IL-1beta) and
CC       IL-18, but it also activates caspase-3 (EC 3.4.22.56), which leads to
CC       cellular apoptosis under pathological conditions.
CC   -!- Belongs to peptidase family C14.
DR   P70343, CASP4_MOUSE;
