ID   3.4.23.41
DE   yapsin 1.
AN   aspartic proteinase 3.
AN   yeast aspartic protease 3.
CA   Hydrolyzes various precursor proteins with Arg or Lys in P1, and commonly
CA   Arg or Lys also in P2. The P3 amino acid is usually non-polar,
CA   but otherwise additional basic amino acids are favorable in both non-
CA   prime and prime positions.
CC   -!- Weakly inhibited by pepstatin.
CC   -!- Can partially substitute for kexin in a deficient strain of
CC       Saccharomyces cerevisiae.
CC   -!- The homologous product of the MKC7 gene (S.cerevisiae) has similar
CC       catalytic activity and has been termed yapsin 2.
CC   -!- Belongs to peptidase family A1.
DR   P53379, MKC7_YEAST ;  P32329, YPS1_YEAST ;
//
ID   3.4.23.42
DE   thermopsin.
CA   Specificity similar to pepsin A, prefers bulky hydrophobic side-chains on
CA   either side of the scissible bond.
CC   -!- Isolated from cells and culture medium of the thermophilic archae
CC       Sulfolobus acidocaldarius, optimum digestion at pH 2 and 90 degrees
CC       Celsius, inhibited by pepstatin.
CC   -!- Another thermostable bacterial endopeptidase has been isolated from
CC       Bacillus sp., but this is not inhibited by pepstatin.
CC   -!- Belongs to peptidase family A5.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.4.99.43.
DR   P17118, THPS_SULAC ;
//
ID   3.4.23.43
DE   prepilin peptidase.
CA   Typically cleaves a -Gly-|-Phe- bond to release an N-terminal, basic
CA   peptide of 5-8 residues from type IV prepilin, and then N-methylates the
CA   new N-terminal amino group, the methyl donor being S-adenosyl-L-
CA   methionine.
CC   -!- Many species of bacteria carry pili on their cell surfaces.
CC   -!- These are virulence determinants in pathogenic strains, and are
CC       assembled biosynthetically from type IV prepilin subunits.
CC   -!- Before assembly, the prepilin molecules require proteolytic
CC       processing, which is done by the prepilin peptidase.
CC   -!- Prepilin peptidase and its homologs play a central role not only in
CC       type IV pilus biogenesis but also in transport of macromolecules
CC       across cell membranes.
CC   -!- Although both peptide-bond hydrolysis and N-methylation are catalyzed
CC       by the same molecule, the methylation can be inhibited without
CC       affecting peptidase activity, and it is believed that the enzyme has
CC       two separate catalytic sites.
CC   -!- Belongs to peptidase family A24.
DR   P45794, LEP4_AERHY ;  A2T195, LEP4_AERS4 ;  P0C423, LEP4_AERSA ;
