//
ID   3.5.1.5
DE   urease.
CA   urea + 2 H2O + H(+) = hydrogencarbonate + 2 NH4(+).
CC   -!- A nickel protein.
CC   -!- Urease catalyzes the hydrolysis of urea to yield ammonia and
CC       carbamate, which spontaneously hydrolyzes to form carbonic acid and a
CC       second molecule of ammonia.
CC   -!- In aqueous solutions the products convert to ammonium and hydrogen
CC       carbonate.
CC   -!- The enzyme is widespread and is found in many bacteria, some archaea,
CC       and many eukaryotes, including plants, some invertebrates,
CC       and numerous eukaryotic microorganisms.
CC   -!- Urease is one of the earlier enzymes to be studied, first obtained In
CC       1874, and named urease in 1890.
CC   -!- The crystallization of urease in 1926 was a significant landmark in
CC       biochemistry, showing for the first time ever that enzymes are
CC       proteins and can be crystallized.
CC   -!- In 1975 it was found that the enzyme contains nickel ions in its
CC       active site.
