//
ID   3.5.1.112
DE   2'-N-acetylparomamine deacetylase.
CA   2'-N-acetylparomamine + H2O = paromamine + acetate.
CC   -!- Involved in the biosynthetic pathways of several clinically important
CC       aminocyclitol antibiotics, including kanamycin, butirosin, neomycin
CC       and ribostamycin.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Streptomyces fradiae can also accept
CC       2'''-acetyl-6'''-hydroxyneomycin C as substrate, cf. EC 3.5.1.113.
DR   Q4H4F3, BTRD_NIACI ;  Q53U10, NEOL_STRFR ;
//
ID   3.5.1.113
DE   2'''-acetyl-6'''-hydroxyneomycin C deacetylase.
CA   2'''-acetyl-6'''-hydroxyneomycin C + H2O = 6'''-deamino-
CA   6'''-hydroxyneomycin C + acetate.
CC   -!- Involved in the biosynthetic pathway of aminoglycoside antibiotics of
CC       the neomycin family.
CC   -!- The enzyme from the bacterium Streptomyces fradiae also catalyzes
CC       EC 3.5.1.112.
DR   Q53U10, NEOL_STRFR ;
//
ID   3.5.1.114
DE   N-acyl-aromatic-L-amino acid amidohydrolase.
AN   aminoacylase 3.
AN   aminoacylase III.
CA   (1) an N-acyl-aromatic L-alpha-amino acid + H2O = an aromatic L-alpha-
CA   amino acid + a carboxylate.
CA   (2) an N-acetyl-L-cysteine-S-conjugate + H2O = an S-substituted
CA   L-cysteine + acetate.
CC   -!- This enzyme is found in animals and is involved in the hydrolysis of
CC       N-acylated or N-acetylated amino acids (except L-aspartate).
CC   -!- It preferentially deacetylates N(alpha)-acetylated aromatic amino
CC       acids and mercapturic acids (S-conjugates of N-acetyl-L-cysteine)
CC       that are usually not deacetylated by EC 3.5.1.14.
CC   -!- Some bacterial aminoacylases demonstrate substrate specificity for
CC       both EC 3.5.1.14 and EC 3.5.1.114. cf. EC 3.5.1.14 and EC 3.5.1.15.
DR   Q6DHI0, ACY3A_DANRE;  Q6DHQ3, ACY3B_DANRE;  Q96HD9, ACY3_HUMAN ;
DR   Q91XE4, ACY3_MOUSE ;  Q5M876, ACY3_RAT   ;  A0JMS7, ACY3_XENLA ;
DR   Q5BJ91, ACY3_XENTR ;  Q32LT9, P2011_DANRE;  Q08BB2, P2012_DANRE;
DR   Q2T9M7, P20D1_BOVIN;  Q5ZL18, P20D1_CHICK;  Q6GTS8, P20D1_HUMAN;
DR   Q8C165, P20D1_MOUSE;  Q08BT9, P20D1_XENTR;
//
ID   3.5.1.115
DE   mycothiol S-conjugate amidase.
CA   mycothiol S-conjugate + H2O = an N-acetyl-L-cysteine-S-conjugate +
CA   1D-myo-inositol 2-amino-2-deoxy-alpha-D-glucopyranoside.
CC   -!- The enzyme that is found in actinomycetes is involved in the
CC       detoxification of oxidizing agents and electrophilic antibiotics.
CC   -!- The enzyme has low activity with 1-O-(2-acetamido-2-deoxy-alpha-D-
CC       glucopyranosyl)-1D-myo-inositol as substrate (cf. EC 3.5.1.103).
DR   A0R2W9, MCA_MYCS2  ;  P9WJN0, MCA_MYCTO  ;  P9WJN1, MCA_MYCTU  ;
DR   Q9ADK0, MCA_STRCO  ;
//
ID   3.5.1.116
DE   ureidoglycolate amidohydrolase.
AN   ureidoglycolate hydrolase.
CA   (S)-ureidoglycolate + H2O + 2 H(+) = glyoxylate + 2 NH4(+) + CO2.
CC   -!- This plant enzyme is involved in the degradation of ureidoglycolate,
CC       an intermediate of purine degradation.
CC   -!- Not to be confused with EC 4.3.2.3, which releases urea rather than
CC       ammonia.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.5.3.19.
DR   Q8VXY9, UAH_ARATH  ;  Q2QMN7, UAH_ORYSJ  ;
//
ID   3.5.1.117
DE   6-aminohexanoate-oligomer endohydrolase.
AN   6-aminohexanoate oligomer hydrolase.
AN   endo-type 6-aminohexanoate oligomer hydrolase.
AN   nylon-6 hydrolase.
AN   nylon hydrolase.
AN   nylon-oligomer hydrolase.
CA   [N-(6-aminohexanoyl)]n + H2O = [N-(6-aminohexanoyl)]n-x +
CA   [N-(6-aminohexanoyl)]x.
CC   -!- The enzyme is involved in degradation of nylon-6 oligomers.
CC   -!- It degrades linear or cyclic oligomers of poly(6-aminohexanoate) with
CC       a degree of polymerization greater than three (n > 3) by endo-type
CC       cleavage, to oligomers of a length of two or more (2 <= x < n).
CC   -!- It shows negligible activity with N-(6-aminohexanoyl)-6-
CC       aminohexanoate (cf. EC 3.5.1.46) or with 1,8-diazacyclotetradecane-
CC       2,9-dione (cf. EC 3.5.2.12).
DR   Q1EPR5, NYLC_AGRS5 ;  Q1EPR4, NYLC_KOCS2 ;  Q79F77, NYLC_PAEUR ;
//
ID   3.5.1.118
DE   gamma-glutamyl hercynylcysteine S-oxide hydrolase.
CA   gamma-L-glutamyl-hercynylcysteine S-oxide + H2O = S-(hercyn-2-yl)-L-
CA   cysteine S-oxide + L-glutamate.
CC   -!- The enzyme is part of the biosynthesis pathway of ergothioneine in
CC       mycobacteria.
DR   A0R5M9, EGTC_MYCS2 ;  Q8VIV2, EGTC_MYCTO ;  O69670, EGTC_MYCTU ;
//
ID   3.5.1.119
DE   Pup amidohydrolase.
AN   depupylase.
AN   depupylase/deamidase.
AN   DPUP.
CA   [prokaryotic ubiquitin-like protein]-C-terminal-L-glutamine + H2O =
CA   [prokaryotic ubiquitin-like protein]-C-terminal-L-glutamate + NH4(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme has been characterized from the bacterium Mycobacterium
CC       tuberculosis.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the hydrolysis of the amido group of the C-terminal
CC       glutamine of prokaryotic ubiquitin-like protein (Pup), thus
CC       activating it for ligation to target proteins, a process catalyzed by
CC       EC 6.3.1.19.
CC   -!- Deamination requires ATP as cofactor but not its hydrolysis.
CC   -!- The enzyme also catalyzes the hydrolytic cleavage of the bond formed
CC       by the ligase, between an epsilon-amino group of a lysine residue of
CC       the target protein and the gamma-carboxylate of the C-terminal
CC       glutamate of the prokaryotic ubiquitin-like protein.
DR   A0QZ49, DOP_MYCS2  ;  P9WNU8, DOP_MYCTO  ;  P9WNU9, DOP_MYCTU  ;
DR   Q53081, DOP_RHOER  ;
//
ID   3.5.1.120
DE   Transferred entry: 3.5.99.11.
//
ID   3.5.1.121
DE   protein N-terminal asparagine amidohydrolase.
CA   N-terminal L-asparaginyl-[protein] + H2O + H(+) = N-terminal L-aspartyl-
CA   [protein] + NH4(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme participates in the eukaryotic ubiquitin-dependent
CC       Arg/N-end rule pathway of protein degradation, promoting the turnover
CC       of intracellular proteins that initiate with Met-Asn.
CC   -!- Following the acetylation and removal of the initiator methionine,
CC       the exposed N-terminal asparagine is deaminated, resulting in its
CC       conversion to L-aspartate.
CC   -!- The latter serves as a substrate for EC 2.3.2.8 making the protein
CC       susceptible to arginylation, polyubiquitination and degradation as
CC       specified by the N-end rule.
DR   O64876, NTAN1_ARATH;  Q96AB6, NTAN1_HUMAN;  Q64311, NTAN1_MOUSE;
DR   Q28955, NTAN1_PIG  ;
//
ID   3.5.1.122
DE   protein N-terminal glutamine amidohydrolase.
CA   N-terminal L-glutaminyl-[protein] + H2O = N-terminal L-glutamyl-[protein]
CA   + NH4(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme participates in the eukaryotic ubiquitin-dependent
CC       Arg/N-end rule pathway of protein degradation, promoting the turnover
CC       of intracellular proteins that initiate with Met-Gln.
CC   -!- Following the acetylation and removal of the initiator methionine,
CC       the exposed N-terminal glutamine is deaminated, resulting in its
CC       conversion to L-glutamate.
CC   -!- The latter serves as a substrate for EC 2.3.2.8 making the protein
CC       susceptible to arginylation, polyubiquitination and degradation as
CC       specified by the N-end rule.
DR   Q7Q968, NTAQ1_ANOGA;  O22944, NTAQ1_ARATH;  Q3T0D3, NTAQ1_BOVIN;
DR   A8Y183, NTAQ1_CAEBR;  Q21775, NTAQ1_CAEEL;  Q1LWX3, NTAQ1_DANRE;
DR   B3MCF3, NTAQ1_DROAN;  B3NQ86, NTAQ1_DROER;  B4J8A0, NTAQ1_DROGR;
DR   Q7K2Y9, NTAQ1_DROME;  B4KPY6, NTAQ1_DROMO;  Q28WL0, NTAQ1_DROPS;
DR   B4HSF8, NTAQ1_DROSE;  B4QGZ1, NTAQ1_DROSI;  B4MDT2, NTAQ1_DROVI;
DR   B4MQL4, NTAQ1_DROWI;  B4P6V4, NTAQ1_DROYA;  Q96HA8, NTAQ1_HUMAN;
DR   Q80WB5, NTAQ1_MOUSE;  B8AXU2, NTAQ1_ORYSI;  Q60E61, NTAQ1_ORYSJ;
DR   Q5BJV9, NTAQ1_RAT  ;  Q5PPU8, NTAQ1_XENLA;
