ID   3.5.1.136
DE   N,N'-diacetylchitobiose non-reducing end deacetylase.
AN   diacetylchitobiose deacetylase.
CA   N,N'-diacetylchitobiose + H2O = beta-D-glucosaminyl-(1->4)-N-acetyl-D-
CA   glucosamine + acetate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the archaeons Thermococcus
CC       kodakarensis and Pyrococcus horikoshii, deacetylates the non-reducing
CC       residue of N,N'-diacetylchitobiose, the end product of the archaeal
CC       chitinase, to produce beta-D-glucosaminyl-(1->4)-N-acetyl-D-
CC       glucosamine.
CC   -!- This is in contrast to EC 3.5.1.105, chitin disaccharide deacetylase,
CC       which deacetylates N,N'-diacetylchitobiose at the reducing residue to
CC       produce N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosaminyl-(1->4)-D-glucosamine.
DR   Q6F4N1, DCHI_THEKO ;
//
ID   3.5.1.137
DE   N-methylcarbamate hydrolase.
AN   carbaryl hydrolase.
AN   carbofuran hydrolase.
CA   an N-methyl carbamate ester + H2O + H(+) = an alcohol + methylamine +
CA   CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the first step in the degradation of several
CC       carbamate insecticides such as carbaryl, carbofuran, isoprocarb,
CC       propoxur, aldicarb and oxamyl.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the cleavage of the ester bond to release
CC       N-methylcarbamate, which spontaneously hydrolyzes to methylamine and
CC       CO2.
CC   -!- The enzymes from several Gram-negative bacteria were shown to be
CC       located in the periplasm.
//
