ID   3.5.2.10
DE   creatininase.
AN   Creatinine amidohydrolase.
CA   creatinine + H2O = creatine.
DR   P83772, CRNA_PSEPU ;
//
ID   3.5.2.11
DE   L-lysine-lactamase.
AN   L-alpha-aminocaprolactam hydrolase.
AN   L-lysinamidase.
CA   L-2-aminohexano-6-lactam + H2O = L-lysine.
CC   -!- Also hydrolyzes L-lysinamide.
//
ID   3.5.2.12
DE   6-aminohexanoate-cyclic-dimer hydrolase.
CA   1,8-diazacyclotetradecane-2,9-dione + H2O = N-(6-aminohexanoyl)-6-
CA   aminohexanoate.
CC   -!- The cyclic dimer of 6-aminohexanoate is converted to the linear
CC       dimer.
DR   P13398, NYLA_PAEUR ;  P13397, NYLA_PSES8 ;
//
ID   3.5.2.13
DE   2,5-dioxopiperazine hydrolase.
AN   cyclo(Gly-Gly) hydrolase.
CA   2,5-dioxopiperazine + H2O = glycylglycine.
CC   -!- Highly specific; does not hydrolyze other dioxopiperazines,
CC       glycylglycine, proteins or barbiturates.
//
ID   3.5.2.14
DE   N-methylhydantoinase (ATP-hydrolyzing).
AN   N-methylhydantoin amidohydrolase.
CA   N-methylhydantoin + ATP + 2 H2O = N-carbamoylsarcosine + ADP + phosphate
CA   + 2 H(+).
//
ID   3.5.2.15
DE   cyanuric acid amidohydrolase.
CA   cyanurate + H2O = 1-carboxybiuret + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the ring cleavage of cyanuric acid,
CC       an intermediate in the degradation of s-triazide herbicides such as
CC       atrazine [2-chloro-4-(ethylamino)-6-(isopropylamino)-1,3,5-triazine].
CC   -!- The enzyme is highly specific for cyanuric acid.
CC   -!- The product was initially thought to be bioret, but was later shown
CC       to be 1-carboxybioret.
DR   A8IKD2, CAH_AZOC5  ;  Q89E06, CAH_BRADU  ;  H0SH23, CAH_BRAS3  ;
DR   W6RJ11, CAH_ECTO5  ;  P0A3V4, CAH_ENTCL  ;  B0UET5, CAH_METS4  ;
DR   Q2RGM7, CAH_MOOTA  ;  P58329, CAH_PSESD  ;  P0A3V5, CAH_PSESP  ;
DR   F4CUJ4, CAH_PSEUX  ;  Q1M7F3, CAH_RHIJ3  ;  C6BAU4, CAH_RHILS  ;
DR   H8ZKW0, CAH_RHOSO  ;  U3N9N6, CAH_SARSX  ;
//
ID   3.5.2.16
DE   maleimide hydrolase.
AN   cyclic imide hydrolase.
AN   imidase.
CA   maleimide + H2O = maleamate + H(+).
CC   -!- Succinimide and glutarimide, and sulfur-containing cyclic imides,
CC       such as rhodanine, can also act as substrates for the enzyme from
CC       Blastobacter sp. A17p-4.
CC   -!- The reverse, cyclization, reaction is also catalyzed, but much more
CC       slowly.
CC   -!- It has lower activity toward cyclic ureides, which are the substrates
CC       of EC 3.5.2.2.
//
ID   3.5.2.17
DE   hydroxyisourate hydrolase.
AN   5-hydroxyisourate hydrolase.
AN   HIUHase.
CA   5-hydroxyisourate + H2O = 5-hydroxy-2-oxo-4-ureido-2,5-dihydro-
CA   1H-imidazole-5-carboxylate + H(+).
CC   -!- The reaction is the first stage in the conversion of
CC       5-hydroxyisourate into (S)-allantoin.
CC   -!- This reaction will also occur spontaneously, but more slowly.
DR   Q21882, HIUH1_CAEEL;  Q92VB9, HIUH1_RHIME;  O44578, HIUH2_CAEEL;
