ID   3.5.4.20
DE   pyrithiamine deaminase.
CA   1-(4-amino-2-methylpyrimid-5-ylmethyl)-3-(2-hydroxyethyl)-2-
CA   methylpyridinium + H2O + H(+) = 1-(4-hydroxy-2-methylpyrimid-5-ylmethyl)-
CA   3-(2-hydroxyethyl)-2-methylpyridinium + NH4(+).
//
ID   3.5.4.21
DE   creatinine deaminase.
CA   creatinine + H2O + H(+) = N-methylhydantoin + NH4(+).
//
ID   3.5.4.22
DE   1-pyrroline-4-hydroxy-2-carboxylate deaminase.
CA   4-hydroxy-1-pyrroline-2-carboxylate + H2O + H(+) = 2,5-dioxopentanoate +
CA   NH4(+).
//
ID   3.5.4.23
DE   blasticidin-S deaminase.
CA   blasticidin S + H2O + H(+) = deaminohydroxyblasticidin S + NH4(+).
CC   -!- Catalyzes the deamination of the cytosine group of the antibiotics
CC       blasticidin S, cytomycin and acetylblasticidin S.
DR   P0C2P0, BSD_ASPTE  ;  P0C2P1, BSD_ASPTN  ;  P0CI02, BSD_COCIM  ;
DR   P33967, BSR_BACCE  ;
//
ID   3.5.4.24
DE   sepiapterin deaminase.
CA   L-sepiapterin + H2O + H(+) = (S)-xanthopterin-B2 + NH4(+).
CC   -!- Also acts on isosepiapterin, more slowly.
//
ID   3.5.4.25
DE   GTP cyclohydrolase II.
AN   GTP 7,8-8,9-dihydrolase (diphosphate-forming).
AN   GTP-8-formylhydrolase.
AN   guanosine triphosphate cyclohydrolase II.
CA   GTP + 4 H2O = 2,5-diamino-6-hydroxy-4-(5-phosphoribosylamino)-pyrimidine
CA   + formate + 2 phosphate + 3 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in prokaryotes and some eukaryotes, hydrolytically
CC       cleaves the C-N bond at positions 8 and 9 of GTP guanine, followed by
CC       a subsequent hydrolytic attack at the base, which liberates formate,
CC       and cleavage of the alpha-beta phosphodiester bond of the
CC       triphosphate to form diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme continues with a slow cleavage of the diphosphate to form
CC       two phosphate ions.
CC   -!- The enzyme requires zinc and magnesium ions for the cleavage
CC       reactions at the GTP guanine and triphosphate sites, respectively.
CC   -!- It is one of the enzymes required for flavin biosynthesis in many
CC       bacterial species, lower eukaryotes, and plants. cf. EC 3.5.4.16,
CC       EC 3.5.4.29, and EC 3.5.4.39.
