ID   3.5.4.43
DE   hydroxydechloroatrazine ethylaminohydrolase.
AN   2,4-dihydroxy-6-(isopropylamino)-1,3,5-triazine ethylaminohydrolase.
AN   hydroxyatrazine hydrolase.
CA   hydroxyatrazine + H2O + H(+) = N-isopropylammelide + ethylamine.
CC   -!- This bacterial enzyme is involved in degradation of the herbicide
CC       atrazine.
CC   -!- The enzyme has a broad substrate range, and requires a
CC       monohydroxylated S-triazine ring with a minimum of one primary or
CC       secondary amine substituent and either a chloride or amine leaving
CC       group.
CC   -!- It catalyzes both deamination and dechlorination reactions.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.5.99.3.
DR   P95442, ATZB_PSESD ;
//
ID   3.5.4.44
DE   ectoine hydrolase.
CA   L-ectoine + H2O = (2S)-2-acetamido-4-aminobutanoate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in some halophilic bacteria, is involved in the
CC       degradation of the compatible solute ectoine.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which belongs to peptidase family M24, only acts in the
CC       direction of ectoine hydrolysis.
CC   -!- It also produces smaller amounts of (2S)-4-acetamido-2-
CC       aminobutanoate, which is recycled back to ectoine by EC 4.2.1.108.
DR   E1V7W1, DOEA_HALED ;
//
ID   3.5.4.45
DE   melamine deaminase.
CA   (1) melamine + H2O = ammeline + NH4(+).
CA   (2) ammeline + H2O + H(+) = ammelide + NH4(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Acidovorax citrulli, performs
CC       the deamination of melamine 15-fold faster than the deamination of
CC       ammeline.
CC   -!- It also has activity with 2-chloro-4,6-diamino-S-triazine, but has no
CC       activity toward halo-substituted triazine ring compounds such as
CC       atrazine (cf. EC 3.8.1.8).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.5.4.n3.
DR   Q9EYU0, TRIA_PARCI ;
