//
ID   3.6.5.3
DE   protein-synthesizing GTPase.
AN   elongation factor (EF).
AN   initiation factor (IF).
AN   peptide-release factor (RF).
AN   peptide-release or termination factor.
CA   GTP + H2O = GDP + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme comprises a family of proteins involved in prokaryotic as
CC       well as eukaryotic protein synthesis.
CC   -!- In the initiation factor complex, it is IF-2b (98 kDa) that binds GTP
CC       and subsequently hydrolyzes it in prokaryotes.
CC   -!- In eukaryotes, it is eIF-2 (150 kDa) that binds GTP.
CC   -!- In the elongation phase, the GTP-hydrolyzing proteins are the EF-Tu
CC       polypeptide of the prokaryotic transfer factor (43 kDa),
CC       the eukaryotic elongation factor EF-1alpha (53 kDa), the prokaryotic
CC       EF-G (77 kDa), the eukaryotic EF-2 (70-110 kDa) and the signal
CC       recognition particle that play a role in endoplasmic reticulum
CC       protein synthesis (325 kDa).
CC   -!- EF-Tu and EF-1alpha catalyze binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the
CC       ribosomal A-site, while EF-G and EF-2 catalyze the translocation of
CC       peptidyl-tRNA from the A-site to the P-site.
CC   -!- GTPase activity is also involved in polypeptide release from the
CC       ribosome with the aid of the pRFs and eRFs.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.48.
