ID   3.13.2.3
DE   (R)-S-adenosyl-L-methionine hydrolase (adenosine-forming).
AN   SAM hydroxide adenosyltransferase.
CA   (R)-S-adenosyl-L-methionine + H2O = adenosine + L-methionine + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in bacteria and archaea, is involved in removing
CC       the (R) isomer of S-adenosyl-L-methionine from the cell.
CC   -!- It catalyzes a nucleophilic attack of water at the C5' carbon of
CC       S-adenosyl-L-methionine to generate adenosine and L-methionine.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.13.1.8.
DR   W0W999, FLA_STRSQ  ;  Q59045, RSAMH_METJA;  O58212, RSAMH_PYRHO;
DR   A8M783, RSAMH_SALAI;  A4X4S2, RSAMH_SALTO;  Q5SLF5, RSAMH_THET8;
//
ID   3.13.2.4
DE   lanthipeptide synthase.
CA   (1) a [protein]-L-serine + a [protein]-L-cysteine = a [protein] with
CA   lanthionine cross-link + H2O (overall reaction).
CA   (2) a [protein]-L-threonine + a [protein]-L-cysteine = a [protein] with
CA   3-methyllanthionine cross-link + H2O (overall reaction).
CC   -!- The lanthipeptides are a family of ribosomally synthesized and post-
CC       translationally modified peptides (RiPPs) that is characterized by
CC       the presence of multiple lanthionine (Lan) and 3-methyllanthionine
CC       (MeLan) crosslinks, which are formed by lanthipeptide synthases.
CC   -!- These enzymes catalyze dehydration of Ser/Thr residues, followed by
CC       intramolecular addition of the thiol group of a Cys residue to the
CC       dehydro amino acids, which results in the formation of the thioether
CC       crosslinks of (methyl)lanthionine.
//
