ID   4.1.1.88
DE   biotin-independent malonate decarboxylase.
AN   malonate carboxy-lyase (biotin-independent).
AN   malonate decarboxylase.
AN   MDC.
CA   malonate + H(+) = acetate + CO2.
CC   -!- Two types of malonate decarboxylase are currently known, both of
CC       which form multienzyme complexes.
CC   -!- This enzyme is a cytosolic protein that is biotin-independent.
CC   -!- The other type is a biotin-dependent, Na(+)-translocating enzyme that
CC       includes both soluble and membrane-bound components (cf. EC 7.2.4.4).
CC   -!- As free malonate is chemically rather inert, it has to be activated
CC       prior to decarboxylation.
CC   -!- In both enzymes, this is achieved by exchanging malonate with an
CC       acetyl group bound to an acyl-carrier protiein (ACP), to form
CC       malonyl-ACP and acetate, with subsequent decarboxylation regenerating
CC       the acetyl-ACP.
CC   -!- The ACP subunit of both enzymes differs from that found in fatty-acid
CC       biosynthesis by having phosphopantethine attached to a serine side-
CC       chain as 2'-(5-triphosphoribosyl)-3'-dephospho-CoA rather than as
CC       phosphopantetheine 4'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The individual enzymes involved in carrying out the reaction of this
CC       enzyme complex are EC 2.3.1.187, EC 2.3.1.39 and EC 4.1.1.87.
CC   -!- The carboxy group is lost with retention of configuration.
//
ID   4.1.1.89
DE   Transferred entry: 7.2.4.4.
//
ID   4.1.1.90
DE   peptidyl-glutamate 4-carboxylase.
AN   gamma-glutamyl carboxylase.
AN   vitamin K-dependent carboxylase.
CA   4-carboxy-L-glutamyl-[protein] + 2,3-epoxyphylloquinone + H2O + H(+) =
CA   phylloquinol + L-glutamyl-[protein] + CO2 + O2.
CC   -!- The enzyme can use various vitamin-K derivatives, including
CC       menaquinol, but does not contain iron.
CC   -!- The mechanism appears to involve the generation of a strong base by
CC       oxygenation of vitamin K.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the post-translational carboxylation of glutamate
CC       residues of several proteins of the blood-clotting system.
CC   -!- 9-12 glutamate residues are converted to 4-carboxyglutamate (Gla) in
CC       a specific domain of the target protein.
CC   -!- The 4-pro-S hydrogen of the glutamate residue is removed and there is
CC       an inversion of stereochemistry at this position.
DR   Q07175, VKGC_BOVIN ;  Q9MYY3, VKGC_DELLE ;  P38435, VKGC_HUMAN ;
DR   Q9QYC7, VKGC_MOUSE ;  Q5RF50, VKGC_PONAB ;  O88496, VKGC_RAT   ;
DR   Q9GL59, VKGC_SHEEP ;
//
ID   4.1.1.91
DE   salicylate decarboxylase.
AN   salicylic acid decarboxylase.
CA   salicylate + H(+) = phenol + CO2.
CC   -!- In the reverse direction the enzyme catalyzes the regioselective
CC       carboxylation of phenol into stoichiometric amounts of salicylate.
CC   -!- The enzyme also catalyzes the reversible decarboxylation of 2,4-
CC       dihydroxybenzoate, 2,6-dihydroxybenzoate, 2,3-dihydroxybenzoate and
CC       4-aminosalicylate.
DR   P0CT50, SDC_CUTMO  ;
//
ID   4.1.1.92
DE   indole-3-carboxylate decarboxylase.
CA   indole-3-carboxylate + H(+) = indole + CO2.
CC   -!- Activated by Zn(2+), Mn(2+) or Mg(2+).
//
ID   4.1.1.93
DE   pyrrole-2-carboxylate decarboxylase.
CA   (1) pyrrole-2-carboxylate + H(+) = 1H-pyrrole + CO2.
CA   (2) pyrrole-2-carboxylate + H2O = 1H-pyrrole + hydrogencarbonate.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes both the carboxylation and decarboxylation
CC       reactions.
CC   -!- However, while bicarbonate is the preferred substrate for the
CC       carboxylation reaction, decarboxylation produces carbon dioxide.
CC   -!- The enzyme is activated by carboxylic acids.
DR   Q9I6N5, P2CDC_PSEAE;
//
ID   4.1.1.94
DE   ethylmalonyl-CoA decarboxylase.
CA   (2S)-ethylmalonyl-CoA + H(+) = butanoyl-CoA + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, which exists in all vertebrates, decarboxylates
CC       ethylmalonyl-CoA, a potentially toxic compound that is formed in low
CC       amounts by the activity of EC 6.4.1.2 and EC 6.4.1.3.
CC   -!- It prefers the S isomer, and can decarboxylate (R)-ethylmalonyl-CoA
CC       with lower efficiency. cf. EC 4.1.1.41.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.1.1.n2.
DR   Q2HJD5, ECHD1_BOVIN;  F1NB38, ECHD1_CHICK;  F1R6N4, ECHD1_DANRE;
DR   Q9NTX5, ECHD1_HUMAN;  Q9D9V3, ECHD1_MOUSE;  Q5R4W0, ECHD1_PONAB;
DR   Q6AYG5, ECHD1_RAT  ;  Q5HZQ8, ECHD1_XENLA;  Q28C91, ECHD1_XENTR;
//
ID   4.1.1.95
DE   L-glutamyl-[BtrI acyl-carrier protein] decarboxylase.
CA   gamma-L-glutamyl-[BtrI ACP] + H(+) = 4-aminobutanoyl-[BtrI ACP] + CO2.
CC   -!- Catalyzes a step in the biosynthesis of the side chain of the
CC       aminoglycoside antibiotics of the butirosin family.
CC   -!- Has very low activity with substrates not bound to an acyl-carrier
CC       protein.
DR   Q2L4H3, BTRK_NIACI ;
//
ID   4.1.1.96
DE   carboxynorspermidine decarboxylase.
AN   carboxyspermidine decarboxylase.
CA   (1) carboxynorspermidine + H(+) = norspermidine + CO2.
CA   (2) carboxyspermidine + H(+) = spermidine + CO2.
CC   -!- Part of a bacterial polyamine biosynthesis pathway.
CC   -!- The enzyme is essential for biofilm formation in the bacterium Vibrio
CC       cholerae.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Campylobacter jejuni only produces spermidine in vivo
CC       even though it shows activity with carboxynorspermidine in vitro.
DR   A8FNH9, NSPC_CAMJ8 ;  Q5QCP2, NSPC_HERAR ;  Q56575, NSPC_VIBAL ;
DR   Q9KRL4, NSPC_VIBCH ;  C6YCI2, NSPC_VIBCO ;  Q8D8D2, NSPC_VIBVU ;
//
ID   4.1.1.97
DE   2-oxo-4-hydroxy-4-carboxy-5-ureidoimidazoline decarboxylase.
AN   OHCU decarboxylase.
CA   5-hydroxy-2-oxo-4-ureido-2,5-dihydro-1H-imidazole-5-carboxylate + H(+) =
CA   (S)-allantoin + CO2.
CC   -!- This enzyme is part of the pathway from urate to (S)-allantoin, which
CC       is present in bacteria, plants and animals (but not in humans).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.1.1.n1.
DR   Q45697, PUCL_BACSB ;  O32141, PUCL_BACSU ;  Q9LVM5, TTHL_ARATH ;
