//
ID   4.1.1.113
DE   trans-aconitate decarboxylase.
CA   trans-aconitate + H(+) = itaconate + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the smut fungus Ustilago maydis,
CC       is involved in an alternative pathway for the biosynthesis of
CC       itaconate. cf. EC 4.1.1.6.
DR   A0A0U2UYC4, TAD1_MYCMD ;
//
ID   4.1.1.114
DE   cis-3-alkyl-4-alkyloxetan-2-one decarboxylase.
CA   a cis-3-alkyl-4-alkyloxetan-2-one = a cis-alkene + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in certain bacterial species, catalyzes the last
CC       step in a pathway for the production of olefins.
DR   Q8EG65, OLEB_SHEON ;  Q8PDW8, OLEB_XANCP ;
//
ID   4.1.1.115
DE   indoleacetate decarboxylase.
AN   IAD.
CA   (indol-3-yl)acetate + H(+) = skatole + CO2.
CC   -!- This glycyl radical enzyme has been isolate from a number of
CC       bacterial species.
CC   -!- Skatole contributes to the characteristic smell of animal feces.
DR   A0A100YXA1, IAD_TRASO  ;
//
ID   4.1.1.116
DE   D-ornithine/D-lysine decarboxylase.
AN   DOKDC.
CA   (1) D-ornithine + H(+) = putrescine + CO2.
CA   (2) D-lysine + H(+) = cadaverine + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Salmonella typhimurium
CC       LT2, is specific for D-ornithine and D-lysine.
CC   -!- Requires pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
DR   Q8ZNC4, DOKDC_SALTY;
//
ID   4.1.1.117
DE   2-[(L-alanin-3-ylcarbamoyl)methyl]-2-hydroxybutanedioate decarboxylase.
CA   2-[(L-alanin-3-ylcarbamoyl)methyl]-2-hydroxybutanedioate + H(+) =
CA   2-[(2-aminoethylcarbamoyl)methyl]-2-hydroxybutanedioate + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Staphylococcus aureus,
CC       participates in the biosynthesis of the siderophore staphyloferrin B.
DR   Q2G1M6, SBNH_STAA8 ;
//
ID   4.1.1.118
DE   isophthalyl-CoA decarboxylase.
AN   IPCD.
CA   isophthalyl-CoA + H(+) = benzoyl-CoA + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Syntrophorhabdus
CC       aromaticivorans, participates in an anaerobic isophthalate
CC       degradation pathway.
CC   -!- The enzyme requires a prenylated flavin mononucleotide cofactor.
//
ID   4.1.1.119
DE   phenylacetate decarboxylase.
CA   2-phenylacetate + H(+) = toluene + CO2.
CC   -!- This bacterial enzyme, isolated from anoxic, toluene-producing
CC       microbial communities, is a glycyl radical enzyme.
CC   -!- It needs to be activated by a dedicated activating enzyme (PhdA).
CC   -!- The activase catalyzes the reductive cleavage of AdoMet, producing a
CC       5'-deoxyadenosyl radical that leads to the production of the glycyl
CC       radical in PhdB.
//
ID   4.1.1.120
DE   3-oxoisoapionate decarboxylase.
CA   3-oxoisoapionate + H(+) = L-erythrulose + CO2.
DR   Q6D8V4, OIAC_PECAS ;
//
ID   4.1.1.121
DE   3-oxoisoapionate-4-phosphate decarboxylase.
CA   3-oxoisoapionate 4-phosphate + H(+) = L-erythrulose 1-phosphate + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from several bacterial species,
CC       participates in the degradation of D-apionate.
CC   -!- It belongs to the RuBisCO-like-protein (RLP) superfamily.
CC   -!- Stereospecificity of 3-oxoisoapionate 4-phosphate has not been
CC       determined.
DR   Q2JZQ0, OIAX_RHIEC ;  B9JK73, OIAX_RHIR8 ;
//
ID   4.1.1.122
DE   L-cysteate decarboxylase.
CA   L-cysteate + H(+) = taurine + CO2.
CC   -!- Requires pyridoxal 5'-phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from chicken, is specific for L-cysteate
CC       and has poor activity with 3-sulfino-L-alanine. cf. EC 4.1.1.29.
//
ID   4.1.1.123
DE   phenyl-phosphate phosphatase/carboxylase.
AN   phenyl phosphate carboxylase.
CA   4-hydroxybenzoate + phosphate + H(+) = phenyl phosphate + CO2 + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Thauera aromatica,
CC       participates in an anaerobic phenol degradation pathway.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the para dephosphorylation and carboxylation of
CC       phenylphosphate to 4-hydroxybenzoate.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Thauera aromatica consists of four different subunits
CC       and requires K(+) and a divalent metal cation (Mg(2+) or Mn(2+)) for
CC       activity.
CC   -!- It is strongly inhibited by oxygen.
//
ID   4.1.1.124
DE   malonyl-[acp] decarboxylase.
AN   decarboxylative ketosynthase.
CA   malonyl-[ACP] + H(+) = acetyl-[ACP] + CO2.
CC   -!- This family of enzymes participates in a process that introduces a
CC       methyl branch into nascent polyketide products.
CC   -!- The process begins with EC 4.1.1.124 which converts the common
CC       extender unit malonyl-[ACP] to acetyl-[ACP]. The enzyme is a mutated
CC       form of a ketosynthase enzyme, in which a Cys residue in the active
CC       site is modified to a Ser residue, leaving the decarboxylase function
CC       intact, but nulifying the ability of the enzyme to form a carbon-
CC       carbon bond.
CC   -!- Next, EC 2.3.3.22 utilizes the acetyl group to introduce the branch
CC       at the beta position of 3-oxoacyl intermediates attached to a
CC       polyketide synthase, forming a 3-hydroxy-3-carboxymethyl
CC       intermediate.
CC   -!- This is followed by dehydration catalyzed by EC 4.2.1.181 (often
CC       referred to as an ECH1 domain), leaving a 3-carboxymethyl group and
CC       forming a double bond between the alpha and beta carbons.
CC   -!- The process concludes with decarboxylation catalyzed by EC 4.1.1.125
CC       (often referred to as an ECH2 domain), leaving a methyl branch at the
CC       beta carbon.
CC   -!- The enzymes are usually encoded by a cluster of genes referred to as
CC       an "HMGS cassette", based on the similarity of the key enzyme to
CC       EC 2.3.3.10. cf. EC 4.1.1.87.
//
ID   4.1.1.125
DE   4-carboxy-3-alkylbut-2-enoyl-[ACP] decarboxylase.
AN   3-carboxymethyl-alk-2-enyl-[acyl-carrier protein] decarboxylase.
CA   a 4-carboxy-3-alkylbut-(2E)-enoyl-[ACP] + H(+) = a 3-alkylbut-(2E)-enoyl-
CA   [ACP] + CO2.
CC   -!- This family of enzymes participates in a process that introduces a
CC       methyl branch into nascent polyketide products.
CC   -!- The process begins with EC 4.1.1.124, which converts the common
CC       extender unit malonyl-[ACP] to acetyl-[ACP]. The enzyme is a mutated
CC       form of a ketosynthase enzyme, in which a Cys residue in the active
CC       site is modified to a Ser residue, leaving the decarboxylase function
CC       intact, but nulifying the ability of the enzyme to form a carbon-
CC       carbon bond.
CC   -!- Next, EC 2.3.3.22 utilizes the acetyl group to introduce the branch
CC       at the beta position of 3-oxoacyl intermediates attached to a
CC       polyketide synthase, forming a 3-hydroxy-3-carboxymethyl
CC       intermediate.
CC   -!- This is followed by dehydration catalyzed by EC 4.2.1.181 (often
CC       referred to as an ECH1 domain) leaving a 3-carboxymethyl group and
CC       forming a double bond between the alpha and beta carbons.
CC   -!- The process concludes with decarboxylation catalyzed by EC 4.1.1.125
CC       (often referred to as an ECH2 domain) leaving a methyl branch at the
CC       beta carbon.
CC   -!- The enzymes are usually encoded by a cluster of genes referred to as
CC       an "HMGS cassette", based on the similarity of the key enzyme to
CC       EC 2.3.3.10.
//
ID   4.1.1.126
DE   anhydromevalonate phosphate decarboxylase.
CA   (2E)-3-methyl-5-phosphooxypent-2-enoate + H(+) = isopentenyl phosphate +
CA   CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes a step in the archaeal prenyl diphosphate
CC       biosynthesis pathway.
CC   -!- It requires a prenylated flavin cofactor that is produced by
CC       EC 2.5.1.129.
DR   Q9YA60, AMPD_AERPE ;  O28630, AMPD_ARCFU ;  Q58533, AMPD_METJA ;
DR   Q8PWQ0, AMPD_METMA ;  P22349, AMPD_METSM ;  P41655, AMPD_METTH ;
DR   O58701, AMPD_PYRHO ;
//
ID   4.1.1.127
DE   carboxyaminopropylagmatine decarboxylase.
CA   N(1)-[(S)-3-amino-3-carboxypropyl]agmatine + H(+) = N(1)-
CA   (3-aminopropyl)agmatine + CO2.
CC   -!- A pyridoxal 5'-phosphate protein.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the cyanobacterium Synechocystis sp.
CC       PCC 6803, participates in a biosynthetic pathway for spermidine.
DR   P73562, CAPDC_SYNY3;
//
ID   4.1.1.128
DE   UDP-N-acetyl-alpha-D-glucosaminuronate decarboxylase.
CA   UDP-2-acetamido-2-deoxy-alpha-D-glucuronate + H(+) = UDP-N-acetyl-alpha-
CA   D-xylosamine + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme has been described from pathogenic Bacillus species,
CC       including Bacillus anthracis, Bacillus thuringiensis, and Bacillus
CC       cereus.
CC   -!- The enzyme requires an NAD cofactor, and the reaction proceeds via a
CC       4-dehydro intermediate, resulting in reduction of NAD(+) to NADH (cf.
CC       EC 1.1.1.305).
CC   -!- However, NADH is not released from the enzyme and is recycled back to
CC       NAD(+) at the completion of the reaction cycle.
CC   -!- The product is incorporated into an extracellular polysaccharide
CC       known as pzX.
DR   A7GKX7, UXNAS_BACCN;
//
ID   4.1.1.129
DE   1'-carboxy-chondrochloren decarboxylase.
CA   (1) 1'-carboxy-chondrochloren A + FAD + 2 H(+) = chondrochloren A + FADH2
CA   + CO2.
CA   (2) 1'-carboxy-chondrochloren B + FAD + 2 H(+) = chondrochloren B + FADH2
CA   + CO2.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Chondromyces crocatus,
CC       catalyzes the final reactions in the biosynthesis of the antibiotics
CC       chondrochloren A and chondrochloren B.
DR   B9ZUK6, CNDG_CHOCO ;
//
ID   4.1.1.130
DE   4-hydroxy-3-methoxy-5-polyprenylbenzoate decarboxylase.
CA   a 4-hydroxy-3-methoxy-5-(all-trans-polyprenyl)benzoate + H(+) =
CA   a 2-methoxy-6-(all-trans-polyprenyl)phenol + CO2.
CC   -!- Requires Zn(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in eukaryotes, is involved in the biosynthesis of
CC       ubiquinone.
CC   -!- The number of isoprenoid subunits in the side chain varies in
CC       different species.
CC   -!- The enzyme does not have any specificity concerning the length of the
CC       polyprenyl tail, and accepts tails of various lengths with similar
CC       efficiency.
