//
ID   4.1.99.23
DE   5-hydroxybenzimidazole synthase.
AN   HBI synthase.
CA   5-amino-1-(5-phospho-beta-D-ribosyl)imidazole + AH2 + S-adenosyl-L-
CA   methionine = 5-hydroxybenzimidazole + 5'-deoxyadenosine + formate +
CA   L-methionine + A + NH4(+) + phosphate + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, purified from bacteria, is part of the anaerobic pathway
CC       for cobalamin biosynthesis.
CC   -!- It binds a [4Fe-4S] cluster that is coordinated by 3 cysteines and an
CC       exchangeable S-adenosyl-L-methionine molecule.
CC   -!- The first stage of catalysis is reduction of the S-adenosyl-L-
CC       methionine to produce L-methionine and a 5'-deoxyadenosin-5'-yl
CC       radical that is crucial for the conversion of the substrate.
DR   A0A0K1TPY7, BZAA_EUBLI ;  Q2RHR4, BZAA_MOOTA ;  A0A0K1TQ05, BZAB_EUBLI ;
DR   Q2RHR5, BZAB_MOOTA ;  Q1JZW3, BZAF_DESA6 ;  C0QJ00, BZAF_DESAH ;
DR   Q39YG0, BZAF_GEOMG ;  P61425, BZAF_GEOSL ;
//
ID   4.1.99.24
DE   L-tyrosine isonitrile synthase.
CA   D-ribulose 5-phosphate + L-tyrosine = (2S)-3-(4-hydroxyphenyl)-2-
CA   isocyanopropanoate + hydroxyacetone + formaldehyde + phosphate + H2O +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- The enzymes from the bacteria Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Xenorhabdus
CC       nematophila are involved in the biosynthesis of paerucumarin and
CC       rhabduscin, respectively.
CC   -!- According to the proposed mechanism, the enzyme forms an imine
CC       intermediate composed of linked L-tyrosine and D-ribulose
CC       5-phosphate, followed by loss of the phosphate group and formation of
CC       a beta-keto imine and keto-enol tautomerization.
CC   -!- This is followed by a C-C bond cleavage, the release of acetol, and a
CC       retro aldol type reaction that releases formaldehyde and forms the
CC       final product. cf. EC 4.1.99.25.
DR   Q9I1L5, PVCA_PSEAE ;  D3V9Q4, PVCA_XENNA ;
//
ID   4.1.99.25
DE   L-tryptophan isonitrile synthase.
CA   D-ribulose 5-phosphate + L-tryptophan = (2S)-3-(1H-indol-3-yl)-2-
CA   isocyanopropanoate + hydroxyacetone + formaldehyde + phosphate + H2O +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- The enzymes from cyanobacteria that belong to the Stigonematales
CC       order participate in the biosynthesis of hapalindole-type alkaloids.
CC   -!- According to the proposed mechanism, the enzyme forms an imine
CC       intermediate composed of linked L-tryptophan and D-ribulose
CC       5-phosphate, followed by loss of the phosphate group and formation of
CC       a beta-keto imine and keto-enol tautomerization.
CC   -!- This is followed by a C-C bond cleavage, the release of acetol, and a
CC       retro aldol type reaction that releases formaldehyde and forms the
CC       final product. cf. EC 4.1.99.24.
DR   V5TF65, AMBI1_FISAU;  V5TES5, AMBI2_FISAU;
//
ID   4.1.99.26
DE   3-amino-5-[(4-hydroxyphenyl)methyl]-4,4-dimethylpyrrolidin-2-one
DE   synthase.
CA   [mycofactocin precursor peptide]-C-terminal glycyl-N-
CA   {[2-(4-hydroxyphenyl)ethenyl]-3-methylbutanamide} + AH2 + S-adenosyl-L-
CA   methionine = [mycofactocin precursor peptide]-C-terminal glycyl-N-
CA   {5-[(4-hydroxyphenyl)methyl]-4,4-dimethyl-2-oxopyrrolidin-3-yl}acetamide
CA   + 5'-deoxyadenosine + L-methionine + A + H(+).
CC   -!- This is a bifunctional radical AdoMet (radical SAM) enzyme that
CC       catalyzes the first two steps in the biosynthesis of the enzyme
CC       cofactor mycofactocin.
CC   -!- Activity requires the presence of the MftB chaperone.
CC   -!- The reaction occurs in the right-to-left direction.
CC   -!- The other activity of the enzyme is EC 1.3.98.7.
DR   A0QSB8, MFTC_MYCS2 ;  P9WJ78, MFTC_MYCTO ;  P9WJ79, MFTC_MYCTU ;
DR   A0PM49, MFTC_MYCUA ;
//
ID   4.1.99.27
DE   cyclopenase.
CA   (-)-cyclopenine = viridicatin + methyl isocyanate + H(+).
CC   -!- This fungal enzyme catalyzes a key reaction in the biosynthesis of
CC       quinolone compounds, converting the benzodiazepine structure into a
CC       quinolone structure.
CC   -!- The enzyme is also active with (-)-4'-methoxycyclopenine.
DR   C8VJQ3, ASQI_EMENI ;
//
ID   4.1.99.28
DE   3'-dehydrocarminate deglycosidase.
CA   3'-dehydrocarminate + H(+) = kermesate + 1,5-anhydro-D-erythro-hex-1-
CA   en-3-ulose.
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- This enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Microbacterium 5-2b,
CC       participates in a carminate degradation pathway.
CC   -!- Following the activity of EC 1.1.3.50 it cleaves the C--C bond in
CC       3'-dehydrocarminate.
CC   -!- The sugar moiety is released lacking a hydroxyl at position 1,
CC       and containing a double bond at positions C-1 and C-2.
CC   -!- The enzyme shows weak activity with other 3'-dehydro-C-glucosides.
DR   P0DXE5, CGDA_MICSX ;  P0DXE6, CGDB_MICSX ;
//
ID   4.1.99.29
DE   5,8-dihydroxy-2-naphthoate synthase.
AN   1,4-dihydroxy-6-naphthoate synthase.
CA   cyclic dehypoxanthinylfutalosinate = 1,4-dihydroxy-6-naphthoate +
CA   dihydroxyacetone.
CC   -!- The enzyme participates in alternative menaquinone biosynthesis
CC       pathways known as the futalosine and modified futalosine pathways,
CC       which occurs in some bacterial species including several human
CC       pathogens such as Helicobacter pylori, Campylobacter jejuni,
CC       and Chlamydia.
DR   Q9KXN1, MQND_STRCO ;  Q5SI12, MQND_THET8 ;
//
ID   4.2.1.1
DE   carbonic anhydrase.
AN   carbonate dehydratase.
AN   carbonate hydro-lyase.
AN   carbonic dehydratase.
CA   hydrogencarbonate + H(+) = CO2 + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the reversible hydration of dissolved CO2 to
CC       carbonic acid, which dissociates to give hydrogencarbonate above
CC       neutral pH.
CC   -!- It is widespread and found in archaea, bacteria, and eukaryotes.
CC   -!- Three distinct classes exist, and appear to have evolved
CC       independently.
CC   -!- Contains zinc.
