ID   4.2.1.59
DE   3-hydroxyacyl-[acyl-carrier-protein] dehydratase.
AN   (3R)-3-hydroxyoctanoyl-[acyl-carrier-protein] hydro-lyase.
AN   3-hydroxyoctanoyl-[acyl-carrier-protein] dehydratase.
AN   beta-hydroxyoctanoyl-ACP-dehydrase.
AN   beta-hydroxyoctanoyl-acyl carrier protein dehydrase.
AN   beta-hydroxyoctanoyl thioester dehydratase.
AN   D-3-hydroxyoctanoyl-[acyl carrier protein] dehydratase.
CA   a (3R)-hydroxyacyl-[ACP] = a (2E)-enoyl-[ACP] + H2O.
CC   -!- This enzyme is responsible for the dehydration step of the
CC       dissociated (type II) fatty-acid biosynthesis system that occurs in
CC       plants and bacteria.
CC   -!- The enzyme uses fatty acyl thioesters of ACP in vivo.
CC   -!- Different forms of the enzyme may have preferences for substrates
CC       with different chain length.
CC   -!- For example, the activity of FabZ, the ubiquitous enzyme in bacteria,
CC       decreases with increasing chain length.
CC   -!- Gram-negative bacteria that produce unsaturated fatty acids, such as
CC       Escherichia coli, have another form (FabA) that prefers intermediate
CC       chain length, and also catalyzes EC 5.3.3.14.
CC   -!- Despite the differences both forms can catalyze all steps leading to
CC       the synthesis of palmitate (C16:0).
CC   -!- FabZ, but not FabA, can also accept unsaturated substrates.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 4.2.1.58, EC 4.2.1.60 and EC 4.2.1.61.
