ID   4.2.1.110
DE   aldos-2-ulose dehydratase.
AN   1,5-anhydro-D-fructose dehydratase (microthecin-forming).
AN   AUDH.
AN   pyranosone dehydratase.
CA   1,5-anhydro-D-fructose = microthecin + H2O.
CC   -!- This enzyme catalyzes two of the steps in the anhydrofructose
CC       pathway, which leads to the degradation of glycogen and starch via
CC       1,5-anhydro-D-fructose.
CC   -!- Aldose-2-uloses such as 2-dehydroglucose can also act as substrates,
CC       but more slowly.
CC   -!- This is a bifunctional enzyme that acts as both a lyase and as an
CC       isomerase.
CC   -!- Differs from EC 4.2.1.111, which can carry out only reaction 1,
CC       is inhibited by its product and requires metal ions for activity.
DR   P84193, AUD_PHACH  ;
//
ID   4.2.1.111
DE   1,5-anhydro-D-fructose dehydratase.
AN   1,5-anhydro-D-arabino-hex-2-ulose dehydratase.
AN   1,5-anhydro-D-fructose 4-dehydratase.
AN   1,5-anhydro-D-fructose hydrolyase.
AN   AF dehydratase.
AN   AFDH.
CA   1,5-anhydro-D-fructose = ascopyrone M + H2O.
CC   -!- Catalyzes one of the steps in the anhydrofructose pathway, which
CC       leads to the degradation of glycogen and starch via 1,5-anhydro-D-
CC       fructose.
CC   -!- The other enzymes involved in this pathway are EC 4.2.1.110,
CC       EC 4.2.2.13 and EC 5.3.2.7.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 4.2.1.110 the enzyme is specific for 1,5-anhydro-D-fructose
CC       as substrate and shows no activity toward aldose-2-uloses such as
CC       2-dehydroglucose; in addition, it is inhibited by its end-product
CC       ascopyrone M and it cannot convert the end-product ascopyrone M into
CC       microthecin, as can EC 4.2.1.110.
//
ID   4.2.1.112
DE   acetylene hydratase.
AN   AHy.
CA   acetaldehyde = acetylene + H2O.
CC   -!- The tungsten center binds a water molecule that is activated by an
CC       adjacent aspartate residue, enabling it to attack acetylene bound in
CC       a distinct hydrophobic pocket.
CC   -!- Ethylene cannot act as a substrate.
DR   Q71EW5, AHY_SYNAC  ;
//
