ID   4.2.1.121
DE   colneleate synthase.
AN   9-DES.
AN   9-divinyl ether synthase.
AN   CYP74D.
AN   CYP74D1.
CA   (9S)-hydroperoxy-(10E,12Z)-octadecadienoate = colneleate + H2O.
CC   -!- It catalyzes the selective removal of pro-R hydrogen at C-8 in the
CC       biosynthesis of colneleic acid.
CC   -!- It forms also (8E)-9-[(1E,3Z,6Z)-nona-1,3,6-trien-1-yloxy]non-8-enoic
CC       acid (i.e. colnelenate) from (9Z,10E,12Z,15Z)-9-hydroperoxy-10,12,15-
CC       octadecatrienoate.
CC   -!- The corresponding 13-hydroperoxides are poor substrates.
CC   -!- The divinyl ethers colneleate and colnelenate have antimicrobial
CC       activity.
DR   Q2WE96, CP74_ALLSA ;  Q0PHS9, DES_CAPAN  ;  Q9FPM6, DES_SOLLC  ;
DR   Q9AVQ1, DES_SOLTU  ;  Q8W2N5, DES_TOBAC  ;
//
ID   4.2.1.122
DE   tryptophan synthase (indole-salvaging).
AN   tryptophan synthase beta2.
CA   indole + L-serine = L-tryptophan + H2O.
CC   -!- Most mesophilic bacteria have a multimeric tryptophan synthase
CC       complex (EC 4.2.1.20) that forms L-tryptophan from L-serine and 1-C-
CC       (indol-3-yl)glycerol 3-phosphate via an indole intermediate.
CC   -!- This intermediate, which is formed by the alpha subunits,
CC       is transferred in an internal tunnel to the beta units, which convert
CC       it to tryptophan.
CC   -!- In thermophilic organisms the high temperature enhances diffusion and
CC       causes the loss of indole.
CC   -!- This enzyme, which does not combine with the alpha unit to form a
CC       complex, salvages the lost indole back to L-tryptophan.
CC   -!- It has a much lower Km for indole than the beta subunit of
CC       EC 4.2.1.20.
