ID   4.2.1.168
DE   GDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxy-alpha-D-mannose 3-dehydratase.
CA   GDP-4-dehydro-alpha-D-rhamnose + L-glutamate = GDP-4-dehydro-3,6-dideoxy-
CA   alpha-D-mannose + 2-oxoglutarate + NH4(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme, involved in beta-L-colitose biosynthesis, is a unique
CC       vitamin-B6-dependent enzyme.
CC   -!- In the first step of catalysis, the bound pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)
CC       cafactor is transaminated to the pyridoxamine 5'-phosphate (PMP) form
CC       of vitamin B6, using L-glutamate as the amino group donor.
CC   -!- The PMP cofactor then forms a Schiff base with the sugar substrate
CC       and the resulting adduct undergoes a 1,4-dehydration to eliminate the
CC       3-OH group.
CC   -!- Hydrolysis of the product from the enzyme restores the PLP cofactor
CC       and results in the release of an unstable enamine intermediate.
CC   -!- This intermediate tautomerizes to form an imine form, which
CC       hydrolyzes spontaneously, releasing ammonia and forming the final
CC       product.
DR   D3QY10, COLD_ECOCB ;  G4WJD4, COLD_YERPU ;
//
ID   4.2.1.169
DE   3-vinylbacteriochlorophyllide d 3(1)-hydratase.
CA   a bacteriochlorophyllide d = a 3-vinylbacteriochlorophyllide d + H2O.
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in green sulfur bacteria (Chlorobiaceae) and green
CC       flimentous bacteria (Chloroflexaceae), is involved in the
CC       biosynthesis of bacteriochlorophylls c, d and e.
CC   -!- It acts it the direction of hydration, and the hydroxyl group that is
CC       formed is essential for the ability of the resulting
CC       bacteriochlorophylls to self-aggregate in the chlorosomes, unique
CC       light-harvesting antenna structures found in these organisms.
