CC       by repeatedly removing the unsaturated residue from the non-reducing
CC       end until only unsaturated monosaccharides are left.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes a beta-elimination reaction, generating a new
CC       unsaturated non-reducing end after removal of the pre-existing one.
DR   Q21FJ0, EALGL_SACD2;  B2FSW8, EALGL_STRMK;
//
ID   4.2.2.27
DE   pectin monosaccharide-lyase.
AN   exo-pectin lyase.
CA   [(1->4)-alpha-D-galacturonosyl methyl ester](n) = [(1->4)-alpha-D-
CA   galacturonosyl methyl ester](n-1) + 4-deoxy-6-O-methyl-L-threo-hex-4-
CA   enopyranuronate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, isolated from the fungus Aspergillus giganteus, acts on
CC       the non-reducing end of methyl-esterified polygalacturonan, releasing
CC       either 4-deoxy(-)-L-threo-hex-4-enopyranuronate or 4-deoxy-6-
CC       O-methyl-L-threo-hex-4-enopyranuronate.
CC   -!- The enzyme is stimulated by divalent cations, with Co(2+) having the
CC       strongest effect.
CC   -!- It is able to act on substrates as short as a disaccharide, and was
CC       active on substrates with degrees of methyl esterification ranging
CC       between 34% and 90%.
//
ID   4.2.2.28
DE   alpha-L-rhamnosyl-(1->4)-beta-D-glucuronate lyase.
AN   L-rhamnose-alpha-1,4-D-glucuronate lyase.
CA   alpha-L-rhamnosyl-(1->4)-D-glucuronate = 4-deoxy-L-threo-hex-4-
CA   enopyranuronate + L-rhamnopyranose.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the phytopathogenic fungus Fusarium
CC       oxysporum, removes the rhamnosyl residue from alpha-L-rhamnosyl-
CC       (1->4)-beta-D-glucuronate or (with lower activity) from
CC       oligosaccharides that contain this motif at the non-reducing end,
CC       leaving an unsaturated glucuronate residue.
CC   -!- Among its natural substrates is the type II arabinogalactan component
CC       of gum arabic.
//
ID   4.2.2.29
DE   peptidoglycan lytic transglycosylase.
AN   endolytic murein transglycosylase.
AN   endolytic transglycosylase.
AN   lytic murein transglycosylase.
AN   lytic transglycosylase.
CA   a peptidoglycan chain = a peptidoglycan chain with N-acetyl-1,6-
CA   anhydromuramyl-[peptide] at the reducing end + a peptidoglycan chain with
CA   N-acetylglucosamine at the non-reducing end.
CC   -!- A group of bacterial enzymes that catalyze the non-hydrolytic
CC       cleavage of peptidoglycan (PG).
CC   -!- The enzymes fragment the polysaccharide chain at the beta-1,4-
CC       glycosidic bond between N-acetylmuramic acid and N-acetylglucosamine
CC       residues by an intramolecular cyclization of the N-acetylmuramyl
CC       moiety to yield a 1,6-anhydro-N-acetyl-beta-D-muramyl product.
CC   -!- Includes endolytic transglycosylase activity that fragments the
CC       glycan chain internally and exolytic transgylcosylase activity that
CC       cleaves a terminal disaccharide from the end of the glycan strand.
CC   -!- The MtlG enzyme of Gram-negative bacteria may function to regulate
CC       glycan strand length within the PG polymer.
DR   O34758, MLTG_BACSU ;  P28306, MLTG_ECOLI ;  P44720, MLTG_HAEIN ;
DR   A0A0H2ZLQ1, MLTG_STRP2 ;  Q8CYJ8, MLTG_STRR6 ;
//
