ID   4.4.1.37
DE   intrinsic cysteine-dependent pyridinium-3,5-bisthiocarboxylic acid
DE   mononucleotide synthase.
AN   P2CMN sulfurtransferase.
AN   P2TMN synthase.
AN   pyridinium-3,5-biscarboxylic acid mononucleotide sulfurtransferase.
AN   pyridinium-3,5-bisthiocarboxylic acid mononucleotide synthase.
CA   (1) pyridinium-3,5-dicarboxylate mononucleotide + [LarE protein]-L-
CA   cysteine + ATP = [LarE protein]-dehydroalanine + pyridinium-3-
CA   carboxylate-5-thiocarboxylate mononucleotide + AMP + diphosphate + H(+).
CA   (2) [LarE protein]-L-cysteine + pyridinium-3-carboxylate-5-
CA   thiocarboxylate mononucleotide + ATP = pyridinium-3,5-bisthiocarboxylate
CA   mononucleotide + [LarE protein]-dehydroalanine + AMP + diphosphate +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme, found in Lactobacillus plantarum, is involved in the
CC       biosynthesis of a nickel-pincer cofactor.
CC   -!- The process starts when one enzyme molecule adenylates pyridinium-
CC       3,5-dicarboxylate mononucleotide (P2CMN) and covalently binds the
CC       adenylated product to an intrinsic cysteine residue.
CC   -!- Next, the enzyme cleaves the carbon-sulfur bond, liberating
CC       pyridinium-3-carboxylate-5-thiocarboxylate mononucleotide (PCTMN) and
CC       leaving a 2-aminoprop-2-enoate (dehydroalanine) residue attached to
CC       the protein.
CC   -!- Since the cysteine residue is not regenerated in vivo, the enzyme is
CC       inactivated during the process.
CC   -!- A second enzyme molecule then repeats the process with PCTMN,
CC       adenylating it and covalently binding it to the same cysteine
CC       residue, followed by liberation of pyridinium-3,5-bisthiocarboxylate
CC       mononucleotide (P2TMN) and the inactivation of the second enzyme
CC       molecule.
CC   -!- cf. EC 4.4.1.45.
DR   F9UST4, LARE_LACPL ;
//
ID   4.4.1.38
DE   isethionate sulfite-lyase.
CA   2-hydroxyethane-1-sulfonate = acetaldehyde + sulfite + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the human gut bacterium Bilophila
CC       wadsworthia, participates in a taurine degradation pathway that leads
CC       to sulfide production.
CC   -!- The active form of the enzyme contains a glycyl radical that is
CC       generated by a dedicated activating enzyme via chemistry involving
CC       S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAM) and a [4Fe-4S] cluster.
DR   E5Y378, ISLA_BILW3 ;  B8J0R1, ISLA_DESDA ;  Q727N1, ISLA_NITV2 ;
DR   Q312S2, ISLA_OLEA2 ;
