ID   5.6.1.2
DE   dynein ATPase.
AN   dynein adenosine 5'-triphosphatase.
CA   ATP + H2O + a dynein associated with a microtubule at position (n) =
CA   ADP + phosphate + a dynein associated with a microtubule at position
CA   (n-1, toward the minus end).
CC   -!- A multisubunit protein complex associated with microtubules.
CC   -!- Hydrolysis of ATP provides energy for the movement of organelles
CC       (endosomes, lysosomes, mitochondria) along microtubules to the
CC       centrosome toward the microtubule's minus end.
CC   -!- It also functions in the movement of eukaryotic flagella and cilia.
CC   -!- It consists of two heavy chains (about 500 kDa), three-four
CC       intermediate chains (about 70 kDa) and four light chains (about 50
CC       kDa).
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.33 and EC 3.6.4.2.
//
ID   5.6.1.3
DE   plus-end-directed kinesin ATPase.
AN   kinesin.
CA   ATP + H2O + a kinesin associated with a microtubule at position (n) =
CA   ADP + phosphate a kinesin associated with a microtubule at position (n+1,
CA   toward the plus end).
CC   -!- Kinesins are a family of motor proteins that move unidirectionally
CC       along microtubules as they hydrolyze ATP.
CC   -!- The enzymes described here move toward the plus end of the
CC       microtubule, in contrast to EC 5.6.1.2 and EC 5.6.1.4.
CC   -!- They are involved in organelle movement in mitosis and meiosis,
CC       and also power vesicular trafficking toward the synapse in neurons.
CC   -!- The motor domain, which contains the ATP- and microtubule-binding
CC       activities, is located at the N-terminus while the C-terminus links
CC       to the cargo being transported.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.4.4.
DR   Q12756, KIF1A_HUMAN;  P33173, KIF1A_MOUSE;  F1M4A4, KIF1A_RAT  ;
DR   O60333, KIF1B_HUMAN;  Q60575, KIF1B_MOUSE;  O88658, KIF1B_RAT  ;
DR   Q12840, KIF5A_HUMAN;  P33175, KIF5A_MOUSE;  Q5R9K7, KIF5A_PONAB;
DR   Q6QLM7, KIF5A_RAT  ;
//
ID   5.6.1.4
DE   minus-end-directed kinesin ATPase.
AN   non-claret disjunctional.
CA   ATP + H2O + a kinesin associated with a microtubule at position (n) =
CA   ADP + phosphate + a kinesin associated with a microtubule at position
CA   (n-1, toward the minus end).
CC   -!- Kinesins are a family of motor proteins that move unidirectionally
CC       along microtubules as they hydrolyze ATP and are involved in
CC       organelle movement.
CC   -!- This enzyme is similar to EC 5.6.1.3 but the organization of the
CC       different domains differs, resulting in movement in the opposite
CC       direction along the microtubules.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.4.5.
DR   Q9US03, KAR3_SCHPO ;  P17119, KAR3_YEAST ;
//
ID   5.6.1.5
DE   proteasome ATPase.
CA   ATP + H2O + polypeptide = ADP + phosphate + unfolded polypeptide.
CC   -!- Belongs to the AAA-type superfamily and, like EC 5.6.1.4, is involved
CC       in channel gating and polypeptide unfolding before proteolysis in the
CC       proteasome.
CC   -!- Six ATPase subunits are present in the regulatory particle (RP) of
CC       26S proteasome.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.4.8.
//
ID   5.6.1.6
DE   channel-conductance-controlling ATPase.
AN   CFTR.
AN   cystic-fibrosis membrane-conductance-regulating protein.
CA   ATP + H2O + closed Cl(-) channel = ADP + phosphate + open Cl(-) channel.
CC   -!- ABC-type (ATP-binding cassette-type) ATPase, characterized by the
CC       presence of two similar ATP-binding domains.
CC   -!- The enzyme is found in animals, and in humans its absence brings
CC       about cystic fibrosis.
CC   -!- Unlike most of the ABC transporters, chloride pumping is not directly
CC       coupled to ATP hydrolysis.
CC   -!- Instead, the passive flow of anions through the channel is gated by
CC       cycles of ATP binding and hydrolysis by the ATP-binding domains.
CC   -!- The enzyme is also involved in the functioning of other transmembrane
CC       channels.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.3.49.
DR   Q07DV2, CFTR_AOTNA ;  Q00PJ2, CFTR_ATEAB ;  Q09YK5, CFTR_ATEGE ;
