//
ID   5.6.2.3
DE   DNA 5'-3' helicase.
AN   5' to 3' DNA helicase.
AN   replication fork helicase.
AN   replicative DNA helicase.
CA   Couples ATP hydrolysis with the unwinding of duplex DNA at the
CA   replication fork by translocating in the 5'-3' direction.
CC   -!- Helicases are motor proteins that can transiently catalyze the
CC       unwinding of energetically stable duplex DNA or RNA molecules by
CC       using ATP hydrolysis as the source of energy.
CC   -!- DNA helicases unwind duplex DNA and are involved in replication,
CC       repair, recombination, transcription, pre-rRNA processing,
CC       and translation initiation.
CC   -!- The best studied DNA 5'-3' helicases are those associated with
CC       replication of the chromosome.
CC   -!- The activity of the prokaryotic replicative helicase (DnaB) is often
CC       stimulated by DNA polymerase III.
CC   -!- As the lagging ssDNA is created, it becomes coated with single-
CC       stranded DNA binding protein (SSB).
CC   -!- Once every 500-2000 nucleotides, primase is stimulated by DnaB
CC       helicase to synthesize a primer at the replication fork.
CC   -!- This primer is elongated by the lagging strand half of DNA polymerase
CC       III holoenzyme.
