//
ID   6.2.1.77
DE   L-lysine--[L-lysyl-carrier protein] ligase.
CA   holo-[peptidyl-carrier protein] + L-lysine + ATP = L-lysyl-[peptidyl-
CA   carrier protein] + AMP + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The adenylation domain of the enzyme catalyzes the activation of
CC       L-lysine to (L-lysyl)adenylate, followed by the transfer of the
CC       activated compound to the free thiol of a phosphopantetheine arm of a
CC       peptidyl-carrier protein domain.
CC   -!- The peptidyl-carrier protein domain may be part of the same protein
CC       (as in the case of ScoA), or of a different protein. This activity is
CC       often found as part of a larger non-ribosomal peptide synthase.
//
ID   6.2.1.n1
DE   Transferred entry: 6.2.1.37.
//
ID   6.2.1.n2
DE   amino acid--[acyl-carrier-protein] ligase.
CA   an L-alpha-amino acid + holo-[ACP] + ATP = an L-alpha-aminoacyl-[ACP] +
CA   AMP + diphosphate.
CC   -!- Catalyzes the ATP-dependent activation of L-alanine, and to a lesser
CC       extent of L-glycine and L-serine, and their transfer to the
CC       phosphopantetheine cofactor covalently attached to a vicinal carrier
CC       protein.
CC   -!- May participate in nonribosomal peptide synthesis or related
CC       processes.
CC   -!- L-proline and L-glutamate are very poor substrates whereas D-amino
CC       acids are not substrates for ATP-dependent activation.
CC   -!- Does not display tRNA aminoacylation activity.
DR   Q89VT8, AACL1_BRADU;  Q89GR3, AACL2_BRADU;  Q7CWR3, AACL_AGRFC ;
//
ID   6.2.1.n3
DE   Transferred entry: 6.2.1.76.
//
ID   6.2.2.1
DE   thioglycine synthase.
CA   glycyl-[methyl-coenzyme M reductase] + hydrogen sulfide + ATP =
CA   thioglycyl-[methyl-coenzyme M reductase] + ADP + phosphate.
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme is found in anaerobic methanogenic and methanotrophic
CC       archaea, where it modifies a glycine residue in EC 2.8.4.1.
CC   -!- Upon binding to its substrate, an external source of sulfide attacks
CC       the target amide bond generating a tetrahedral intermediate.
CC   -!- The amide oxyanion attacks the gamma-phosphate of ATP, releasing ADP
CC       and forming a phosphorylated thiolate intermediate that collapses to
CC       form thioglycine and phosphate.
CC   -!- In most organisms activity requires a second protein (TfuA) , which
CC       may allosterically activate this enzyme or assist in the delivery of
CC       sulfide to the substrate.
//
ID   6.2.2.2
DE   oxazoline synthase.
AN   cyanobactin cyclodehydratase.
AN   cyanobactin heterocyclase.
CA   (1) L-amino acyl-L-seryl-[protein] + ATP = (S,S)-2-(C-substituted-
CA   aminomethyl)-4-acyl-2-oxazoline-[protein] + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CA   (2) L-amino acyl-L-threonyl-[protein] + ATP = (S,S)-2-(C-substituted-
CA   aminomethyl)-4-acyl-5-methyl-2-oxazoline-[protein] + ADP + phosphate +
CA   H(+).
CA   (3) L-amino acyl-L-cysteinyl-[protein] + ATP = (1S,4R)-2-(C-substituted-
CA   aminomethyl)-4-acyl-2-thiazoline-[protein] + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, which participates in the biosynthesis of ribosomal
CC       peptide natural products (RiPPs), converts L-cysteine, L-serine and
CC       L-threonine residues to thiazoline, oxazoline, and methyloxazoline
CC       rings, respectively.
CC   -!- The enzyme requires two domains - a cyclodehydratase domain, known as
CC       a YcaO domain, and a substrate recognition domain (RRE domain) that
CC       controls the regiospecificity of the enzyme.
CC   -!- The RRE domain can either be fused to the YcaO domain or occur as a
CC       separate protein; however both domains are required for activity.
CC   -!- The enzyme can process multiple residues within the same substrate
CC       peptide, and all enzymes characterized so far follow a defined order,
CC       starting with the L-cysteine closest to the C-terminus.
CC   -!- The reaction involves phosphorylation of the preceding ribosomal
CC       peptide backbone amide bond, forming ADP and a phosphorylated
CC       intermediate, followed by release of the phosphate group.
CC   -!- In some cases the enzyme catalyzes a side reaction in which the
CC       phosphorylated intermediate reacts with ADP to form AMP and
CC       diphosphate.
//
ID   6.2.2.3
DE   thiazoline synthase.
CA   L-amino acyl-L-cysteinyl-[protein] + ATP = (1S,4R)-2-(C-substituted-
CA   aminomethyl)-4-acyl-2-thiazoline-[protein] + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, which participates in the biosynthesis of some ribosomal
CC       peptide natural products (RiPPs) such as the trunkamides, converts
CC       L-cysteine residues to thiazoline rings.
CC   -!- The enzyme requires two domains - a cyclodehydratase domain, known as
CC       a YcaO domain, and a substrate recognition domain (RRE domain) that
CC       controls the regiospecificity of the enzyme.
CC   -!- The RRE domain can either be fused to the YcaO domain or occur as a
CC       separate protein; however both domains are required for activity.
CC   -!- The enzyme can process multiple L-cysteine residues within the same
CC       substrate peptide, and all enzymes characterized so far follow a
CC       defined order, starting with the L-cysteine closest to the
CC       C-terminus.
CC   -!- The reaction involves phosphorylation of the preceding ribosomal
CC       peptide backbone amide bond, forming ADP and a phosphorylated
CC       intermediate, followed by release of the phosphate group.
CC   -!- In some cases the enzyme catalyzes a side reaction in which the
CC       phosphorylated intermediate reacts with ADP to form AMP and
CC       diphosphate.
CC   -!- This activity is also catalyzed by the related enzyme EC 6.2.2.2.
CC   -!- That enzyme differs by having an RRE domain that also recognizes
CC       L-serine and L-threonine residues, which are converted to oxazoline
CC       and methyloxazoline rings, respectively.
//
