//
ID   6.3.1.7
DE   4-methyleneglutamate--ammonia ligase.
AN   4-methyleneglutamine synthetase.
CA   4-methylene-L-glutamate + NH4(+) + ATP = 4-methylene-L-glutamine + AMP +
CA   diphosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Glutamine can act instead of NH3, more slowly.
//
ID   6.3.1.8
DE   glutathionylspermidine synthase.
AN   gamma-L-glutamyl-L-cysteinyl-glycine:spermidine ligase (ADP-forming).
AN   glutathione:spermidine ligase (ADP-forming).
AN   glutathionylspermidine synthetase.
AN   GSP synthetase.
CA   spermidine + glutathione + ATP = glutathionylspermidine + ADP +
CA   phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Involved in the synthesis of trypanothione in trypanosomatids.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Escherichia coli is bifunctional and also catalyzes
CC       the EC 3.5.1.78 reaction, resulting in a net hydrolysis of ATP.
DR   P90518, GSP_CRIFA  ;  P0AES0, GSP_ECOLI  ;  P0AES1, GSP_SHIFL  ;
//
ID   6.3.1.9
DE   trypanothione synthase.
AN   TSR synthetase.
CA   (1) spermidine + glutathione + ATP = glutathionylspermidine + ADP +
CA   phosphate + H(+).
CA   (2) glutathionylspermidine + glutathione + ATP = trypanothione + ADP +
CA   phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from several trypanosomatids (e.g.
CC       Trypanosoma cruzi) catalyzes two subsequent reactions, leading to
CC       production of trypanothione from glutathione and spermidine.
CC   -!- Some trypanosomatids (e.g. Crithidia species and some Leishmania
CC       species) also contain an enzyme that only carries out the first
CC       reaction (cf. EC 6.3.1.8).
CC   -!- The enzyme is bifunctional, and also catalyzes the hydrolysis of
CC       glutathionylspermidine and trypanothione (cf. EC 3.5.1.78).
DR   O60993, TRYS_CRIFA ;  Q9GT49, TRYS_TRYCC ;
//
ID   6.3.1.10
DE   adenosylcobinamide-phosphate synthase.
CA   (1) (R)-1-aminopropan-2-yl phosphate + adenosylcob(III)yrate + ATP =
CA   adenosylcob(III)inamide phosphate + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CA   (2) (R)-1-aminopropan-2-ol + adenosylcob(III)yrate + ATP =
CA   adenosylcob(III)inamide + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme forms part of the anaerobic cobalamin biosynthesis
CC       pathway.
CC   -!- One of the substrates for this reaction, (R)-1-aminopropan-2-yl
CC       phosphate, is produced by CobD (EC 4.1.1.81).
//
ID   6.3.1.11
DE   glutamate--putrescine ligase.
AN   gamma-glutamylputrescine synthetase.
CA   putrescine + L-glutamate + ATP = gamma-L-glutamylputrescine + ADP +
CA   phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Forms part of a bacterial putrescine utilization pathway.
DR   I3R584, GLNA2_HALMT;  P78061, PUUA_ECOLI ;
//
ID   6.3.1.12
DE   D-aspartate ligase.
AN   Aslfm.
AN   D-aspartic acid-activating enzyme.
CA   [beta-GlcNAc-(1->4)-Mur2Ac(oyl-L-Ala-gamma-D-Glu-L-Lys-D-Ala-D-Ala)](n) +
CA   n D-aspartate + n ATP = [beta-GlcNAc-(1->4)-Mur2Ac(oyl-L-Ala-gamma-D-Glu-
CA   6-N-(beta-D-Asp)-L-Lys-D-Ala-D-Ala)]n + n ADP + n phosphate + n H(+).
CC   -!- Forms part of the peptidoglycan assembly pathway of Gram-positive
CC       bacteria grown in medium containing D-Asp.
CC   -!- Normally, the side chains the acylate the 6-amino group of the
CC       L-lysine residue contain L-Ala-L-Ala but these amino acids are
CC       replaced by D-Asp when D-Asp is included in the medium.
CC   -!- Hybrid chains containing L-Ala-D-Asp, L-Ala-L-Ala-D-Asp or D-Asp-L-
CC       Ala are not formed.
CC   -!- Highly specific for D-aspartate, as L-aspartate, D-glutamate,
CC       D-alanine, D-iso-asparagine and D-malic acid are not substrates.
CC   -!- In Enterococcus faecium, the substrate D-aspartate is produced by
CC       EC 5.1.1.13.
DR   H8L902, ASL_ENTFU  ;
//
ID   6.3.1.13
DE   L-cysteine:1D-myo-inositol 2-amino-2-deoxy-alpha-D-glucopyranoside
DE   ligase.
AN   MshC ligase.
AN   mycothiol ligase.
CA   1D-myo-inositol 2-amino-2-deoxy-alpha-D-glucopyranoside + L-cysteine +
CA   ATP = 1D-myo-inositol 2-(L-cysteinylamino)-2-deoxy-alpha-D-
CA   glucopyranoside + AMP + diphosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme is a key enzyme in the biosynthesis of mycothiol, a small
CC       molecular weight thiol found in Mycobacteria spp. and other
CC       actinomycetes.
CC   -!- Mycothiol plays a fundamental role in these organisms by helping to
CC       provide protection from the effects of reactive oxygen species and
CC       electrophiles, including many antibiotics.
CC   -!- The enzyme may represent a novel target for new classes of
CC       antituberculars.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 6.1.1.n1.
DR   Q4JXD7, MSHC1_CORJK;  B1VEQ2, MSHC1_CORU7;  Q4JVN7, MSHC2_CORJK;
