//
ID   6.3.5.5
DE   carbamoyl-phosphate synthase (glutamine-hydrolyzing).
AN   carbamoyl phosphate synthetase.
AN   carbamoyl phosphate synthetase (glutamine).
AN   carbamoyl-phosphate synthetase (glutamine-hydrolyzing).
AN   carbamoylphosphate synthetase II.
AN   CPS.
AN   GD-CPSase.
AN   glutamine-dependent carbamoyl-phosphate synthase.
AN   glutamine-dependent carbamoyl phosphate synthetase.
CA   hydrogencarbonate + L-glutamine + 2 ATP + H2O = carbamoyl phosphate +
CA   L-glutamate + 2 ADP + phosphate + 2 H(+).
CC   -!- The product carbamoyl phosphate is an intermediate in the
CC       biosynthesis of arginine and the pyrimidine nucleotides.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Escherichia coli has three separate active sites,
CC       which are connected by a molecular tunnel that is almost 100 A in
CC       length.
CC   -!- The amidotransferase domain within the small subunit of the enzyme
CC       hydrolyzes glutamine to ammonia via a thioester intermediate.
CC   -!- The ammonia migrates through the interior of the protein, where it
CC       reacts with carboxyphosphate to produce the carbamate intermediate.
CC   -!- The carboxyphosphate intermediate is formed by the phosphorylation of
CC       hydrogencarbonate by ATP at a site contained within the N-terminal
CC       half of the large subunit.
CC   -!- The carbamate intermediate is transported through the interior of the
CC       protein to a second site within the C-terminal half of the large
CC       subunit, where it is phosphorylated by another ATP to yield the final
CC       product, carbamoyl phosphate. cf. EC 6.3.4.16.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 2.7.2.9.
