ID   6.4.1.9
DE   coenzyme F430 synthetase.
CA   15,17(3)-seco-F430-17(3)-acid + ATP = coenzyme F430 + ADP + phosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme, studied from the methanogenic archaeon Methanosarcina
CC       acetivorans, catalyzes the last step in the biosynthesis of the
CC       nickel-containing tetrapyrrole cofactor coenzyme F430, which is
CC       required by EC 2.8.4.1.
DR   Q8TJZ6, CFBE_METAC ;  Q46FL3, CFBE_METBF ;  Q57706, CFBE_METJA ;
//
ID   6.4.1.10
DE   atromentin synthase.
CA   2 3-(4-hydroxyphenyl)pyruvate + 2 ATP = atromentin + 2 AMP +
CA   2 diphosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in some such as members of the Boletales and
CC       Eurotiales order, is a non-ribosomal peptide synthase-like enzyme
CC       comprising three domains - an adelylation (A) domain, an ayl-carrier
CC       protein (ACP) domain, and a thioesterase (TE) domain.
CC   -!- The enzyme activates 3-(4-hydroxyphenyl)pyruvate by adenylation and
CC       catalyzes the dimerization/cyclization of two such molecules to form
CC       atromentin.
CC   -!- Atromentin is a precursor for assorted metabolites produced by the
CC       fungi, including pulvinic acids and 2,5-diarylcyclopentenones.
//
ID   6.4.1.11
DE   polyporic acid synthase.
CA   2 3-phenylpyruvate + 2 ATP = polyporic acid + 2 AMP + 2 diphosphate +
CA   H(+).
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in mushrooms of the Basidiomycota and Ascomycota
CC       phyla, is a non-ribosomal peptide synthase-like enzyme comprising
CC       three domains - an adelylation (A) domain, an ayl-carrier protein
CC       (ACP) domain, and a thioesterase (TE) domain.
CC   -!- The enzyme activates 3-phenyl-2-oxopropanoate by adenylation and
CC       catalyzes the dimerization/cyclization of two such molecules to form
CC       polyporic acid.
CC   -!- Polyporic acid is a precursor for assorted metabolites produced by
CC       the fungi, such as the corticins.
//
ID   6.4.1.12
DE   didemethylasterriquinone D synthase.
CA   2 indole-3-pyruvate + 2 ATP = didemethylasterriquinone D + 2 AMP +
CA   2 diphosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- Requires Mg(2+).
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the fungus Aspergillus nidulans, is a
CC       non-ribosomal peptide synthase-like enzyme comprising three domains -
CC       an adelylation (A) domain, an ayl-carrier protein (ACP) domain, and a
CC       thioesterase (TE) domain.
CC   -!- The enzyme activates (indol-3-yl)pyruvate by adenylation and
CC       catalyzes the dimerization/cyclization of two such molecules to form
CC       didemethylasterriquinone D.
CC   -!- Didemethylasterriquinone D is subsequently converted to terrequinone
CC       A, a cytotoxic compound that displays a broad range of bioactivities.
//
ID   6.5.1.1
DE   DNA ligase (ATP).
AN   DNA joinase.
AN   DNA repair enzyme.
AN   polydeoxyribonucleotide synthase (ATP).
AN   polynucleotide ligase (ATP).
AN   sealase.
CA   ATP + (deoxyribonucleotide)n-3'-hydroxyl + 5'-phospho-
CA   (deoxyribonucleotide)m = (deoxyribonucleotide)n+m + AMP + diphosphate.
CC   -!- The enzyme catalyzes the ligation of DNA strands with 3'-hydroxyl and
CC       5'-phosphate termini, forming a phosphodiester and sealing certain
CC       types of single-strand breaks in duplex DNA.
CC   -!- Catalysis occurs by a three-step mechanism, starting with the
CC       activation of the enzyme by ATP, forming a phosphoramide bond between
CC       adenylate and a lysine residue.
CC   -!- The adenylate group is then transferred to the 5'-phosphate terminus
CC       of the substrate, forming the capped structure
CC       5'-(5'-diphosphoadenosine)-[DNA].
CC   -!- Finally, the enzyme catalyzes a nucleophilic attack of the 3'-OH
CC       terminus on the capped terminus, which results in formation of the
CC       phosphodiester bond and release of the adenylate.
CC   -!- RNA can also act as substrate, to some extent. cf. EC 6.5.1.2,
CC       EC 6.5.1.6 and EC 6.5.1.7.
