ID   6.6.1.2
DE   cobaltochelatase.
AN   hydrogenobyrinic acid a,c-diamide cobaltochelatase.
CA   hydrogenobyrinate a,c-diamide + Co(2+) + ATP + H2O = cob(II)yrinate a,c
CA   diamide + ADP + phosphate + 5 H(+).
CC   -!- This enzyme, which forms part of the aerobic (late cobalt insertion)
CC       cobalamin biosynthesis pathway, is a type I chelatase, being
CC       heterotrimeric and ATP-dependent.
CC   -!- It comprises two components, one of which corresponds to CobN and the
CC       other is composed of two polypeptides, specified by cobS and cobT in
CC       Pseudomonas denitrificans, and named CobST.
CC   -!- Hydrogenobyrinate is a very poor substrate.
CC   -!- ATP can be replaced by dATP or CTP but the reaction proceeds more
CC       slowly.
CC   -!- CobN exhibits a high affinity for hydrogenobyrinate a,c-diamide.
CC   -!- The oligomeric protein CobST possesses at least one sulfhydryl group
CC       that is essential for ATP-binding.
CC   -!- See EC 4.99.1.3 for the cobaltochelatase that participates in the
CC       anaerobic cobalamin biosynthesis pathway.
DR   Q9HZQ3, COBN_PSEAE ;  P29929, COBN_SINSX ;  P29933, COBS_SINSX ;
DR   P29934, COBT_SINSX ;
//
ID   6.7.1.1
DE   3-amino-2-hydroxy-4-methoxybenzoate diazotase.
CA   3-amino-2-hydroxy-4-methoxybenzoate + nitrite + ATP = cremeomycin + AMP +
CA   diphosphate + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from Streptomyces cremeus, catalyzes the
CC       last step in the biosynthesis of the ortho-diazoquinone cremeomycin.
DR   A0A0K2JLU1, CREM_STRCM ;
//
ID   6.7.1.2
DE   3-aminoavenalumate diazotase.
CA   3-aminoavenalumate + nitrite + ATP = 3-diazoavenalumate + AMP +
CA   diphosphate + H2O.
CC   -!- The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Streptomyces sp. RI-77,
CC       participates in the biosynthesis of avenalumate, a phenolic acid
CC       originally described from oat (Avena sativa).
CC   -!- It can also act on 3-aminocoumarate and 3-amino-4-hydroxybenzoate
CC       with lower activity.
DR   P0DXD8, AVAA6_STRSQ;
//
ID   7.1.1.1
DE   proton-translocating NAD(P)(+) transhydrogenase.
CA   NAD(+) + NADPH + H(+)(in) = NADH + NADP(+) + H(+)(out).
CC   -!- The enzyme is a membrane bound proton-translocating pyridine
CC       nucleotide transhydrogenase that couples the reversible reduction of
CC       NADP by NADH to an inward proton translocation across the membrane.
CC   -!- In the bacterium Escherichia coli the enzyme provides a major source
CC       of cytosolic NADPH.
CC   -!- Detoxification of reactive oxygen species in mitochondria by
CC       glutathione peroxidases depends on NADPH produced by this enzyme.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.6.1.2 and EC 1.6.1.5.
