ID   7.1.1.9
DE   cytochrome-c oxidase.
AN   complex IV (mitochondrial electron transport).
AN   cytochrome a3.
AN   cytochrome aa3.
AN   cytochrome ba3.
AN   cytochrome bb3.
AN   cytochrome caa3.
AN   cytochrome cbb3.
AN   cytochrome oxidase.
AN   Warburg's respiratory enzyme.
CA   4 Fe(II)-[cytochrome c] + O2 + 8 H(+)(in) = 4 Fe(III)-[cytochrome c] +
CA   2 H2O + 4 H(+)(out).
CC   -!- An oligomeric membrane heme-Cu:O2 reductase-type enzyme that
CC       terminates the respiratory chains of aerobic and facultative aerobic
CC       organisms.
CC   -!- The reduction of O2 to water is accompanied by the extrusion of four
CC       protons.
CC   -!- The cytochrome-aa3 enzymes of mitochondria and many bacterial species
CC       are the most abundant group, but other variations, such as the
CC       bacterial cytochrome-cbb3 enzymes, also exist.
CC   -!- All of the variants have a conserved catalytic core subunit (subunit
CC       I) that contains a low-spin heme (of a- or b-type), a binuclear metal
CC       center composed of a high-spin heme iron (of a-, o-, or b-type heme,
CC       referred to as a3, o3 or b3 heme), and a Cu atom (CuB).
CC   -!- Besides subunit I, the enzyme usually has at least two other core
CC       subunits: subunit II is the primary electron acceptor; subunit III
CC       usually does not contain any cofactors, but in the case of cbb3-type
CC       enzymes it is a diheme c-type cytochrome.
CC   -!- While most bacterial enzymes consist of only 3-4 subunits,
CC       the mitochondrial enzyme is much more complex and contains 14
CC       subunits.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 1.9.3.1.
