//
ID   7.1.1.10
DE   ferredoxin--quinone oxidoreductase (H(+)-translocating).
AN   NDH11 complex.
AN   NDH12 complex.
AN   NDH-1L' complex.
AN   NDH-1L complex.
CA   a plastoquinone + 2 reduced [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + 6 H(+)(in) =
CA   a plastoquinol + 2 oxidized [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + 4 H(+)(out).
CC   -!- The enzyme, present in plants and cyanobacteria, couples electron
CC       transport from ferredoxin to plastoquinone and proton pumping from
CC       the cytoplasm to the thylakoid lumen.
CC   -!- It participates in cyclic electron flow, retuning electrons generated
CC       by photosystem I to the plastoquinone pool, thus bypassing the
CC       generation of reducing power.
CC   -!- It may also participate in respiration using electrons originating
CC       from NADPH via the action of EC 1.18.1.2 operating in the direction
CC       of ferredoxin reduction.
CC   -!- It is a large complex, with some of its subunits resembling those
CC       from the bacterial/mitochondrial EC 7.1.1.2.
CC   -!- However, it lacks the NADH-oxidizing module and instead has a module
CC       that interacts with ferredoxin.
CC   -!- Several forms of the enzyme exist, differing in their exact
CC       combination of subunits used.
CC   -!- Some of the forms participate in carbon dioxide hydration rather than
CC       electron transfer.
//
ID   7.1.1.11
DE   ferredoxin--NAD(+) oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting).
AN   Rnf complex.
CA   2 reduced [2Fe-2S]-[ferredoxin] + NAD(+) + 3 H(+)(in) = 2 oxidized [2Fe-
CA   2S]-[ferredoxin] + NADH + 2 H(+)(out).
CC   -!- This iron-sulfur and flavin-containing electron transport complex,
CC       isolated from some anaerobic bacteria, couples the energy from
CC       reduction of NAD(+) by ferredoxin to pumping protons out of the cell,
CC       generating a proton motive force across the cytoplasmic membrane.
CC   -!- Most similar complexes pump sodium ions rather than protons (cf.
CC       EC 7.2.1.2).
//
ID   7.1.1.12
DE   succinate dehydrogenase (electrogenic, proton-motive force generating).
CA   a menaquinone + succinate + 2 H(+)(out) = a menaquinol + fumarate +
CA   2 H(+)(in).
CC   -!- The enzyme is very similar to EC 1.3.5.1 but differs by containing
CC       two heme molecules (located in the membrane anchor component) in
CC       addition to FAD and three iron-sulfur clusters.
CC   -!- Unlike EC 1.3.5.1, this enzyme catalyzes an electrogenic reaction,
CC       enabled by electron-bifurcation via the heme molecules.
CC   -!- In the direction of succinate oxidation by menaquinone, which is
CC       endergonic, the reaction is driven by the transmembrane
CC       electrochemical proton potential.
CC   -!- In the direction of fumarate reduction, the electrogenic electron
CC       transfer reaction is compensated by transmembrane proton transfer
CC       pathway known as the E-pathway, which results in overall
CC       electroneutrality.
//
ID   7.1.2.1
DE   P-type H(+)-exporting transporter.
AN   H(+)-transporting ATPase.
AN   proton-translocating P-type ATPase.
AN   proton transport ATPase.
CA   ATP + H2O + H(+)(in) = ADP + phosphate + 2 H(+)(out).
CC   -!- A P-type ATPase that undergoes covalent phosphorylation during the
CC       transport cycle.
CC   -!- This enzyme occurs in protozoa, fungi and plants, and generates an
CC       electrochemical potential gradient of protons across the plasma
CC       membrane.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.35 and EC 3.6.3.6.
