ID   7.1.2.2
DE   H(+)-transporting two-sector ATPase.
AN   ATP synthase.
AN   chloroplast ATPase.
AN   F0F1-ATPase.
AN   F1-ATPase.
AN   FoF1-ATPase.
AN   H(+)-transporting ATPase.
AN   H(+)-transporting ATP synthase.
AN   mitochondrial ATPase.
CA   ATP + H2O + 4 H(+)(in) = ADP + phosphate + 5 H(+)(out).
CC   -!- A multisubunit non-phosphorylated ATPase that is involved in the
CC       transport of ions.
CC   -!- Large enzymes of mitochondria, chloroplasts and bacteria with a
CC       membrane sector (Fo, Vo, Ao) and a cytoplasmic-compartment sector
CC       (F1, V1, A1).
CC   -!- The F-type enzymes of the inner mitochondrial and thylakoid membranes
CC       act as ATP synthases.
CC   -!- All of the enzymes included here operate in a rotational mode, where
CC       the extramembrane sector (containing 3 alpha- and 3 beta-subunits) is
CC       connected via the delta-subunit to the membrane sector by several
CC       smaller subunits.
CC   -!- Within this complex, the gamma- and epsilon-subunits, as well as the
CC       9-12 c subunits rotate by consecutive 120 degree angles and perform
CC       parts of ATP synthesis.
CC   -!- This movement is driven by the H(+) electrochemical potential
CC       gradient.
CC   -!- The V-type (in vacuoles and clathrin-coated vesicles) and A-type
CC       (archaeal) enzymes have a similar structure but, under physiological
CC       conditions, they pump H(+) rather than synthesize ATP.
CC   -!- Formerly EC 3.6.1.34 and EC 3.6.3.14.
