ID   7.4.2.12
DE   ABC-type cystine transporter.
AN   cystine ABC transporter.
AN   cystine transporting ATPase.
CA   (1) L-cystine(out) + ATP + H2O = L-cystine(in) + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CA   (2) D-cystine(out) + ATP + H2O = D-cystine(in) + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- ABC-type (ATP-binding cassette-type) transporter, characterized by
CC       the presence of two similar ATP-binding domains/proteins and two
CC       integral membrane domains/proteins.
CC   -!- A bacterial enzyme that interacts with an extracytoplasmic substrate
CC       binding protein and mediates the high affinity import of trace
CC       cystine.
CC   -!- The enzyme from Escherichia coli K-12 can import both isomers of
CC       cystine and a variety of related molecules including djenkolate,
CC       lanthionine, diaminopimelate and homocystine.
DR   P37774, TCYN_ECOLI ;
//
ID   7.4.2.13
DE   ABC-type tyrosine transporter.
CA   L-tyrosine(out) + ATP + H2O = L-tyrosine(in) + ADP + phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- An ATP-binding cassette (ABC) type transporter, characterized by the
CC       presence of two similar ATP-binding domains/proteins and two integral
CC       membrane domains/proteins.
CC   -!- The enzyme, found in Clostridioides, interacts with an
CC       extracytoplasmic substrate binding lipoprotein and mediates the
CC       import of L-tyrosine.
CC   -!- L-phenylalanine is also tranported however with lower efficiency.
DR   P0DX43, TYRAP_CLODR;
//
ID   7.4.2.14
DE   ABC-type antigen peptide transporter.
CA   a peptide antigen(in) + ATP + H2O = a peptide antigen(out) + ADP +
CA   phosphate + H(+).
CC   -!- An ATP-binding cassette (ABC) type transporter, characterized by the
CC       presence of two similar ATP-binding domains/proteins and two integral
CC       membrane domains/proteins.
CC   -!- Does not undergo phosphorylation during the transport process.
CC   -!- This entry describes vertebrate transporters involved in the
CC       transport of antigens from the cytoplasm to the endoplasmic reticulum
CC       for association with major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I
CC       molecules.
CC   -!- The substrates are generated mainly from degradation of cytosolic
CC       proteins by the proteasome.
